{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T03:43:20.359Z","ordinal":28549,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f70b-846a-7860-9aa2-ec3e0d5fdc10","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-ccb8f64f-3847-49b8-8dbf-6f2cd4dc3282","process_id":"48479","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,100p' docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md; sed -n '1,180p' docs/v1.2.2/README.md 2>/dev/null || true; rg -n '^\\| (VC-2|VC-6|VC-9|CX-4|CX-6|CX-7|RD-6|CS-6|MQ-7|CS-11|WM-3|PC-1|MQ-1|MQ-2|MQ-4|RD-1|RD-2)' docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"sed -n '1,100p' docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md; sed -n '1,180p' docs/v1.2.2/README.md 2>/dev/null || true; rg -n '^\\| (VC-2|VC-6|VC-9|CX-4|CX-6|CX-7|RD-6|CS-6|MQ-7|CS-11|WM-3|PC-1|MQ-1|MQ-2|MQ-4|RD-1|RD-2)' docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# v1.2.2 backlog (written 2026-11-18, after v1.2.1 shipped)\n\nWhat the v1.2.1 plan ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md)) did not finish, with the reason and the first step for each. Nothing here is scheduled by date; the owner chooses what ships. Units marked **needs** cannot be finished by writing code alone.\n\n## Needs a resource or an owner decision\n\n| Unit | Not done | Needs | First step |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | Containers, a pinned task subset, a model-cost budget (decisions D3, D4) | Pick the subset and budget; build the adapter against a mock gateway first. No external measurement is published for v1.2.1. |\n| MQ-4 | Scheduled gateway conformance: the job script, claim protocol and alert path are built and tested (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`) | A local scheduler on the host that can reach the gateway (a timer unit example is documented, nothing is installed) and a first live run with a prepared config | Install the timer (via Ansible if the host is managed that way), keep a `last-good-result.json` baseline, run once by hand |\n| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | A macOS runner and an arm64 or musl runner (decision D6, runner cost) | Decide runner cost; reuse `release.yml` packaging |\n| PC-1 | Median PR CI under 40 minutes (measured 37 to 45 minutes) | Time on the runners | Shard the CLI suite and cache toolchains; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is already in place |\n\n## Partly done: what is left\n\n| Unit | Done in v1.2.1 / since | Left |\n|---|---|---|\n| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations (`/requirements obligate|minimise|retire|reference`), replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording for file tools and the shell; exact-test result binding in the verifier output | Still open: a shell edit to a test file larger than 64 KiB, or outside the 20000-entry scan, is not recorded; weakening is recorded, never blocked. Runner output can still be fabricated by a workspace-controlled test command, so an output line is evidence of a report, not proof the test body executed. |\n| VC-6 | Baseline for untouched callers, shell-changed files, `go vet`, pyright and mypy, `UNAVAILABLE` for a missing tool | One check runs per project (tsc, then cargo, go, python); a resumed task has no baseline; cargo is excluded from the baseline as not cheap |\n| VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\n| CX-4 | Opt-in masking (`NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS`) with ids resolved through `read_archive` | A default threshold (not shown safe); a dedicated id-read tool; measurement of masking on real tasks |\n| CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\n| CX-7 | `@path` imports for AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, CODEX.md and .nexusrules (trusted workspaces, real-path containment) | Loading more than the first non-empty instruction file; `~/` and absolute imports |\n| RD-6 | `SessionManager.forkSession` and `/fork [n]` (source never mutated, compaction archive verified on resume); `--fork-session`/`--fork-at` on `run`, `repl` and the top level; a worktree-bound session is refused | Carrying a worktree binding into a fork (today it is refused: a fork would share the checkout; needs a decision on making a second checkout); `forkedFrom` is lost once the fork's own turns are saved; the REPL `/fork` worktree refusal has no automated test (the `--fork-session` flag is done: run, repl, top level) |\n| CS-6 | Test `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts`, weekly non-required workflow `lsp-real-servers.yml`, `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`; typescript-language-server and pyright validated locally (pass; mutation-checked); fixed the missing `--stdio` for TS/JS/Python | The workflow has not yet run on a hosted runner; rust-analyzer and gopls cases are written but have run nowhere (not installed on the dev host), so treat them as unvalidated; hover, definition, the trust and sandbox gates and multi-file projects are not exercised against real servers; versions of rust-analyzer and gopls are not pinned by the test |\n| MT-3 | One `redactPlain` and one `safeTerminal` use in place of 7 and 3 inline copies (part 1); part 2: a characterization test pins 23 variants over a shared corpus, `formatSafeSidecarText` now calls `safeTerminal`, and every remaining variant has a row with the reason it differs in `docs/v1.2.1/REDACTION_VARIANTS.md` | Nothing planned. The audit found only one more behaviour-preserving collapse; the rest differ in bytes (escape stripping, classifier or no classifier, control handling order, credential-key replacement). Module-private variants (`ceri` `redactedJson`, `safeTerminalText`, `flat`, `redactDeep`) are not in the characterization test |\n| MQ-7 | Slices B and C; D–G implemented in PR #627 (pending review and CI) | D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker), G (bounded campaigns) are available for review; see PR #627. |\n| CS-11 | Deterministic loop-pattern transcripts against the real agent turn and the measured table in `docs/LOOP_PATTERNS.md`; the pattern 2b gap (a command failing with only volatile text changing) closed by context-anchored volatile-text patterns, measured on an 86-pair labelled corpus (same-failure merged 6 of 38 to 34 of 38, different-failure merged 0 of 48 before and after) | Four same-failure pairs with no context word to anchor on (digit-less random suffix, unanchored counter, queue depth, short sha), left unmerged on purpose |\n| WM-3 | All five gateway aliases measured (single runs) | A reasoning-aware pass rule for aliases with reasoning off (the runner marks them `passed: false` for the hybrid-reasoning check); repeats, to see run-to-run variation |\n\n## Landed after this list was first written (2026-11-18)\n\n- **VC-7** (done): the verifier convergence stop is also judged on the `run_tests` / `run_command` verifier path (`cli/test/vc7_run_tests_convergence.test.ts`).\n- **CS-5** (done): an example-call request is never recovered into an executed call.\n- **MQ-3** (done, validated): the nightly real-project smoke failed on its first scheduled run (pytest installed with `pip install --user` was invisible under the smoke's throwaway `HOME`); fixed in #614; a manual hosted run (44314195365) passed with all four repositories REGRESSION_FREE.\n- **CS-6** (validated on a hosted runner, see the run recorded in `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`): typescript-language-server, pyright, gopls and rust-analyzer. The Rust case exposed two real problems, both fixed: a setup mismatch (the component must be on the default toolchain) and a client that returned on rust-analyzer's empty first publish (progress-aware waiting now). The first run also confirmed the `--stdio` fix. Hover and go-to-definition across two files now run against all four real servers (hosted run 44347025600). Still open: larger multi-file projects; the sandbox-refusal path is tested only with the fake server.\n- **MQ-4**, **VC-2**, **RD-6**: rows above describe what is and is not done.\n\n## Not started / deferred in their rows\n\nCS-9 (per-command approval rules), CS-10 (RPC worker isolation), VC-8 (per-role tool allowlists beyond reviewer and researcher), VC-10 (steering semantic judge, gated), RD-5 (superseded by v1.3 AC-1), RD-7 (`nexus uninstall`), MT-2 (split the two ~5.4k-line TUI files), PC-5 (settled by v1.3 GV-4).\n\n## Release-note items for the next release\n\n- Headless review-mode runs that need an approval nobody can give now end `BLOCKED` with `NEEDS_INPUT` and a machine-readable question (resume with `nexus run --resume <task> --answer <id>=approve|decline`); before, the model could route around the refusal. `--yes` and auto runs and subagents are unchanged.\n- `main` CI now runs `check_requirements --enforce` in the `test-matrix` job.\n- The compiler-exposure scan no longer kills its enumeration child after EOF (a race that failed scans under load).\n\n## Process lessons from v1.2.1\n\n- A release workflow runs tests that pull-request CI skips (host-conditional ones); run those under a flooded environment before tagging.\n- A background agent's reported test subset is not the suite: two failures in the VC-9 PR were found only by CI.\n- An agent's own mutation report is not evidence until re-run; one PR had a surviving mutation, another reported a mutation as caught when it was not.\n- CI colours its output and runs on Node 22 and 24 with different timing: agent PRs need local runs with `FORCE_COLOR=1 CI=true`, and tables kept in sync by a test (such as the global option table in `eval_cli.ts`) need the new flag added in the same change. Two real failures in the `--fork-session` PR and one in the VC-9 PR were found only by CI.\n- A timing-sensitive PTY test (`the whole live TUI through a pseudo-terminal`) failed once on Node 22 in the VC-7 PR and passed on rerun; it is a known-flaky class (see `flake-report`), recorded here rather than hidden.\n- A background agent can stall behind its own watcher loops for hours; give agents a foreground-only rule and check their worktree for changes when a status request goes unanswered.\n9:| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | Containers, a pinned task subset, a model-cost budget (decisions D3, D4) | Pick the subset and budget; build the adapter against a mock gateway first. No external measurement is published for v1.2.1. |\n10:| MQ-4 | Scheduled gateway conformance: the job script, claim protocol and alert path are built and tested (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`) | A local scheduler on the host that can reach the gateway (a timer unit example is documented, nothing is installed) and a first live run with a prepared config | Install the timer (via Ansible if the host is managed that way), keep a `last-good-result.json` baseline, run once by hand |\n11:| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | A macOS runner and an arm64 or musl runner (decision D6, runner cost) | Decide runner cost; reuse `release.yml` packaging |\n12:| PC-1 | Median PR CI under 40 minutes (measured 37 to 45 minutes) | Time on the runners | Shard the CLI suite and cache toolchains; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is already in place |\n18:| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations (`/requirements obligate|minimise|retire|reference`), replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording for file tools and the shell; exact-test result binding in the verifier output | Still open: a shell edit to a test file larger than 64 KiB, or outside the 20000-entry scan, is not recorded; weakening is recorded, never blocked. Runner output can still be fabricated by a workspace-controlled test command, so an output line is evidence of a report, not proof the test body executed. |\n19:| VC-6 | Baseline for untouched callers, shell-changed files, `go vet`, pyright and mypy, `UNAVAILABLE` for a missing tool | One check runs per project (tsc, then cargo, go, python); a resumed task has no baseline; cargo is excluded from the baseline as not cheap |\n20:| VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\n21:| CX-4 | Opt-in masking (`NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS`) with ids resolved through `read_archive` | A default threshold (not shown safe); a dedicated id-read tool; measurement of masking on real tasks |\n22:| CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\n23:| CX-7 | `@path` imports for AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, CODEX.md and .nexusrules (trusted workspaces, real-path containment) | Loading more than the first non-empty instruction file; `~/` and absolute imports |\n24:| RD-6 | `SessionManager.forkSession` and `/fork [n]` (source never mutated, compaction archive verified on resume); `--fork-session`/`--fork-at` on `run`, `repl` and the top level; a worktree-bound session is refused | Carrying a worktree binding into a fork (today it is refused: a fork would share the checkout; needs a decision on making a second checkout); `forkedFrom` is lost once the fork's own turns are saved; the REPL `/fork` worktree refusal has no automated test (the `--fork-session` flag is done: run, repl, top level) |\n25:| CS-6 | Test `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts`, weekly non-required workflow `lsp-real-servers.yml`, `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`; typescript-language-server and pyright validated locally (pass; mutation-checked); fixed the missing `--stdio` for TS/JS/Python | The workflow has not yet run on a hosted runner; rust-analyzer and gopls cases are written but have run nowhere (not installed on the dev host), so treat them as unvalidated; hover, definition, the trust and sandbox gates and multi-file projects are not exercised against real servers; versions of rust-analyzer and gopls are not pinned by the test |\n27:| MQ-7 | Slices B and C; D–G implemented in PR #627 (pending review and CI) | D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker), G (bounded campaigns) are available for review; see PR #627. |\n28:| CS-11 | Deterministic loop-pattern transcripts against the real agent turn and the measured table in `docs/LOOP_PATTERNS.md`; the pattern 2b gap (a command failing with only volatile text changing) closed by context-anchored volatile-text patterns, measured on an 86-pair labelled corpus (same-failure merged 6 of 38 to 34 of 38, different-failure merged 0 of 48 before and after) | Four same-failure pairs with no context word to anchor on (digit-less random suffix, unanchored counter, queue depth, short sha), left unmerged on purpose |\n29:| WM-3 | All five gateway aliases measured (single runs) | A reasoning-aware pass rule for aliases with reasoning off (the runner marks them `passed: false` for the hybrid-reasoning check); repeats, to see run-to-run variation |\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":56625}},"started_at_ms":1795146200358,"completed_at_ms":1795146200359}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T03:43:20.510Z","ordinal":28550,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1f70d-35bd-74a1-9a4a-53100aaa6190","call_id":"call_dfc2613ee28a4c35a12fa2dd9fd58649","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# v1.2.2 backlog (written 2026-11-18, after v1.2.1 shipped)\n\nWhat the v1.2.1 plan ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md)) did not finish, with the reason and the first step for each. Nothing here is scheduled by date; the owner chooses what ships. Units marked **needs** cannot be finished by writing code alone.\n\n## Needs a resource or an owner decision\n\n| Unit | Not done | Needs | First step |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | Containers, a pinned task subset, a model-cost budget (decisions D3, D4) | Pick the subset and budget; build the adapter against a mock gateway first. No external measurement is published for v1.2.1. |\n| MQ-4 | Scheduled gateway conformance: the job script, claim protocol and alert path are built and tested (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`) | A local scheduler on the host that can reach the gateway (a timer unit example is documented, nothing is installed) and a first live run with a prepared config | Install the timer (via Ansible if the host is managed that way), keep a `last-good-result.json` baseline, run once by hand |\n| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | A macOS runner and an arm64 or musl runner (decision D6, runner cost) | Decide runner cost; reuse `release.yml` packaging |\n| PC-1 | Median PR CI under 40 minutes (measured 37 to 45 minutes) | Time on the runners | Shard the CLI suite and cache toolchains; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is already in place |\n\n## Partly done: what is left\n\n| Unit | Done in v1.2.1 / since | Left |\n|---|---|---|\n| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations (`/requirements obligate|minimise|retire|reference`), replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording for file tools and the shell; exact-test result binding in the verifier output | Still open: a shell edit to a test file larger than 64 KiB, or outside the 20000-entry scan, is not recorded; weakening is recorded, never blocked. Runner output can still be fabricated by a workspace-controlled test command, so an output line is evidence of a report, not proof the test body executed. |\n| VC-6 | Baseline for untouched callers, shell-changed files, `go vet`, pyright and mypy, `UNAVAILABLE` for a missing tool | One check runs per project (tsc, then cargo, go, python); a resumed task has no baseline; cargo is excluded from the baseline as not cheap |\n| VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\n| CX-4 | Opt-in masking (`NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS`) with ids resolved through `read_archive` | A default threshold (not shown safe); a dedicated id-read tool; measurement of masking on real tasks |\n| CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\n| CX-7 | `@path` imports for AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, CODEX.md and .nexusrules (trusted workspaces, real-path containment) | Loading more than the first non-empty instruction file; `~/` and absolute imports |\n| RD-6 | `SessionManager.forkSession` and `/fork [n]` (source never mutated, compaction archive verified on resume); `--fork-session`/`--fork-at` on `run`, `repl` and the top level; a worktree-bound session is refused | Carrying a worktree binding into a fork (today it is refused: a fork would share the checkout; needs a decision on making a second checkout); `forkedFrom` is lost once the fork's own turns are saved; the REPL `/fork` worktree refusal has no automated test (the `--fork-session` flag is done: run, repl, top level) |\n| CS-6 | Test `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts`, weekly non-required workflow `lsp-real-servers.yml`, `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`; typescript-language-server and pyright validated locally (pass; mutation-checked); fixed the missing `--stdio` for TS/JS/Python | The workflow has not yet run on a hosted runner; rust-analyzer and gopls cases are written but have run nowhere (not installed on the dev host), so treat them as unvalidated; hover, definition, the trust and sandbox gates and multi-file projects are not exercised against real servers; versions of rust-analyzer and gopls are not pinned by the test |\n| MT-3 | One `redactPlain` and one `safeTerminal` use in place of 7 and 3 inline copies (part 1); part 2: a characterization test pins 23 variants over a shared corpus, `formatSafeSidecarText` now calls `safeTerminal`, and every remaining variant has a row with the reason it differs in `docs/v1.2.1/REDACTION_VARIANTS.md` | Nothing planned. The audit found only one more behaviour-preserving collapse; the rest differ in bytes (escape stripping, classifier or no classifier, control handling order, credential-key replacement). Module-private variants (`ceri` `redactedJson`, `safeTerminalText`, `flat`, `redactDeep`) are not in the characterization test |\n| MQ-7 | Slices B and C; D–G implemented in PR #627 (pending review and CI) | D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker), G (bounded campaigns) are available for review; see PR #627. |\n| CS-11 | Deterministic loop-pattern transcripts against the real agent turn and the measured table in `docs/LOOP_PATTERNS.md`; the pattern 2b gap (a command failing with only volatile text changing) closed by context-anchored volatile-text patterns, measured on an 86-pair labelled corpus (same-failure merged 6 of 38 to 34 of 38, different-failure merged 0 of 48 before and after) | Four same-failure pairs with no context word to anchor on (digit-less random suffix, unanchored counter, queue depth, short sha), left unmerged on purpose |\n| WM-3 | All five gateway aliases measured (single runs) | A reasoning-aware pass rule for aliases with reasoning off (the runner marks them `passed: false` for the hybrid-reasoning check); repeats, to see run-to-run variation |\n\n## Landed after this list was first written (2026-11-18)\n\n- **VC-7** (done): the verifier convergence stop is also judged on the `run_tests` / `run_command` verifier path (`cli/test/vc7_run_tests_convergence.test.ts`).\n- **CS-5** (done): an example-call request is never recovered into an executed call.\n- **MQ-3** (done, validated): the nightly real-project smoke failed on its first scheduled run (pytest installed with `pip install --user` was invisible under the smoke's throwaway `HOME`); fixed in #614; a manual hosted run (44314195365) passed with all four repositories REGRESSION_FREE.\n- **CS-6** (validated on a hosted runner, see the run recorded in `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`): typescript-language-server, pyright, gopls and rust-analyzer. The Rust case exposed two real problems, both fixed: a setup mismatch (the component must be on the default toolchain) and a client that returned on rust-analyzer's empty first publish (progress-aware waiting now). The first run also confirmed the `--stdio` fix. Hover and go-to-definition across two files now run against all four real servers (hosted run 44347025600). Still open: larger multi-file projects; the sandbox-refusal path is tested only with the fake server.\n- **MQ-4**, **VC-2**, **RD-6**: rows above describe what is and is not done.\n\n## Not started / deferred in their rows\n\nCS-9 (per-command approval rules), CS-10 (RPC worker isolation), VC-8 (per-role tool allowlists beyond reviewer and researcher), VC-10 (steering semantic judge, gated), RD-5 (superseded by v1.3 AC-1), RD-7 (`nexus uninstall`), MT-2 (split the two ~5.4k-line TUI files), PC-5 (settled by v1.3 GV-4).\n\n## Release-note items for the next release\n\n- Headless review-mode runs that need an approval nobody can give now end `BLOCKED` with `NEEDS_INPUT` and a machine-readable question (resume with `nexus run --resume <task> --answer <id>=approve|decline`); before, the model could route around the refusal. `--yes` and auto runs and subagents are unchanged.\n- `main` CI now runs `check_requirements --enforce` in the `test-matrix` job.\n- The compiler-exposure scan no longer kills its enumeration child after EOF (a race that failed scans under load).\n\n## Process lessons from v1.2.1\n\n- A release workflow runs tests that pull-request CI skips (host-conditional ones); run those under a flooded environment before tagging.\n- A background agent's reported test subset is not the suite: two failures in the VC-9 PR were found only by CI.\n- An agent's own mutation report is not evidence until re-run; one PR had a surviving mutation, another reported a mutation as caught when it was not.\n- CI colours its output and runs on Node 22 and 24 with different timing: agent PRs need local runs with `FORCE_COLOR=1 CI=true`, and tables kept in sync by a test (such as the global option table in `eval_cli.ts`) need the new flag added in the same change. Two real failures in the `--fork-session` PR and one in the VC-9 PR were found only by CI.\n- A timing-sensitive PTY test (`the whole live TUI through a pseudo-terminal`) failed once on Node 22 in the VC-7 PR and passed on rerun; it is a known-flaky class (see `flake-report`), recorded here rather than hidden.\n- A background agent can stall behind its own watcher loops for hours; give agents a foreground-only rule and check their worktree for changes when a status request goes unanswered.\n9:| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | Containers, a pinned task subset, a model-cost budget (decisions D3, D4) | Pick the subset and budget; build the adapter against a mock gateway first. No external measurement is published for v1.2.1. |\n10:| MQ-4 | Scheduled gateway conformance: the job script, claim protocol and alert path are built and tested (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`) | A local scheduler on the host that can reach the gateway (a timer unit example is documented, nothing is installed) and a first live run with a prepared config | Install the timer (via Ansible if the host is managed that way), keep a `last-good-result.json` baseline, run once by hand |\n11:| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | A macOS runner and an arm64 or musl runner (decision D6, runner cost) | Decide runner cost; reuse `release.yml` packaging |\n12:| PC-1 | Median PR CI under 40 minutes (measured 37 to 45 minutes) | Time on the runners | Shard the CLI suite and cache toolchains; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is already in place |\n18:| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations (`/requirements obligate|minimise|retire|reference`), replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording for file tools and the shell; exact-test result binding in the verifier output | Still open: a shell edit to a test file larger than 64 KiB, or outside the 20000-entry scan, is not recorded; weakening is recorded, never blocked. Runner output can still be fabricated by a workspace-controlled test command, so an output line is evidence of a report, not proof the test body executed. |\n19:| VC-6 | Baseline for untouched callers, shell-changed files, `go vet`, pyright and mypy, `UNAVAILABLE` for a missing tool | One check runs per project (tsc, then cargo, go, python); a resumed task has no baseline; cargo is excluded from the baseline as not cheap |\n20:| VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\n21:| CX-4 | Opt-in masking (`NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS`) with ids resolved through `read_archive` | A default threshold (not shown safe); a dedicated id-read tool; measurement of masking on real tasks |\n22:| CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\n23:| CX-7 | `@path` imports for AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, CODEX.md and .nexusrules (trusted workspaces, real-path containment) | Loading more than the first non-empty instruction file; `~/` and absolute imports |\n24:| RD-6 | `SessionManager.forkSession` and `/fork [n]` (source never mutated, compaction archive verified on resume); `--fork-session`/`--fork-at` on `run`, `repl` and the top level; a worktree-bound session is refused | Carrying a worktree binding into a fork (today it is refused: a fork would share the checkout; needs a decision on making a second checkout); `forkedFrom` is lost once the fork's own turns are saved; the REPL `/fork` worktree refusal has no automated test (the `--fork-session` flag is done: run, repl, top level) |\n25:| CS-6 | Test `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts`, weekly non-required workflow `lsp-real-servers.yml`, `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`; typescript-language-server and pyright validated locally (pass; mutation-checked); fixed the missing `--stdio` for TS/JS/Python | The workflow has not yet run on a hosted runner; rust-analyzer and gopls cases are written but have run nowhere (not installed on the dev host), so treat them as unvalidated; hover, definition, the trust and sandbox gates and multi-file projects are not exercised against real servers; versions of rust-analyzer and gopls are not pinned by the test |\n27:| MQ-7 | Slices B and C; D–G implemented in PR #627 (pending review and CI) | D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker), G (bounded campaigns) are available for review; see PR #627. |\n28:| CS-11 | Deterministic loop-pattern transcripts against the real agent turn and the measured table in `docs/LOOP_PATTERNS.md`; the pattern 2b gap (a command failing with only volatile text changing) closed by context-anchored volatile-text patterns, measured on an 86-pair labelled corpus (same-failure merged 6 of 38 to 34 of 38, different-failure merged 0 of 48 before and after) | Four same-failure pairs with no context word to anchor on (digit-less random suffix, unanchored counter, queue depth, short sha), left unmerged on purpose |\n29:| WM-3 | All five gateway aliases measured (single runs) | A reasoning-aware pass rule for aliases with reasoning off (the runner marks them `passed: false` for the hybrid-reasoning check); repeats, to see run-to-run variation |\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1f70b-846a-7860-9aa2-ec3e0d5fdc10","create_time":1795146200.509742}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":12591,"sampled_through_sequence":12587,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
