{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T03:45:47.420Z","ordinal":28626,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f70b-846a-7860-9aa2-ec3e0d5fdc10","session_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","root_turn_id":"01a1f70b-846a-7860-9aa2-ec3e0d5fdc10","response_id":"resp_05793ea695c2aa2c016affc24d622487d28f00837f951330c3","usage":{"input_tokens":224951,"cached_input_tokens":222336,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1619,"reasoning_output_tokens":1412,"total_tokens":226570},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":3187109,"cached_input_tokens":2959616,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":11446,"reasoning_output_tokens":7961,"total_tokens":3198555},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":602623557,"cached_input_tokens":595939712,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1261371,"reasoning_output_tokens":657766,"total_tokens":603884928}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T03:45:47.603Z","ordinal":28627,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f70b-846a-7860-9aa2-ec3e0d5fdc10","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-ed6143a0-1287-45e9-8fda-c0a39b2a1d96","process_id":"12374","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","cat docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md; sed -n '1,280p' cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts; sed -n '1,260p' docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md; sed -n '1,220p' .github/workflows/lsp-real-servers.yml; git status --short --branch; git rev-parse HEAD; git rev-parse origin/main"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"cat docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md; sed -n '1,280p' cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts; sed -n '1,260p' docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md; sed -n '1,220p' .github/workflows/lsp-real-servers.yml; git status --short --branch; git rev-parse HEAD; git rev-parse origin/main"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# Reference coding with XERJ\n\nThe detail behind the \"Reference coding\" rule in `AGENTS.md`: how to reach the local XERJ node, what to query, and what to leave alone.\n\n- Before non-trivial code with a peer-project analogue (feature, protocol, parser, worktree or provider handling, tool, test harness), query the local XERJ node and record what you took, borrowed or rejected in the PR's \"Reference coding\" section. Exempt: trivial edits, doc-only changes, fixes fully specified by a failing test.\n- XERJ is an owner-host tool, not a project dependency (`docs/v1.1.0/DECISIONS.md` §1). Where it is unavailable (contributor machine, CI), write \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" in that section and use `git grep` plus the peer's public source.\n- The node is the user service `xerj` on `http://localhost:9200`; confirm with `systemctl --user is-active xerj` and use that node. Stop a hub with `xerj hub stop`.\n- Corpora: `xerj corpus list`. Retrieval is lexical (BM25), so query with identifiers and words from the code: `xerj code <corpus> \"<words>\" -k 4 --full 600`, `xerj search --prefix <index-prefix> \"<words>\" -k 4`, `xerj def --prefix <prefix> \"<symbol>\"`. The first query can take over a minute; set a timeout.\n- `nexus-cli`, `nexus-core`, `nexus-test`, `nexus-docs` index `origin/main` only (hourly), so open PRs are absent: use `git grep` on your branch.\n- Reference code is read-only inspiration. Follow each result's licence tag; copy verbatim only permissively licensed code, with a citation. Keep XERJ and reference corpora away from held-out evaluation tasks (`docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md`). Keep document text local (`rerank` sends it to a third party). Leave indices you did not create alone.\n- After real use: `xerj feedback --dry-run`, then `xerj feedback --submit --agent \"<model>\" --used-for \"<task>\" --verdict \"<what worked and what did not>\"`, with secrets and private data left out.\n// Real language servers (CS-6). The v1.2.0 LSP support (lsp.ts) is otherwise tested only against test/fixtures/fake_lsp_server.mjs.\n// Here the product's own LspBridgeManager starts the real server, found the way the product finds it (the default\n// extension -> command table, resolved on the scrubbed PATH buildLspEnvironment returns), on a tiny fixture project:\n//   - a file with one deliberate error must yield status \"ok\" with an error diagnostic naming it;\n//   - a clean file must yield status \"ok\" with an EMPTY list (a real report), which is not \"unavailable\";\n//   - a manager whose server cannot start must yield \"unavailable\" (the contrast the two above are read against).\n// A server that is not installed SKIPS with its reason, unless NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE names it (\"1\" or \"all\" = every server,\n// or a comma list such as \"typescript,python\"), in which case its absence is a failure. The scheduled workflow\n// .github/workflows/lsp-real-servers.yml sets it. The availability probe runs with the SAME environment the product\n// gives the server (buildLspEnvironment), so a tool visible only to the shell (a `pip install --user` under another HOME)\n// is reported missing instead of passing the probe and failing later.\nimport { test } from \"node:test\";\nimport assert from \"node:assert/strict\";\nimport { spawnSync } from \"node:child_process\";\nimport * as fs from \"node:fs\";\nimport * as os from \"node:os\";\nimport * as path from \"node:path\";\nimport { LspBridgeManager, buildLspEnvironment } from \"../src/lsp.js\";\n\ninterface Case {\n  /** Why this case is not yet validated on a real run; it then reports as a todo unless NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE names it. */\n  unvalidated?: string;\n  id: string;\n  label: string;\n  cmd: string;\n  /** Run to prove the binary works (exit 0); null when the server has no such mode and the PATH lookup must do. */\n  probe: string[] | null;\n  files: Record<string, string>;\n  bad: string;\n  clean: string;\n  /** Matches the message of the deliberate error. */\n  message: RegExp;\n  timeoutMs: number;\n}\n\nconst CASES: Case[] = [\n  {\n    id: \"typescript\",\n    label: \"typescript-language-server\",\n    cmd: \"typescript-language-server\",\n    probe: [\"--version\"],\n    files: {\n      \"tsconfig.json\": JSON.stringify({ compilerOptions: { strict: true, noEmit: true, target: \"es2022\", module: \"esnext\" }, include: [\"*.ts\"] }),\n      \"bad.ts\": 'export const n: number = \"not a number\";\\n',\n      \"clean.ts\": \"export const ok: number = 1;\\n\",\n    },\n    bad: \"bad.ts\",\n    clean: \"clean.ts\",\n    message: /string.*not assignable.*number|not assignable/i,\n    timeoutMs: 60_000,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"python\",\n    label: \"pyright-langserver\",\n    cmd: \"pyright-langserver\",\n    probe: null,\n    files: {\n      \"pyrightconfig.json\": JSON.stringify({ typeCheckingMode: \"basic\" }),\n      \"bad.py\": 'x: int = \"not an int\"\\n',\n      \"clean.py\": \"x: int = 1\\n\",\n    },\n    bad: \"bad.py\",\n    clean: \"clean.py\",\n    message: /str.*not assignable.*int|not assignable/i,\n    timeoutMs: 60_000,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"rust\",\n    label: \"rust-analyzer\",\n    cmd: \"rust-analyzer\",\n    probe: [\"--version\"],\n    files: {\n      \"Cargo.toml\": '[package]\\nname = \"lspfixture\"\\nversion = \"0.0.0\"\\nedition = \"2021\"\\n',\n      \"src/main.rs\": 'fn main() {\\n    let x: i32 = \"not an int\";\\n    let _ = x;\\n}\\n',\n      \"src/clean.rs\": \"pub fn ok() -> i32 {\\n    1\\n}\\n\",\n    },\n    bad: \"src/main.rs\",\n    clean: \"src/clean.rs\",\n    message: /mismatched types|expected i32/i,\n    timeoutMs: 120_000,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"go\",\n    label: \"gopls\",\n    cmd: \"gopls\",\n    probe: [\"version\"],\n    files: {\n      \"go.mod\": \"module lspfixture\\n\\ngo 1.21\\n\",\n      \"bad.go\": 'package main\\n\\nfunc main() {\\n\\tvar x int = \"not an int\"\\n\\t_ = x\\n}\\n',\n      \"clean.go\": \"package main\\n\\nfunc ok() int {\\n\\treturn 1\\n}\\n\",\n    },\n    bad: \"bad.go\",\n    clean: \"clean.go\",\n    message: /cannot use|string.*int/i,\n    timeoutMs: 90_000,\n  },\n];\n\nfunction required(id: string): boolean {\n  const v = (process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE ?? \"\").trim().toLowerCase();\n  if (v === \"\") return false;\n  if (v === \"1\" || v === \"all\" || v === \"true\") return true;\n  return v.split(\",\").map((s) => s.trim()).includes(id);\n}\n\n/** Where the product would find `cmd`: the first executable on the PATH it gives a language server for `workspace`. */\nfunction resolveOnProductPath(cmd: string, workspace: string): { bin: string | null; env: Record<string, string> } {\n  const env = buildLspEnvironment(workspace);\n  for (const dir of (env.PATH ?? \"\").split(path.delimiter)) {\n    const candidate = path.join(dir, cmd);\n    try {\n      fs.accessSync(candidate, fs.constants.X_OK);\n      if (fs.statSync(candidate).isFile()) return { bin: candidate, env };\n    } catch {\n      /* next entry */\n    }\n  }\n  return { bin: null, env };\n}\n\n/** null when usable; otherwise the reason it is not. */\nfunction unavailableReason(c: Case, workspace: string): string | null {\n  const { bin, env } = resolveOnProductPath(c.cmd, workspace);\n  if (!bin) return `${c.cmd} is not on the PATH the product gives a language server`;\n  if (c.probe) {\n    // In the fixture directory, as the product launches the server: a rustup proxy picks its toolchain from the working directory (a repo's rust-toolchain.toml would hide a missing default component).\n    const r = spawnSync(bin, c.probe, { env, cwd: workspace, encoding: \"utf-8\", timeout: 20_000 });\n    if (r.status !== 0) {\n      const why = (r.stderr || r.error?.message || `exit ${r.status}`).toString().trim().split(\"\\n\")[0];\n      return `${c.cmd} ${c.probe.join(\" \")} failed under the product environment (${why})`;\n    }\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\nfunction writeFixture(files: Record<string, string>): string {\n  const dir = fs.realpathSync(fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), \"nexus-lsp-real-\")));\n  for (const [name, text] of Object.entries(files)) {\n    fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(path.join(dir, name)), { recursive: true });\n    fs.writeFileSync(path.join(dir, name), text);\n  }\n  return dir;\n}\n\nfor (const c of CASES) {\n  // An unvalidated case still runs and reports; it fails the run only when NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE names it.\n  const todo = c.unvalidated && !required(c.id) ? c.unvalidated : false;\n  test(`real ${c.label}: a deliberate error is reported, a clean file is a real empty report`, { timeout: c.timeoutMs * 2 + 30_000, todo }, async (t) => {\n    const dir = writeFixture(c.files);\n    const reason = unavailableReason(c, dir);\n    if (reason) {\n      if (required(c.id)) assert.fail(`NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE names ${c.id} but ${reason}`);\n      t.skip(`${reason}; install it to run this case (CI sets NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE to make this a failure)`);\n      return;\n    }\n    // Default server table (no `servers` override) and no gate: a trusted, unsandboxed workspace.\n    const mgr = new LspBridgeManager(dir, { gate: () => null });\n    try {\n      const bad = await mgr.diagnostics(c.bad, c.timeoutMs);\n      assert.equal(bad.status, \"ok\", `bad file: ${JSON.stringify(bad)}`);\n      if (bad.status === \"ok\") {\n        const errors = bad.diagnostics.filter((d) => d.severity === \"error\");\n        assert.ok(errors.length >= 1, `expected an error diagnostic, got ${JSON.stringify(bad.diagnostics)}`);\n        assert.ok(errors.some((d) => c.message.test(d.message)), `no error matched ${c.message}: ${JSON.stringify(errors.map((d) => d.message))}`);\n      }\n\n      const clean = await mgr.diagnostics(c.clean, c.timeoutMs);\n      assert.equal(clean.status, \"ok\", `clean file must be a published report, not unavailable: ${JSON.stringify(clean)}`);\n      if (clean.status === \"ok\") assert.deepEqual(clean.diagnostics.filter((d) => d.severity === \"error\"), []);\n    } finally {\n      mgr.shutdownAll();\n    }\n\n    // The contrast: no server for the file's content is \"unavailable\", never an empty ok report.\n    const broken = new LspBridgeManager(dir, { servers: { [path.extname(c.clean)]: { cmd: \"nexus-no-such-language-server\", languageId: c.id } } });\n    try {\n      const res = await broken.diagnostics(c.clean, 1000);\n      assert.equal(res.status, \"unavailable\");\n    } finally {\n      broken.shutdownAll();\n    }\n  });\n}\n\ntest(\"a server named by NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE that is absent fails; an unnamed absent one skips\", () => {\n  const saved = process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE;\n  try {\n    process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE = \"typescript, go\";\n    assert.deepEqual(CASES.map((c) => required(c.id)), [true, false, false, true]);\n    process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE = \"1\";\n    assert.ok(CASES.every((c) => required(c.id)));\n    delete process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE;\n    assert.ok(CASES.every((c) => !required(c.id)));\n  } finally {\n    if (saved === undefined) delete process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE; else process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE = saved;\n  }\n  const d = writeFixture({});\n  const ghost: Case = { ...CASES[0], cmd: \"nexus-no-such-language-server\" };\n  assert.match(unavailableReason(ghost, d) ?? \"\", /not on the PATH/);\n});\n\ntest(\"the default servers for typescript and python are launched with --stdio (they exit without it)\", () => {\n  const dir = writeFixture({});\n  const mgr = new LspBridgeManager(dir);\n  try {\n    for (const f of [\"a.ts\", \"a.tsx\", \"a.js\", \"a.py\"]) assert.deepEqual(mgr.getServerForFile(f)?.args, [\"--stdio\"], f);\n    for (const f of [\"a.rs\", \"a.go\"]) assert.equal(mgr.getServerForFile(f)?.args, undefined, f);\n  } finally {\n    mgr.shutdownAll();\n  }\n});\n\n// Navigation (hover and definition) against the same real servers: a function defined in one file and called in another.\n// A server answers these only once it has analysed the project, so the call is retried until a deadline; a server that never\n// answers is a failure, and an answer is checked for what it names, not merely for existing.\ninterface NavCase {\n  id: string;\n  files: Record<string, string>;\n  /** The call site (1-based line and character, as the product's lsp_hover/lsp_definition take them, on the callee's name) and the file that defines it. */\n  at: { file: string; line: number; character: number };\n  definedIn: string;\n  hoverMentions: RegExp;\n}\n\nconst NAV: NavCase[] = [\n  {\n    id: \"typescript\",\n    files: {\n      \"tsconfig.json\": JSON.stringify({ compilerOptions: { strict: true, noEmit: true, target: \"es2022\", module: \"esnext\", moduleResolution: \"bundler\" }, include: [\"*.ts\"] }),\n      \"lib.ts\": \"export function greet(name: string): string {\\n  return name;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"service.ts\": 'export { greet } from \"./lib\";\\n',\n      \"use.ts\": 'import { greet } from \"./service\";\\nexport const msg: string = greet(\"a\");\\n',\n    },\n    at: { file: \"use.ts\", line: 2, character: 30 },\n    definedIn: \"lib.ts\",\n    hoverMentions: /greet/,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"python\",\n    files: {\n      \"pyrightconfig.json\": JSON.stringify({ typeCheckingMode: \"basic\" }),\n      \"lib.py\": \"def greet(name: str) -> str:\\n    return name\\n\",\n      \"service.py\": \"from lib import greet\\n\",\n      \"use.py\": 'from service import greet\\n\\nmsg: str = greet(\"a\")\\n',\n    },\n    at: { file: \"use.py\", line: 3, character: 13 },\n    definedIn: \"lib.py\",\n    hoverMentions: /greet/,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"rust\",\n    files: {\n      \"Cargo.toml\": '[package]\\nname = \"lspnav\"\\nversion = \"0.0.0\"\\nedition = \"2021\"\\n',\n      \"src/util.rs\": \"pub fn greet(name: &str) -> String {\\n    name.to_string()\\n}\\n\",\n      \"src/service.rs\": \"pub use crate::util::greet;\\n\",\n      \"src/main.rs\": 'mod service;\\nmod util;\\nfn main() {\\n    let _ = service::greet(\"a\");\\n}\\n',\n    },\n    at: { file: \"src/main.rs\", line: 4, character: 25 },\n    definedIn: \"src/util.rs\",\n    hoverMentions: /greet/,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"go\",\n    files: {\n      \"go.mod\": \"module lspnav\\n\\ngo 1.21\\n\",\n      \"lib.go\": \"package main\\n\\nfunc greet(name string) string {\\n\\treturn name\\n}\\n\",\n      \"service.go\": \"package main\\n\\nfunc welcome(name string) string {\\n\\treturn greet(name)\\n}\\n\",\n      \"main.go\": 'package main\\n\\nfunc main() {\\n\\t_ = welcome(\"a\")\\n}\\n',\n    },\n    at: { file: \"main.go\", line: 4, character: 7 },\n    definedIn: \"service.go\",\n    hoverMentions: /welcome/,\n  },\n];\n\nfor (const n of NAV) {\n  const c = CASES.find((x) => x.id === n.id)!;\n  test(`real ${c.label}: hover and definition name the function defined in another file`, { timeout: c.timeoutMs * 2 + 30_000 }, async (t) => {\n    const dir = writeFixture(n.files);\n    const reason = unavailableReason(c, dir);\n# Real language servers (CS-6)\n\n`cli/src/lsp.ts` (the `lsp_diagnostics`, `lsp_hover` and `lsp_definition` tools) is tested in the ordinary suite against `cli/test/fixtures/fake_lsp_server.mjs`. `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts` also runs the product's own `LspBridgeManager` against real servers on fixture projects written to a temporary directory. Navigation cases use a three-source-file import/re-export chain (or equivalent package-level call chain) and require hover and definition to resolve through that project. The `lsp-real-servers` workflow runs it weekly (Monday 04:17 UTC) and on demand. It is not a required check.\n\nPer server the test asserts three things: a file with one deliberate error yields `status: \"ok\"` with an error diagnostic whose message names it; a clean file yields `status: \"ok\"` with an empty list (a published report, which is not `unavailable`); and a manager whose server cannot start yields `unavailable`.\n\n| Server | Command the product starts | Fixture error | Needed to run |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| typescript | `typescript-language-server --stdio` | `const n: number = \"...\"` under `strict` | `typescript`, `typescript-language-server` |\n| python | `pyright-langserver --stdio` | `x: int = \"...\"` | `pyright` |\n| rust | `rust-analyzer` | `let x: i32 = \"...\"` in a Cargo project | `rust-analyzer` |\n| go | `gopls` | `var x int = \"...\"` in a module | `gopls` |\n\n## Skip versus fail\n\nThe server is looked up on the PATH the product gives a language server (`buildLspEnvironment`: absolute entries only, none inside the workspace), and where the server has a `--version` mode it is run under that same environment. A server that is absent, or present but not runnable there, SKIPS with the reason. `NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE` turns that into a failure: `1` (or `all`) requires every server, a comma list (`typescript,python`) requires those. Ids: `typescript`, `python`, `rust`, `go`. The workflow sets `typescript,python`; rust-analyzer and gopls are best-effort installs there, so their cases run when the install worked and skip otherwise.\n\n```sh\nnpm i -g typescript typescript-language-server pyright   # or put their bin directory on PATH\ncd cli && node scripts/run_tests.mjs test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts\nNEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE=1 node scripts/run_tests.mjs test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts   # missing server = failure\n```\n\nThe suite runs under a throwaway HOME, so a tool installed only for the current user (`pip install --user`, `~/.cargo/bin` reached through the real HOME) is invisible. The workflow installs with `npm -g`, and sets `RUSTUP_HOME` and `CARGO_HOME` for the rustup proxy.\n\n## What it found\n\nThe first real run showed the default launch commands for TypeScript, JavaScript and Python were missing `--stdio`; both servers print a usage error and exit without it, so `lsp_diagnostics` for those files was always `unavailable` in practice. Fixed in `getServerForFile`; a test that needs no server pins the arguments.\n\n## What it does not cover\n\n- The trust and sandbox gates with a real server (they are tested with the fake server; a gated manager never starts one).\n- A larger real project: navigation uses three source files, but there is no large workspace-wide diagnostics fixture, incremental-edit sequence, or server crash/restart scenario.\n- Server versions other than the pinned ones (typescript 5.6.3, typescript-language-server 4.3.4, pyright 1.1.390; gopls (`@latest`: v0.16.2 stopped building on a newer runner Go on 2026-11-18) and the runner's rust-analyzer are unpinned).\n- Hosted-runner result (manual run 44323291710 on `3732d911`, 2026-11-18): **typescript-language-server, pyright and gopls passed** (a deliberate error reported, a clean file a real empty report, a broken server `unavailable`), which also confirms on real servers that the default launch needs `--stdio`. **rust-analyzer failed**: the product reported \"not installed or failed to start\" although the rustup proxy answered `--version` in the test's probe. The cause is not diagnosed (it needs a host with the rust-analyzer component to reproduce); the Rust case therefore runs as a `todo` (reported, not failing the job) unless `NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE` names it, and the workflow requires `typescript,python,go`. A manual run of that change on its branch (run 44323915622) completed successfully: 5 pass, 0 fail, 1 todo (Rust).\n- **rust-analyzer (2026-11-18, reproduced locally in an isolated rustup home):** two separate problems, both fixed. (1) *Setup:* the workflow added the component to the toolchain pinned by the repository's `rust-toolchain.toml`, but the product launches the server in a fixture directory outside the repository, where rustup uses the **default** toolchain, which had no rust-analyzer (\"failed to start\" in about 150 ms); the test's `--version` probe ran from the repository and could not see it. The probe now runs in the fixture directory and the workflow adds the component from a directory with no override. (2) *Product behaviour:* `lsp_diagnostics` used to return on the first `publishDiagnostics`, which rust-analyzer sends **empty** before it has loaded the Cargo project and analysed types, so a file with a type error was reported clean. A server can now be marked `awaitProgress` (rust-analyzer is, in the built-in table): the client advertises `window.workDoneProgress`, answers the server's own requests (`window/workDoneProgress/create`, `workspace/configuration`, ...), tracks `$/progress`, and after the first publish waits until the server's progress has ended and been quiet for 1.5 s (or, if it reports none, a 4 s grace), always within the caller's timeout. Server-free tests (`lsp_progress.test.ts`) pin it with a fake staged server (four mutations each fail a test); the real-server case passes locally against rust-analyzer 1.100.0 (about 13 s for a first call on a tiny crate).\n- **Hosted validation of this change:** manual run 44334778543 on the branch (2026-11-18): typescript-language-server, pyright, gopls and rust-analyzer all pass (6 tests, 0 fail, 0 todo; Rust about 18 s). Earlier runs of the same workflow found the setup mismatch (44323291710), the empty-first-publish behaviour (44332636397) and a `gopls@v0.16.2` build break on the runner's newer Go (now `@latest`).\n- **Three-file navigation extension:** hosted run [44606102675](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/actions/runs/44606102675) on commit `99c6049f` (2026-11-19) passed all 10 tests with `NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE=typescript,python,go,rust`: all four real servers passed the diagnostics and navigation cases, with 0 failures, skips, or todos. This is a small three-source-file chain, not evidence for large-project behavior.\n- **Hover and definition (CS-6 follow-up):** each real server is asked, through the product, to hover and go to the definition through a three-source-file project (positions are 1-based, as `lsp_hover` and `lsp_definition` take them); the call is retried until the case timeout because servers answer only after analysis, and the definition must point into the defining file. This checks a small import/re-export chain; it does not establish behavior on large projects.\n- **Cost and limit:** a first Rust diagnostics call on a large crate can take as long as the project load, and is bounded by the caller's timeout, after which the existing \"published no diagnostics\" `unavailable` applies. Later calls return as soon as progress is quiet. A change to an already-open file is not waited on separately: the latest published list is returned.\nname: LSP real servers\n\n# Weekly run of the product's language-server client (cli/src/lsp.ts) against REAL servers on a tiny fixture project\n# (CS-6): a deliberate error must be reported, and a clean file must be a published empty report, not \"unavailable\".\n# The ordinary suite exercises the client only against a fake server. Not a required check. See docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md\n# for what it covers and what it does not.\n#\n# typescript-language-server and pyright are installed with npm at pinned versions and are REQUIRED here\n# (NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE): a missing one fails the job. rust-analyzer and gopls are best-effort installs; if one cannot be\n# installed its case skips with a reason in the log.\non:\n  schedule:\n    - cron: \"17 4 * * 1\"\n  workflow_dispatch:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  lsp:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-22.04\n    timeout-minutes: 30\n    env:\n      NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE: typescript,python,go,rust\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@949feb2413d6458794dcd2491c4babbbce0c15c1 # v7.1.0\n        with:\n          node-version: 22.22.1\n      # Installed system-wide (npm -g puts them on an absolute PATH entry): the tests run under a throwaway HOME, where\n      # per-user installs (pip --user) are invisible (the MQ-3 first-run failure).\n      - name: Install typescript-language-server and pyright\n        run: npm install --global --no-audit --no-fund typescript@5.6.3 typescript-language-server@4.3.4 pyright@1.1.390\n      - name: Install rust-analyzer (best effort)\n        continue-on-error: true\n        run: |\n          # The product launches the server in a fixture directory outside this repository, where rustup uses the DEFAULT toolchain,\n          # not the one the repository's rust-toolchain.toml pins: add the component there (from a directory with no override).\n          (cd \"$RUNNER_TEMP\" && rustup component add rust-analyzer)\n          # The rustup proxy finds its toolchain through RUSTUP_HOME / CARGO_HOME; the tests' throwaway HOME hides the defaults.\n          echo \"RUSTUP_HOME=$(rustup show home)\" >> \"$GITHUB_ENV\"\n          echo \"CARGO_HOME=${CARGO_HOME:-$HOME/.cargo}\" >> \"$GITHUB_ENV\"\n      - name: Install gopls (best effort)\n        continue-on-error: true\n        run: |\n          # Not pinned: an old gopls stops building when the runner image moves to a newer Go (gopls v0.16.2 failed to build on 2026-11-18); this job exists to meet the current servers.\n          # @latest may need a newer Go than the runner has and download that toolchain; the proxy can fail transiently (a stream error on 2026-11-18), so retry.\n          for attempt in 1 2 3; do\n            GOBIN=\"$RUNNER_TEMP/gobin\" go install golang.org/x/tools/gopls@latest && break\n            [ \"$attempt\" = 3 ] && exit 1\n            sleep 15\n          done\n          echo \"$RUNNER_TEMP/gobin\" >> \"$GITHUB_PATH\"\n      - run: npm ci --prefix cli\n      - name: Versions on the runner\n        run: |\n          typescript-language-server --version\n          pyright --version\n          rust-analyzer --version || true\n          gopls version || true\n      - name: Run the real-server tests\n        working-directory: cli\n        run: node scripts/run_tests.mjs test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts\n## main...origin/main\nf873787a9f5c56f7fbdfe1dcd837b71ec25d4bca\nf873787a9f5c56f7fbdfe1dcd837b71ec25d4bca\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":42985}},"started_at_ms":1795146347602,"completed_at_ms":1795146347603}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T03:45:47.749Z","ordinal":28628,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1f70f-74e4-7131-9b7e-aebc6fe9f80a","call_id":"call_80c4d34b5daa41ea81ca031519fbc11d","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# Reference coding with XERJ\n\nThe detail behind the \"Reference coding\" rule in `AGENTS.md`: how to reach the local XERJ node, what to query, and what to leave alone.\n\n- Before non-trivial code with a peer-project analogue (feature, protocol, parser, worktree or provider handling, tool, test harness), query the local XERJ node and record what you took, borrowed or rejected in the PR's \"Reference coding\" section. Exempt: trivial edits, doc-only changes, fixes fully specified by a failing test.\n- XERJ is an owner-host tool, not a project dependency (`docs/v1.1.0/DECISIONS.md` §1). Where it is unavailable (contributor machine, CI), write \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" in that section and use `git grep` plus the peer's public source.\n- The node is the user service `xerj` on `http://localhost:9200`; confirm with `systemctl --user is-active xerj` and use that node. Stop a hub with `xerj hub stop`.\n- Corpora: `xerj corpus list`. Retrieval is lexical (BM25), so query with identifiers and words from the code: `xerj code <corpus> \"<words>\" -k 4 --full 600`, `xerj search --prefix <index-prefix> \"<words>\" -k 4`, `xerj def --prefix <prefix> \"<symbol>\"`. The first query can take over a minute; set a timeout.\n- `nexus-cli`, `nexus-core`, `nexus-test`, `nexus-docs` index `origin/main` only (hourly), so open PRs are absent: use `git grep` on your branch.\n- Reference code is read-only inspiration. Follow each result's licence tag; copy verbatim only permissively licensed code, with a citation. Keep XERJ and reference corpora away from held-out evaluation tasks (`docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md`). Keep document text local (`rerank` sends it to a third party). Leave indices you did not create alone.\n- After real use: `xerj feedback --dry-run`, then `xerj feedback --submit --agent \"<model>\" --used-for \"<task>\" --verdict \"<what worked and what did not>\"`, with secrets and private data left out.\n// Real language servers (CS-6). The v1.2.0 LSP support (lsp.ts) is otherwise tested only against test/fixtures/fake_lsp_server.mjs.\n// Here the product's own LspBridgeManager starts the real server, found the way the product finds it (the default\n// extension -> command table, resolved on the scrubbed PATH buildLspEnvironment returns), on a tiny fixture project:\n//   - a file with one deliberate error must yield status \"ok\" with an error diagnostic naming it;\n//   - a clean file must yield status \"ok\" with an EMPTY list (a real report), which is not \"unavailable\";\n//   - a manager whose server cannot start must yield \"unavailable\" (the contrast the two above are read against).\n// A server that is not installed SKIPS with its reason, unless NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE names it (\"1\" or \"all\" = every server,\n// or a comma list such as \"typescript,python\"), in which case its absence is a failure. The scheduled workflow\n// .github/workflows/lsp-real-servers.yml sets it. The availability probe runs with the SAME environment the product\n// gives the server (buildLspEnvironment), so a tool visible only to the shell (a `pip install --user` under another HOME)\n// is reported missing instead of passing the probe and failing later.\nimport { test } from \"node:test\";\nimport assert from \"node:assert/strict\";\nimport { spawnSync } from \"node:child_process\";\nimport * as fs from \"node:fs\";\nimport * as os from \"node:os\";\nimport * as path from \"node:path\";\nimport { LspBridgeManager, buildLspEnvironment } from \"../src/lsp.js\";\n\ninterface Case {\n  /** Why this case is not yet validated on a real run; it then reports as a todo unless NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE names it. */\n  unvalidated?: string;\n  id: string;\n  label: string;\n  cmd: string;\n  /** Run to prove the binary works (exit 0); null when the server has no such mode and the PATH lookup must do. */\n  probe: string[] | null;\n  files: Record<string, string>;\n  bad: string;\n  clean: string;\n  /** Matches the message of the deliberate error. */\n  message: RegExp;\n  timeoutMs: number;\n}\n\nconst CASES: Case[] = [\n  {\n    id: \"typescript\",\n    label: \"typescript-language-server\",\n    cmd: \"typescript-language-server\",\n    probe: [\"--version\"],\n    files: {\n      \"tsconfig.json\": JSON.stringify({ compilerOptions: { strict: true, noEmit: true, target: \"es2022\", module: \"esnext\" }, include: [\"*.ts\"] }),\n      \"bad.ts\": 'export const n: number = \"not a number\";\\n',\n      \"clean.ts\": \"export const ok: number = 1;\\n\",\n    },\n    bad: \"bad.ts\",\n    clean: \"clean.ts\",\n    message: /string.*not assignable.*number|not assignable/i,\n    timeoutMs: 60_000,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"python\",\n    label: \"pyright-langserver\",\n    cmd: \"pyright-langserver\",\n    probe: null,\n    files: {\n      \"pyrightconfig.json\": JSON.stringify({ typeCheckingMode: \"basic\" }),\n      \"bad.py\": 'x: int = \"not an int\"\\n',\n      \"clean.py\": \"x: int = 1\\n\",\n    },\n    bad: \"bad.py\",\n    clean: \"clean.py\",\n    message: /str.*not assignable.*int|not assignable/i,\n    timeoutMs: 60_000,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"rust\",\n    label: \"rust-analyzer\",\n    cmd: \"rust-analyzer\",\n    probe: [\"--version\"],\n    files: {\n      \"Cargo.toml\": '[package]\\nname = \"lspfixture\"\\nversion = \"0.0.0\"\\nedition = \"2021\"\\n',\n      \"src/main.rs\": 'fn main() {\\n    let x: i32 = \"not an int\";\\n    let _ = x;\\n}\\n',\n      \"src/clean.rs\": \"pub fn ok() -> i32 {\\n    1\\n}\\n\",\n    },\n    bad: \"src/main.rs\",\n    clean: \"src/clean.rs\",\n    message: /mismatched types|expected i32/i,\n    timeoutMs: 120_000,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"go\",\n    label: \"gopls\",\n    cmd: \"gopls\",\n    probe: [\"version\"],\n    files: {\n      \"go.mod\": \"module lspfixture\\n\\ngo 1.21\\n\",\n      \"bad.go\": 'package main\\n\\nfunc main() {\\n\\tvar x int = \"not an int\"\\n\\t_ = x\\n}\\n',\n      \"clean.go\": \"package main\\n\\nfunc ok() int {\\n\\treturn 1\\n}\\n\",\n    },\n    bad: \"bad.go\",\n    clean: \"clean.go\",\n    message: /cannot use|string.*int/i,\n    timeoutMs: 90_000,\n  },\n];\n\nfunction required(id: string): boolean {\n  const v = (process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE ?? \"\").trim().toLowerCase();\n  if (v === \"\") return false;\n  if (v === \"1\" || v === \"all\" || v === \"true\") return true;\n  return v.split(\",\").map((s) => s.trim()).includes(id);\n}\n\n/** Where the product would find `cmd`: the first executable on the PATH it gives a language server for `workspace`. */\nfunction resolveOnProductPath(cmd: string, workspace: string): { bin: string | null; env: Record<string, string> } {\n  const env = buildLspEnvironment(workspace);\n  for (const dir of (env.PATH ?? \"\").split(path.delimiter)) {\n    const candidate = path.join(dir, cmd);\n    try {\n      fs.accessSync(candidate, fs.constants.X_OK);\n      if (fs.statSync(candidate).isFile()) return { bin: candidate, env };\n    } catch {\n      /* next entry */\n    }\n  }\n  return { bin: null, env };\n}\n\n/** null when usable; otherwise the reason it is not. */\nfunction unavailableReason(c: Case, workspace: string): string | null {\n  const { bin, env } = resolveOnProductPath(c.cmd, workspace);\n  if (!bin) return `${c.cmd} is not on the PATH the product gives a language server`;\n  if (c.probe) {\n    // In the fixture directory, as the product launches the server: a rustup proxy picks its toolchain from the working directory (a repo's rust-toolchain.toml would hide a missing default component).\n    const r = spawnSync(bin, c.probe, { env, cwd: workspace, encoding: \"utf-8\", timeout: 20_000 });\n    if (r.status !== 0) {\n      const why = (r.stderr || r.error?.message || `exit ${r.status}`).toString().trim().split(\"\\n\")[0];\n      return `${c.cmd} ${c.probe.join(\" \")} failed under the product environment (${why})`;\n    }\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\nfunction writeFixture(files: Record<string, string>): string {\n  const dir = fs.realpathSync(fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), \"nexus-lsp-real-\")));\n  for (const [name, text] of Object.entries(files)) {\n    fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(path.join(dir, name)), { recursive: true });\n    fs.writeFileSync(path.join(dir, name), text);\n  }\n  return dir;\n}\n\nfor (const c of CASES) {\n  // An unvalidated case still runs and reports; it fails the run only when NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE names it.\n  const todo = c.unvalidated && !required(c.id) ? c.unvalidated : false;\n  test(`real ${c.label}: a deliberate error is reported, a clean file is a real empty report`, { timeout: c.timeoutMs * 2 + 30_000, todo }, async (t) => {\n    const dir = writeFixture(c.files);\n    const reason = unavailableReason(c, dir);\n    if (reason) {\n      if (required(c.id)) assert.fail(`NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE names ${c.id} but ${reason}`);\n      t.skip(`${reason}; install it to run this case (CI sets NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE to make this a failure)`);\n      return;\n    }\n    // Default server table (no `servers` override) and no gate: a trusted, unsandboxed workspace.\n    const mgr = new LspBridgeManager(dir, { gate: () => null });\n    try {\n      const bad = await mgr.diagnostics(c.bad, c.timeoutMs);\n      assert.equal(bad.status, \"ok\", `bad file: ${JSON.stringify(bad)}`);\n      if (bad.status === \"ok\") {\n        const errors = bad.diagnostics.filter((d) => d.severity === \"error\");\n        assert.ok(errors.length >= 1, `expected an error diagnostic, got ${JSON.stringify(bad.diagnostics)}`);\n        assert.ok(errors.some((d) => c.message.test(d.message)), `no error matched ${c.message}: ${JSON.stringify(errors.map((d) => d.message))}`);\n      }\n\n      const clean = await mgr.diagnostics(c.clean, c.timeoutMs);\n      assert.equal(clean.status, \"ok\", `clean file must be a published report, not unavailable: ${JSON.stringify(clean)}`);\n      if (clean.status === \"ok\") assert.deepEqual(clean.diagnostics.filter((d) => d.severity === \"error\"), []);\n    } finally {\n      mgr.shutdownAll();\n    }\n\n    // The contrast: no server for the file's content is \"unavailable\", never an empty ok report.\n    const broken = new LspBridgeManager(dir, { servers: { [path.extname(c.clean)]: { cmd: \"nexus-no-such-language-server\", languageId: c.id } } });\n    try {\n      const res = await broken.diagnostics(c.clean, 1000);\n      assert.equal(res.status, \"unavailable\");\n    } finally {\n      broken.shutdownAll();\n    }\n  });\n}\n\ntest(\"a server named by NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE that is absent fails; an unnamed absent one skips\", () => {\n  const saved = process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE;\n  try {\n    process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE = \"typescript, go\";\n    assert.deepEqual(CASES.map((c) => required(c.id)), [true, false, false, true]);\n    process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE = \"1\";\n    assert.ok(CASES.every((c) => required(c.id)));\n    delete process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE;\n    assert.ok(CASES.every((c) => !required(c.id)));\n  } finally {\n    if (saved === undefined) delete process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE; else process.env.NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE = saved;\n  }\n  const d = writeFixture({});\n  const ghost: Case = { ...CASES[0], cmd: \"nexus-no-such-language-server\" };\n  assert.match(unavailableReason(ghost, d) ?? \"\", /not on the PATH/);\n});\n\ntest(\"the default servers for typescript and python are launched with --stdio (they exit without it)\", () => {\n  const dir = writeFixture({});\n  const mgr = new LspBridgeManager(dir);\n  try {\n    for (const f of [\"a.ts\", \"a.tsx\", \"a.js\", \"a.py\"]) assert.deepEqual(mgr.getServerForFile(f)?.args, [\"--stdio\"], f);\n    for (const f of [\"a.rs\", \"a.go\"]) assert.equal(mgr.getServerForFile(f)?.args, undefined, f);\n  } finally {\n    mgr.shutdownAll();\n  }\n});\n\n// Navigation (hover and definition) against the same real servers: a function defined in one file and called in another.\n// A server answers these only once it has analysed the project, so the call is retried until a deadline; a server that never\n// answers is a failure, and an answer is checked for what it names, not merely for existing.\ninterface NavCase {\n  id: string;\n  files: Record<string, string>;\n  /** The call site (1-based line and character, as the product's lsp_hover/lsp_definition take them, on the callee's name) and the file that defines it. */\n  at: { file: string; line: number; character: number };\n  definedIn: string;\n  hoverMentions: RegExp;\n}\n\nconst NAV: NavCase[] = [\n  {\n    id: \"typescript\",\n    files: {\n      \"tsconfig.json\": JSON.stringify({ compilerOptions: { strict: true, noEmit: true, target: \"es2022\", module: \"esnext\", moduleResolution: \"bundler\" }, include: [\"*.ts\"] }),\n      \"lib.ts\": \"export function greet(name: string): string {\\n  return name;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"service.ts\": 'export { greet } from \"./lib\";\\n',\n      \"use.ts\": 'import { greet } from \"./service\";\\nexport const msg: string = greet(\"a\");\\n',\n    },\n    at: { file: \"use.ts\", line: 2, character: 30 },\n    definedIn: \"lib.ts\",\n    hoverMentions: /greet/,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"python\",\n    files: {\n      \"pyrightconfig.json\": JSON.stringify({ typeCheckingMode: \"basic\" }),\n      \"lib.py\": \"def greet(name: str) -> str:\\n    return name\\n\",\n      \"service.py\": \"from lib import greet\\n\",\n      \"use.py\": 'from service import greet\\n\\nmsg: str = greet(\"a\")\\n',\n    },\n    at: { file: \"use.py\", line: 3, character: 13 },\n    definedIn: \"lib.py\",\n    hoverMentions: /greet/,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"rust\",\n    files: {\n      \"Cargo.toml\": '[package]\\nname = \"lspnav\"\\nversion = \"0.0.0\"\\nedition = \"2021\"\\n',\n      \"src/util.rs\": \"pub fn greet(name: &str) -> String {\\n    name.to_string()\\n}\\n\",\n      \"src/service.rs\": \"pub use crate::util::greet;\\n\",\n      \"src/main.rs\": 'mod service;\\nmod util;\\nfn main() {\\n    let _ = service::greet(\"a\");\\n}\\n',\n    },\n    at: { file: \"src/main.rs\", line: 4, character: 25 },\n    definedIn: \"src/util.rs\",\n    hoverMentions: /greet/,\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"go\",\n    files: {\n      \"go.mod\": \"module lspnav\\n\\ngo 1.21\\n\",\n      \"lib.go\": \"package main\\n\\nfunc greet(name string) string {\\n\\treturn name\\n}\\n\",\n      \"service.go\": \"package main\\n\\nfunc welcome(name string) string {\\n\\treturn greet(name)\\n}\\n\",\n      \"main.go\": 'package main\\n\\nfunc main() {\\n\\t_ = welcome(\"a\")\\n}\\n',\n    },\n    at: { file: \"main.go\", line: 4, character: 7 },\n    definedIn: \"service.go\",\n    hoverMentions: /welcome/,\n  },\n];\n\nfor (const n of NAV) {\n  const c = CASES.find((x) => x.id === n.id)!;\n  test(`real ${c.label}: hover and definition name the function defined in another file`, { timeout: c.timeoutMs * 2 + 30_000 }, async (t) => {\n    const dir = writeFixture(n.files);\n    const reason = unavailableReason(c, dir);\n# Real language servers (CS-6)\n\n`cli/src/lsp.ts` (the `lsp_diagnostics`, `lsp_hover` and `lsp_definition` tools) is tested in the ordinary suite against `cli/test/fixtures/fake_lsp_server.mjs`. `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts` also runs the product's own `LspBridgeManager` against real servers on fixture projects written to a temporary directory. Navigation cases use a three-source-file import/re-export chain (or equivalent package-level call chain) and require hover and definition to resolve through that project. The `lsp-real-servers` workflow runs it weekly (Monday 04:17 UTC) and on demand. It is not a required check.\n\nPer server the test asserts three things: a file with one deliberate error yields `status: \"ok\"` with an error diagnostic whose message names it; a clean file yields `status: \"ok\"` with an empty list (a published report, which is not `unavailable`); and a manager whose server cannot start yields `unavailable`.\n\n| Server | Command the product starts | Fixture error | Needed to run |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| typescript | `typescript-language-server --stdio` | `const n: number = \"...\"` under `strict` | `typescript`, `typescript-language-server` |\n| python | `pyright-langserver --stdio` | `x: int = \"...\"` | `pyright` |\n| rust | `rust-analyzer` | `let x: i32 = \"...\"` in a Cargo project | `rust-analyzer` |\n| go | `gopls` | `var x int = \"...\"` in a module | `gopls` |\n\n## Skip versus fail\n\nThe server is looked up on the PATH the product gives a language server (`buildLspEnvironment`: absolute entries only, none inside the workspace), and where the server has a `--version` mode it is run under that same environment. A server that is absent, or present but not runnable there, SKIPS with the reason. `NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE` turns that into a failure: `1` (or `all`) requires every server, a comma list (`typescript,python`) requires those. Ids: `typescript`, `python`, `rust`, `go`. The workflow sets `typescript,python`; rust-analyzer and gopls are best-effort installs there, so their cases run when the install worked and skip otherwise.\n\n```sh\nnpm i -g typescript typescript-language-server pyright   # or put their bin directory on PATH\ncd cli && node scripts/run_tests.mjs test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts\nNEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE=1 node scripts/run_tests.mjs test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts   # missing server = failure\n```\n\nThe suite runs under a throwaway HOME, so a tool installed only for the current user (`pip install --user`, `~/.cargo/bin` reached through the real HOME) is invisible. The workflow installs with `npm -g`, and sets `RUSTUP_HOME` and `CARGO_HOME` for the rustup proxy.\n\n## What it found\n\nThe first real run showed the default launch commands for TypeScript, JavaScript and Python were missing `--stdio`; both servers print a usage error and exit without it, so `lsp_diagnostics` for those files was always `unavailable` in practice. Fixed in `getServerForFile`; a test that needs no server pins the arguments.\n\n## What it does not cover\n\n- The trust and sandbox gates with a real server (they are tested with the fake server; a gated manager never starts one).\n- A larger real project: navigation uses three source files, but there is no large workspace-wide diagnostics fixture, incremental-edit sequence, or server crash/restart scenario.\n- Server versions other than the pinned ones (typescript 5.6.3, typescript-language-server 4.3.4, pyright 1.1.390; gopls (`@latest`: v0.16.2 stopped building on a newer runner Go on 2026-11-18) and the runner's rust-analyzer are unpinned).\n- Hosted-runner result (manual run 44323291710 on `3732d911`, 2026-11-18): **typescript-language-server, pyright and gopls passed** (a deliberate error reported, a clean file a real empty report, a broken server `unavailable`), which also confirms on real servers that the default launch needs `--stdio`. **rust-analyzer failed**: the product reported \"not installed or failed to start\" although the rustup proxy answered `--version` in the test's probe. The cause is not diagnosed (it needs a host with the rust-analyzer component to reproduce); the Rust case therefore runs as a `todo` (reported, not failing the job) unless `NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE` names it, and the workflow requires `typescript,python,go`. A manual run of that change on its branch (run 44323915622) completed successfully: 5 pass, 0 fail, 1 todo (Rust).\n- **rust-analyzer (2026-11-18, reproduced locally in an isolated rustup home):** two separate problems, both fixed. (1) *Setup:* the workflow added the component to the toolchain pinned by the repository's `rust-toolchain.toml`, but the product launches the server in a fixture directory outside the repository, where rustup uses the **default** toolchain, which had no rust-analyzer (\"failed to start\" in about 150 ms); the test's `--version` probe ran from the repository and could not see it. The probe now runs in the fixture directory and the workflow adds the component from a directory with no override. (2) *Product behaviour:* `lsp_diagnostics` used to return on the first `publishDiagnostics`, which rust-analyzer sends **empty** before it has loaded the Cargo project and analysed types, so a file with a type error was reported clean. A server can now be marked `awaitProgress` (rust-analyzer is, in the built-in table): the client advertises `window.workDoneProgress`, answers the server's own requests (`window/workDoneProgress/create`, `workspace/configuration`, ...), tracks `$/progress`, and after the first publish waits until the server's progress has ended and been quiet for 1.5 s (or, if it reports none, a 4 s grace), always within the caller's timeout. Server-free tests (`lsp_progress.test.ts`) pin it with a fake staged server (four mutations each fail a test); the real-server case passes locally against rust-analyzer 1.100.0 (about 13 s for a first call on a tiny crate).\n- **Hosted validation of this change:** manual run 44334778543 on the branch (2026-11-18): typescript-language-server, pyright, gopls and rust-analyzer all pass (6 tests, 0 fail, 0 todo; Rust about 18 s). Earlier runs of the same workflow found the setup mismatch (44323291710), the empty-first-publish behaviour (44332636397) and a `gopls@v0.16.2` build break on the runner's newer Go (now `@latest`).\n- **Three-file navigation extension:** hosted run [44606102675](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/actions/runs/44606102675) on commit `99c6049f` (2026-11-19) passed all 10 tests with `NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE=typescript,python,go,rust`: all four real servers passed the diagnostics and navigation cases, with 0 failures, skips, or todos. This is a small three-source-file chain, not evidence for large-project behavior.\n- **Hover and definition (CS-6 follow-up):** each real server is asked, through the product, to hover and go to the definition through a three-source-file project (positions are 1-based, as `lsp_hover` and `lsp_definition` take them); the call is retried until the case timeout because servers answer only after analysis, and the definition must point into the defining file. This checks a small import/re-export chain; it does not establish behavior on large projects.\n- **Cost and limit:** a first Rust diagnostics call on a large crate can take as long as the project load, and is bounded by the caller's timeout, after which the existing \"published no diagnostics\" `unavailable` applies. Later calls return as soon as progress is quiet. A change to an already-open file is not waited on separately: the latest published list is returned.\nname: LSP real servers\n\n# Weekly run of the product's language-server client (cli/src/lsp.ts) against REAL servers on a tiny fixture project\n# (CS-6): a deliberate error must be reported, and a clean file must be a published empty report, not \"unavailable\".\n# The ordinary suite exercises the client only against a fake server. Not a required check. See docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md\n# for what it covers and what it does not.\n#\n# typescript-language-server and pyright are installed with npm at pinned versions and are REQUIRED here\n# (NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE): a missing one fails the job. rust-analyzer and gopls are best-effort installs; if one cannot be\n# installed its case skips with a reason in the log.\non:\n  schedule:\n    - cron: \"17 4 * * 1\"\n  workflow_dispatch:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  lsp:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-22.04\n    timeout-minutes: 30\n    env:\n      NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE: typescript,python,go,rust\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@949feb2413d6458794dcd2491c4babbbce0c15c1 # v7.1.0\n        with:\n          node-version: 22.22.1\n      # Installed system-wide (npm -g puts them on an absolute PATH entry): the tests run under a throwaway HOME, where\n      # per-user installs (pip --user) are invisible (the MQ-3 first-run failure).\n      - name: Install typescript-language-server and pyright\n        run: npm install --global --no-audit --no-fund typescript@5.6.3 typescript-language-server@4.3.4 pyright@1.1.390\n      - name: Install rust-analyzer (best effort)\n        continue-on-error: true\n        run: |\n          # The product launches the server in a fixture directory outside this repository, where rustup uses the DEFAULT toolchain,\n          # not the one the repository's rust-toolchain.toml pins: add the component there (from a directory with no override).\n          (cd \"$RUNNER_TEMP\" && rustup component add rust-analyzer)\n          # The rustup proxy finds its toolchain through RUSTUP_HOME / CARGO_HOME; the tests' throwaway HOME hides the defaults.\n          echo \"RUSTUP_HOME=$(rustup show home)\" >> \"$GITHUB_ENV\"\n          echo \"CARGO_HOME=${CARGO_HOME:-$HOME/.cargo}\" >> \"$GITHUB_ENV\"\n      - name: Install gopls (best effort)\n        continue-on-error: true\n        run: |\n          # Not pinned: an old gopls stops building when the runner image moves to a newer Go (gopls v0.16.2 failed to build on 2026-11-18); this job exists to meet the current servers.\n          # @latest may need a newer Go than the runner has and download that toolchain; the proxy can fail transiently (a stream error on 2026-11-18), so retry.\n          for attempt in 1 2 3; do\n            GOBIN=\"$RUNNER_TEMP/gobin\" go install golang.org/x/tools/gopls@latest && break\n            [ \"$attempt\" = 3 ] && exit 1\n            sleep 15\n          done\n          echo \"$RUNNER_TEMP/gobin\" >> \"$GITHUB_PATH\"\n      - run: npm ci --prefix cli\n      - name: Versions on the runner\n        run: |\n          typescript-language-server --version\n          pyright --version\n          rust-analyzer --version || true\n          gopls version || true\n      - name: Run the real-server tests\n        working-directory: cli\n        run: node scripts/run_tests.mjs test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts\n## main...origin/main\nf873787a9f5c56f7fbdfe1dcd837b71ec25d4bca\nf873787a9f5c56f7fbdfe1dcd837b71ec25d4bca\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1f70b-846a-7860-9aa2-ec3e0d5fdc10","create_time":1795146347.7489624}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":12626,"sampled_through_sequence":12620,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
