{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:20:07.969Z","ordinal":29764,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_05793ea695c2aa2c016affca75c72087d2855463aceebaad60","status":"completed","call_id":"call_f1c3fc67e37b46d5966244685c27e85c","name":"exec","input":"const r=await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rg -n -i 'provider matrix|cache.*share|cached.*tokens|prompt.cache' docs | head -120; find \\\"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\\\" -maxdepth 1 -type d -name '*PROVIDER*' -o -type d -name '*CLOUD*' | sed 's#.*/##'\",workdir:\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",yield_time_ms:10000,max_output_tokens:2000});text(r.output)\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","create_time":1795148405.7804236}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":13131,"sampled_through_sequence":13129,"client_authored":false}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:20:08.014Z","ordinal":29765,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","session_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","root_turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","response_id":"resp_05793ea695c2aa2c016affca71cf8087d2a5bfaf0c911ad0aa","usage":{"input_tokens":241133,"cached_input_tokens":235648,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":284,"reasoning_output_tokens":156,"total_tokens":241417},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":3456235,"cached_input_tokens":3431936,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":6647,"reasoning_output_tokens":4476,"total_tokens":3462882},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":624030305,"cached_input_tokens":617073152,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1335681,"reasoning_output_tokens":706656,"total_tokens":625365986}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:20:08.198Z","ordinal":29766,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-6af09fe8-a3c7-49c7-80bd-49e6bc02b4c5","process_id":"59741","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n -i 'provider matrix|cache.*share|cached.*tokens|prompt.cache' docs | head -120; find \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\" -maxdepth 1 -type d -name '*PROVIDER*' -o -type d -name '*CLOUD*' | sed 's#.*/##'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rg -n -i 'provider matrix|cache.*share|cached.*tokens|prompt.cache' docs | head -120; find \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\" -maxdepth 1 -type d -name '*PROVIDER*' -o -type d -name '*CLOUD*' | sed 's#.*/##'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:107:| CX-4 | Tool-output masking above a token threshold, with the full output retrievable by id (references: Gemini CLI `toolOutputMaskingService.ts:25`, Cline cached tool results). A masked output stays in the evidence store, and the independent reviewer's package (requirements, scope, diff, verifier receipts) does not depend on conversation context, so masking cannot remove evidence (read in `independent_review.ts`, 2026-11-15) | request-shape snapshot shows the mask marker; the id resolves; the reviewer package is identical before and after masking | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL, opt-in. Done: `NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS=<n>` masks, in the request only, a tool result above n estimated tokens once the model has acted on it (a later assistant message exists); the marker names a content id and the full output resolves with `read_archive` query `tool-output:<id>` (live conversation, then archive). Never masked: results not yet acted on, `read_archive`/`read_findings`/`read_objective` results, the pinned ledger, findings and durable records (system messages). Stored conversation and sessions are unchanged. `cli/src/core/tool_output_mask.ts`, `cli/test/tool_output_mask.test.ts` (request-shape marker, id resolves, ledger intact, off by default). The reviewer package is built from the diff and verifier receipts (`operation_runner_execution.ts` `reviewEvidence`), not the conversation, and the review module does not import masking; the test pins that, but it is a structural guard, not an end-to-end review run. Decision: default off, because safe-by-default would need a measured threshold on real tasks (masking can cost the model a retrieval step); a retrieved output enters the conversation whole and is not masked again. Not done: a dedicated id-read tool (the existing archive tool is reused), a default threshold, masking measured on the eval set.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:109:| CX-6 | Prompt caching: request cache hints for providers that support them; measure cached-token share | share reported in the matrix | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL. Done: Anthropic `cache_control` breakpoints (system prompt, pinned ledger) behind the provider's declared `promptCache` capability and the operator's `NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`; request-shape test shows the marker; the usage summary reports the cached share (`cached N of M input tokens (P%)`). Not done: default-on (needs live Anthropic verification, none made), Bedrock `cachePoint`, operator `providers.json` declaration of the capability, a cached share in the provider matrix doc. OpenAI and Gemini cache implicitly and need no hint.\ndocs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md:56:| WP-1107 — GBNF tool grammar | Generate tool-schema grammars and integrate supported provider paths. | Commit `1518780d` adds the bounded schema compiler. Commits through `0d506762` connect strict function schemas on the **vLLM request path only**; llama.cpp and Ollama requests keep their native tool schemas (no grammar or format constraint), and the hybrid reasoning grammars in the roadmap are not implemented. The owner-approved full v1.1.2 scope supersedes the older deferral in `ROADMAP-REVIEW.md` §2.5. | **REDUCED SCOPE DELIVERED; SOURCE GATES PASS; INDEPENDENT REVIEW REQUEST CHANGES, FINDINGS WAIVED; LIVE PROVIDER MATRIX NOT MEASURED** | Open items are listed in [the release notes](v1.1.2.md#provider-handling-wp-1107): no expiry for the server-kind cache, `X-Task-Class` can reach a replaced non-gateway server, null normalisation gaps, request counting can turn off, matrix provenance not bound to checkout content. G1.1-4 was not measured. |\ndocs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md:95:**Not done in this record.** No release package for the final stable version was built here, installed, or acceptance-tested (the package rows below are for earlier source). The supported-provider matrix (G1.1-4) was not run. Release-level PTY evidence and installed trace capture on the final artifact were not collected.\ndocs/CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md:215:Prompt caching is opt-in: with `NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, the built-in Anthropic provider (the only one that declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) sends `cache_control` breakpoints on the system prompt and the pinned ledger. It has not been verified against the live API and Anthropic ignores a prefix below its minimum cacheable length, so a short prompt shows no cache hits. Other protocols cache automatically on the provider side (OpenAI, Gemini) or need provider-specific markers not implemented here (Bedrock `cachePoint`). The usage summary reports `cached N of M input tokens (P%)` whenever a provider reported cache counts.\ndocs/CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md:280:and killed after 15 s. Tokens are cached and refreshed one minute before expiry; concurrent requests share one refresh;\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:264:**RC and remaining acceptance:** Public [v1.0.0-rc.10](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/releases/tag/v1.0.0-rc.10) remains an unaccepted prerelease from source `16ee8ca3643824741b6da67936b41f262414fdf0`, not the current main source. PR #265's merged OpenRouter evidence covers one pinned-model verification and one synthetic small task. Issue [#1](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/issues/1) remains open without custodian acceptance; no held-out task payload was accessed or run. Installed exact-RC qualification, standalone/provider matrix coverage, T2/T3/T4, the complete terminal matrix, and independent exact-RC acceptance remain incomplete. No stable `v1.0.0` tag or release is published. Existing publication authorization remains gated on every mandatory acceptance gate.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:751:5. **Stage 4 — Installed-RC qualification:** Install the workflow-produced `v1.0.0-rc.1` package via the documented installer and execute `ACCEPTANCE.md` §§4–8 (standalone/provider matrix, CI/scripted/manual terminal matrix, T2 40×5 held-out comparison, T4 9×5 fault matrix, T3 8×5 long-task matrix, install matrix, SPEC-09 §3.2 docs checklist, and §8 independent acceptance). Archive all evidence under `docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/v1.0.0-rc.1/`.\ndocs/v1.1.4/FINDINGS.md:96:| A4a (WP-1316 supported domain) | P2 | **OPEN, accepted limitation, for the owner at release review.** Three Entire review rounds each fixed one exotic git state and exposed the next, so per-state handling was replaced by one predicate, `snapshotSupport` (`cli/src/core/specialist_inputs.ts`), applied by `candidateDiff` and by the debugger snapshot (`snapshotCandidate`). **Supported:** a stage-0 index of files and symlinks (every `ls-files -v` tag `H`); no sparse checkout; `core.filemode` and `core.symlinks` true; `core.ignorecase` not true; no gitlink (submodule); no unresolved merge; a workspace at the repository root; SHA-1 objects; every path valid UTF-8; no `filter=` attribute and no `.gitattributes` over 1 MiB or unreadable. Exercised by the sweep in `cli/test/specialist_inputs_domain.test.ts` and not refused: detached HEAD, linked worktrees, untracked, ignored and nested-repository paths (in a plain checkout), intent-to-add entries, renames and copies, symlinks, a merge or cherry-pick resolved in the index. **Refused:** each state above that is not supported. **Checkout trust:** both builders run git only after `trustedGitArgs` (`verifyCheckout`) and against the pinned `--git-dir`/`--work-tree`, so a unit worktree whose `.git` pointer was replaced, or that holds a planted repository (a nested repository included), is refused and nothing of it is read or copied. **What each consumer sees on a refusal:** `security_reviewer` runs without the diff and is told why (`candidate diff unavailable: ...`); `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` fall back to a plain `git diff` against the base (`plainCandidateDiff`: private base-tree index, protected paths and protected content left out, no driver run, same checkout trust) and are told it is a plain diff, so a domain refusal does not block the review (an untrusted checkout, a driver refusal, a path count over 2,000, a timeout or any other git failure still blocks; the plain diff refuses (explicit error) when any path in the base tree, index or untracked listing is not valid UTF-8, even an unrelated one, and does not show a force-added ignored new file); a `failure_debugger` cannot be given a snapshot of a refused candidate, so the unit fails with the reason and an operator retry (fix the state, then retry) rather than debugging a wrong tree. A failed rebuild of a stale snapshot leaves the old worktree and branch in place. **Protected-content comparison (new files vs protected sources):** sound only for regular, readable files within the size bound; a protected path that is a symlink (never dereferenced), a special file, a directory in its place, oversized or unreadable means 'cannot compare' and is treated like a protected change: every new file is omitted and counted (`omittedNew`). While a readable protected source exists, every NEW symlink or other non-regular file is omitted too (a link to a directory is followed by `git diff --no-index`). `mv .env notes.txt` of an ignored, untracked `.env` is not detectable (the source is gone from disk and was never tracked): only credential redaction guards it. Protected sources are read through a no-follow descriptor whose real path is checked inside the repository on Linux (a parent directory swapped for a link after the check is refused); on other platforms that window is narrowed, not closed. Out of scope: encoded or re-formatted copies (base64, quoting, values only) and lines under 8 characters; hard links are regular files and are compared. Residual: lines pasted into a file that already existed (status M) are never line-compared; a commented-out secret line is not matched; in the plain diff for a subdirectory workspace the protected-source listings are relative to that subdirectory; `.nexus` links and files over the bound are left out by design; the snapshot does not run the comparison. The plain review diff keeps the real index's staged removals (`git rm --cached` of a file that stays on disk). **Known gaps, not claims of coverage:** the snapshot (`add -A`) and the diff leave out ignored files (`node_modules`, build output, `.env`), so a failure that depends on them is not reproduced; a `-diff` attribute can push the diff over its size limit (reported as too large); git without `GIT_ATTR_SOURCE` silently ignores it (the snapshot's `--path-format=absolute` is the only option here that makes an old git error), so a worktree `.gitattributes` driver would apply there and is caught only by the `filter=` refusal and `check-attr`; the predicate is evaluated once and the listings run after it, so a concurrent index change in a shared (non-isolated) checkout can slip between them; a partial clone is protected from lazy fetch only through `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH` (a newer git; older git ignores it); an unreadable file or an old git fails closed with a raw git message. SHA-256 refusal is tested for the diff only. A finding outside this list is still possible. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:22:| CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\ndocs/v1.1.0/WP-1107-PLAN.md:57:- Tests: `subsequent context discovery reuses cached advertisement without hitting probe endpoint again`; the three standalone profile tests assert positive detected kinds; `REQ-AGT-118: /tokenize works on llama.cpp (root /tokenize { tokens }) and vLLM (root /tokenize { count })` uses the documented vLLM identity response.\ndocs/ARCHITECTURE.md:64:   - *Filesystem:* the host root is bound read-only (`--ro-bind / /`, remounted read-only) with a fresh `/dev`, `/proc` and an empty `/tmp`; the workspace is bound writable. `$HOME` is replaced by an empty tmpfs, so `~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, `~/.gnupg`, `~/.config/nexus` and browser profiles do not exist inside. Only toolchain directories are bound back read-only: `~/.cargo`, `~/.rustup`, `~/.nvm`, `~/.local/bin`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cache/pip`, `~/go/pkg`, the `PATH` entries that lie under `$HOME` (with their `<prefix>` for a `.../bin` entry), and the paths the operator lists in `NEXUS_SANDBOX_HOME_READONLY` (comma-separated; operator environment or `config.env` only, never a project `.env`). A candidate that is, contains or sits inside a credential location (the same list plus `~/.config`, `~/.local/share`, `~/.cache` as whole directories) is refused, including through a symlink. `~/.cargo/credentials` and `~/.cargo/credentials.toml` are masked with `/dev/null`. `/run/user/<uid>` (ssh/gpg agents, user bus) is an empty tmpfs, and `/run/docker.sock` and `/var/run/docker.sock` are masked. The workspace bind is applied on top of the masked `$HOME`, so when the workspace is `$HOME`, or contains a credential path, those paths are overlaid (a tmpfs for a directory, `/dev/null` for a file) after the workspace bind; they appear empty. `nexus doctor` (`sandbox.confinement`) lists the effective mounts, masks and refusals.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-10-v1-qualification-and-evaluation.md:82:7. **REQ-QUAL-107. Standalone and provider matrix.** The live standalone set (a T2 subset of ≥ 10 tasks plus a T4 subset) is run through the gateway-stripping proxy in its plain profile and its emulated-server profiles (DEC-16). `providers verify` is run live for each provider listed as supported (DEC-06). Receipts are archived.\ndocs/reviews/evidence/nexus_maturity_2026-10-11_verification/token_accounting.json:20:  \"sum_cached_input_tokens\": 606720,\ndocs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md:50:| REQ-QUAL-107 | ACC-PRV-STANDALONE, ACC-PRV-LIVE | Installed-RC standalone and provider matrix |\ndocs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md:150:### 4.3 Provider matrix (E5)\ndocs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md:335:- [ ] §4 standalone and provider matrix: all rows PASS.\ndocs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md:47:| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\ndocs/v1.1.4/PLAN.md:89:- **Per-turn matching:** score the operator's prompt against each trusted skill's name and description with a local lexical ranker (no model call, no new dependency). When the top scores clear a threshold, add the top 3 to 5 with full descriptions as a system note placed immediately after the newest operator message, never in `messages[0]` and never at a fixed early index. The note is appended to the conversation once, when that operator message arrives, and stays where it was written; it is not re-inserted per request. That keeps every request an append-only extension of the previous one, so the local prefix cache survives each new prompt. This placement differs from the pinned objective, which `requestMessages` (`cli/src/agent.ts`, around line 2711) inserts at index 1 of each request whenever the ledger must be pinned: copying that pattern would put a per-prompt note at the start of the conversation and invalidate the cache on every operator message. The note is bounded by its own share of the window.\ndocs/reviews/evidence/nexus_maturity_2026-10-11_verification/inference_receipts_session_window.jsonl:2:{\"at\":\"2026-10-11T16:24:06.849Z\",\"requestId\":\"054e8e92-60bf-45d7-9a79-7348a153ede1\",\"role\":\"main\",\"providerId\":\"chatgpt\",\"selectedModel\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"effectiveModel\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"endpointOrigin\":\"https://chatgpt.com\",\"protocol\":\"openai-responses\",\"disclosure\":\"cloud\",\"providerRequestId\":\"resp_02f612b542cbc835016acbb825f04081a0b62e98b0ebfb4b45\",\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":10745,\"outputTokens\":20,\"cachedInputTokens\":0,\"reasoningTokens\":0},\"costConfidence\":\"unknown\",\"outcome\":\"completed\",\"usageConfidence\":\"provider\"}\ndocs/reviews/evidence/nexus_maturity_2026-10-11_verification/inference_receipts_session_window.jsonl:3:{\"at\":\"2026-10-11T16:24:09.164Z\",\"requestId\":\"63079814-0ff9-4b64-abcd-2400adf42026\",\"role\":\"main\",\"providerId\":\"chatgpt\",\"selectedModel\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"effectiveModel\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"endpointOrigin\":\"https://chatgpt.com\",\"protocol\":\"openai-responses\",\"disclosure\":\"cloud\",\"providerRequestId\":\"resp_0614c7ea52ea880d016acbb827cf4887d2afbaa8e7bb935c58\",\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":11664,\"outputTokens\":23,\"cachedInputTokens\":0,\"reasoningTokens\":0},\"costConfidence\":\"unknown\",\"outcome\":\"completed\",\"usageConfidence\":\"provider\"}\ndocs/reviews/evidence/nexus_maturity_2026-10-11_verification/inference_receipts_session_window.jsonl:4:{\"at\":\"2026-10-11T16:24:11.079Z\",\"requestId\":\"8ac802ec-b404-4015-9630-0a7fd14bf7a5\",\"role\":\"main\",\"providerId\":\"chatgpt\",\"selectedModel\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"effectiveModel\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"endpointOrigin\":\"https://chatgpt.com\",\