{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:21:16.568Z","ordinal":29799,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-9f3bf236-d793-44cd-93b5-aaadf75d428f","process_id":"40937","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n \"^- \\[|^##|^###|blocked|decision|defer|pending|TODO|owner\" docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n '^- '\"\\\\[|\"'^##|''^###|blocked|decision|defer|pending|TODO|owner' docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md","query":"^- \\[|^##|^###|blocked|decision|defer|pending|TODO|owner","path":"BACKLOG.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:3:Status: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes, at the owner's request (\"draft the v1.3 platform track as recommended\"). It follows v1.2.1 and v1.2.2 ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md), decision D12). Nothing here is a claim that Nexus Code is frontier-grade, and no unit starts without the decisions in section 7.\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:5:## 1. Purpose and theme\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:14:Non-goals: a marketplace, a desktop or mobile app, a Nexus-specific VS Code extension (ACP clients already exist), hosted or multi-tenant operation (dropped 2026-11-15, decision D8), plugin auto-update, any new runtime dependency without a decision.\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:16:## 2. Evidence and its limits\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:24:## 3. Principles\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:30:5. **Explicit trust.** Project-defined hooks, plugins and policy run only after a trust decision keyed by a content hash; a changed file asks again.\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:32:7. **No new runtime dependency** without a decision. ACP is plain JSON-RPC; hash chains and ed25519 signing use `node:crypto`.\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:35:## 4. Workstreams and units\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:39:### PF: protocol and embedding foundation\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:43:| PF-1 | Publish the client protocol: JSON Schema generated from `cli/src/protocol.ts`, a checked-in copy, a TS/Rust parity test replacing hand-sync, capability negotiation in `hello` / `initialize` (version range, `experimental` flag), `docs/PROTOCOL.md`; wire-level conformance fixtures (reference: opencode `packages/cli/test/acp/wire-fixture.ts`) | schema diff fails CI when a type changes without a version decision; a recorded session replays through the fixture harness | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:46:| PF-4 | Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar | an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:48:### AC: ACP and IDE access\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:53:| AC-2 | ACP conformance: fixture suite plus runs against two real ACP clients (decision D14), recorded as fixtures; no live model | both clients complete the basic flow; fixtures committed | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:57:### VR: Nexus as the verification layer\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:62:| VR-2 | Expose verification as an MCP tool and an ACP extension so another harness can call Nexus to check its own work (`serve-mcp` today exposes workspace tools, not verification); the caller can read the verdict but cannot change it | an external client's session gets a verdict and a receipt id; tampering with the request cannot raise the verdict | M | P1 (decision D20) |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:64:### BG: background and scheduled runs\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:68:| BG-1 | Detached runner: a user-level job runner that owns durable operations so a run survives its CLI process (decision D15: a documented `systemd --user` unit versus a self-daemonizing mode); foreground mode unchanged | kill the CLI, the run continues and resumes cleanly; the SI-7 kill test passes against the runner | L | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:69:| BG-2 | Schedules: a spec (trigger `cron`, `every` or `once`; objective ledger id; `max_rounds`; timeout; policy `read-only` or `worktree-write`; optional deterministic wake `check` command with hard limits, reference opencode `schedule/check.ts:23-39`: 30 s, 20 tool calls, 32 KB, cannot create schedules or worktrees) stored beside operations (`next_due_at`, `remaining_runs`, `last_outcome` as in opencode `core/src/schedule/sql.ts`); `nexus schedule add\\|list\\|rm\\|run-now`; each run in a fresh worktree; unattended flag in every receipt; no allow-always; delivery is a receipt plus a pointer file | a scheduled run on a seeded failing verifier ends BLOCKED and says so; an unattended run asking for approval is denied, not blocked forever; missed runs are reported, not silently skipped | L | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:70:| BG-3 | Event triggers (file change, webhook) | deferred: needs a trust and abuse model first | M | P2 (v1.4) |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:72:### GV: governance\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:76:| GV-1 | Managed policy with fixed precedence **system > user > project**: `/etc/nexus/policy.toml` (path per decision D16), `~/.config/nexus/policy.toml`, then the existing `.nexus-security.json`; restrict-only semantics (deny, require a human, disable auto/yolo, allowlist MCP servers and plugins, set verification-path modes); the effective tier is printed beside every decision; a parse error in the system or user tier **fails closed**; a project policy file is hashed and a changed one asks again (reference: Gemini CLI tiers, MATCH/MISMATCH/NEW integrity check and system settings paths; opencode managed policy; Codex `requirements.toml`) | a project policy cannot loosen a system policy; a corrupted system policy refuses to start with a clear message instead of failing open; precedence table covered by tests | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:77:| GV-2 | Audit export: `nexus audit export <task\\|session>` writes a bundle (session export, receipts, evidence, verification attempts, flight log excerpt) with a manifest of SHA-256 hashes and a hash chain, optionally signed with an operator-provided ed25519 key (`node:crypto`, decision D17); `nexus audit verify` | a bundle with one altered byte fails verification and names the file; an unsigned bundle verifies by hash only and says so | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:82:### HK: hooks and plugins\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:86:| HK-1 | Declarative command hooks: events `pre-tool`, `post-tool`, `pre-verify`, `post-verify`, `permission-request`, `session-start`, `session-end`, `stop`, `pre-compact`; JSON on stdin (`session_id`, `cwd`, `hook_event_name`, `tool_name`, `tool_input`) and on stdout (`decision`, `reason`, `hookSpecificOutput`), exit 2 blocks with stderr as the reason, a timeout, a scrubbed environment (reuse `buildMcpEnvironment`), results written to the receipt. A hook may only deny or tighten arguments, never grant (Gemini CLI also ignores `allow` from lower tiers). Project-defined hooks need a trust decision keyed by a hash of the command (reference: Gemini CLI `trusted_hooks.json`, goose `hooks/mod.rs`, Codex hook trust). Handler types: command only (no peer shipped http, prompt or subagent handlers in what was read) | a hook that returns `allow` for a denied action has no effect; a changed project hook asks again; the contract is documented so existing hook scripts of that shape transfer | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:88:| HK-3 | Per-plugin trust instead of per-directory, and run workspace-defined plugin hooks as subprocesses rather than in-process (a trusted workspace today runs every `.js` file in it with full process authority) | decision D18; migration note for existing plugins | M | P2 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:90:### PA: parallel agents and goals\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:94:| PA-1 | Parallel run visibility: `nexus operation watch` (CLI view first) showing units, worktrees and owners; a worktree owner id recorded in the receipt (reference: Codex `worktree` crate `owner` and `bind_thread`); a concurrency cap tied to a CPU and memory budget instead of a count only | two parallel units show their owners and states; the cap refuses a third when the budget is exhausted | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:98:## 5. Sequencing\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:107:## 6. Release gates\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:117:| G-7 | Notes keep the framing: \"ledger closed, qualification pending\" unless an external result (E2 per the v1.2.1 plan §7) exists |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:119:## 7. Decisions needed\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:133:## 8. Risks\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:3:Status: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes. Evidence and the full gap register are in [`GAP_ANALYSIS.md`](GAP_ANALYSIS.md); gap ids (GA-nn) refer to it. Owner decisions are recorded in section 6 (answered 2026-11-15) and nothing marked \"decision\" starts without one.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:5:## 1. Purpose and theme\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:15:Non-goals: new verification features beyond the deferred SI-5/DV-3/DV-4 set, new providers, a hosted product, any claim of frontier-grade.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:17:## 2. Rules for this release\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:19:- Every unit is test-first, one PR, with local Code / Security / Slop reviews on the exact head before merge (prompts: `memory/entire-agent-review-prompts.md`), required CI green at the exact head, and honest framing in notes: \"ledger closed, qualification pending\" until an external result exists.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:20:- No new runtime dependency without an owner decision. No change to tags (immutable). Dependabot PRs roll in.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:21:- A behaviour change that can break an existing user (containment default, concurrency validation, shell-write hard stop) is a decision (section 6), a release-notes line, and an opt-out.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:25:## 3. Release gates\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:32:| G-2 | Every P0 unit merged; every P1 unit merged or carries a dated owner deferral |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:40:## 4. Workstreams and units\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:44:### MQ: measure and qualify\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:51:| MQ-3 | Real-project smoke: a nightly job that runs a scripted task on pinned copies of representative repositories (a JS project with `node_modules`, a Makefile project, a Python project, a Rust project, and the owner's own repositories where licensing allows) and fails when `task_completed` errors, hangs or ends UNVERIFIED for an avoidable reason. This is the test that would have caught the v1.1.4 every-completion error before release | job red on a seeded regression (a repo whose verifier cannot be bound must complete as REGRESSION_FREE). **Done 2026-11-17** (branch `v1/mq3`): `scripts/real_project_smoke.mjs`, `cli/test/real_project_smoke.test.ts`, nightly `.github/workflows/real-project-smoke.yml` (not required), `docs/REAL_PROJECT_SMOKE.md`. Covers a JS project with `node_modules`, a Makefile project with 140 referenced files, a 24-deep tree and a pytest project; red when a completion errors, hangs or is not REGRESSION_FREE/VERIFIED; mutation-checked against a reintroduced unbindable-verifier block. **Does not cover:** a live model (the gateway is a scripted mock), real third-party or owner repositories (all synthetic), a Rust project, sandbox, worktree or resume modes | M | P0 **First hosted run 2026-11-18 (schedule, main 3c54e601): 3 of 4 repositories passed, the pytest one failed on a setup defect (pytest installed with `pip install --user` was invisible to the isolated HOME); fixed, rerun to be recorded.** **Validated on a hosted runner 2026-11-18 (manual run 44314195365 on a7c07e74: all four repositories REGRESSION_FREE) after the pytest setup fix.** |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:53:| MQ-5 | CERI baseline identity bound to evidence (audit F02): baseline manifest, model digest, sampling and configuration recorded and verified when a comparison is made; a comparison without bound baselines is labelled non-certifying (decision D7, included) | a comparison against an edited baseline fails; test with a swapped manifest | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:55:| MQ-7 | Audit Slices B–G, decide each as a unit or a dated deferral, then build in this order: B evidence lifecycle, C verified baselines and paired comparisons (needed for MQ-2 to be called certifying), E request-context manifests, D installed-evaluator adapter, F recovery reference checker, G bounded campaigns (D7). B and C are P1; D, E, F, G are P2 and may roll | each slice either merged with tests or carries a dated deferral in this file. **2026-11-17:** slices B and C built (branch `v1/mq7bc`; builds on MQ-5/MQ-6): B adds resolved (realpath) containment of evidence paths, validated run ids (reserved names, length, case-only collisions) and the written same-user limitation in `docs/CERI_EVIDENCE.md`; C adds a fixture identity, `mismatchedFields` naming every differing identity field, `loadVerifiedBaseline`/`compareToVerifiedBaseline`, and removes the caller flag as a route to CONFIRMED_REGRESSION (a verified earlier run is required). **Deferred 2026-11-17 (D7):** slices D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker) and G (bounded campaigns) roll to the next release (v1.2.2) per D7; not started | L | P1/P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:60:### VC: verification completeness (the v1.2.0 deferrals)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:66:| VC-3 | **Done 2026-11-18.** Lineage (parent attempt, transition hash of the two candidate identities: not a content diff), the authorisation gate for a named reference (`/requirements reference`) and the behavioural differential (`/requirements differential <R> --inputs <file>`) are built. The reference is an operator-declared command; the candidate is the objective's latest verifier command; each declared input (JSON array of strings, at most 50, each at most 64 KiB) goes to both on stdin through the sandbox with no network, 30 s per run, output capped at 1 MiB; outputs are normalised with `normalizeOutcomeText` and compared with the exit code. A timeout, error or over-cap run is inconclusive for that input, never agreement. The result is finite agreement on the tested inputs (`agreementStatement`), never equivalence; there is no model-facing path. DV-4 attempt lineage (parent attempt, diff hash) and behavioural differential against a declared reference only when an authorised requirement names one (the Anthropic 2026 trends report's flagship long run, Rakuten's seven-hour single run at 99.9% numerical accuracy against a reference method, succeeded because a reference existed: the reference is what lets a long run stay autonomous. Raising this to P1 if MQ-1 shows reference-checkable tasks are common is an owner decision, not made here) | baseline-defect correction not blocked when an authoritative requirement contradicts the baseline; finite-case agreement never reported as equivalence | M | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:67:| VC-4 | **Done 2026-11-17:** the only pending scenario, `cumulative-change-03`, is active (pending count 0); `compaction-03` became active with CX-5. Close the two pending CERI scenarios once VC-1 lands; assign an owner to every scenario in `semantic_integrity` | pending count 0 or each pending carries a unit id | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:72:| VC-9 | **Partly done 2026-11-18** (audit: `docs/v1.2.1/VC-9-needs-input-audit.md`; glue: `cli/src/core/needs_input.ts`, `--answer`). Done: tool approval (write, exec, egress), verification-file edit, verifier and type-check approval end BLOCKED / NEEDS_INPUT with a question and resume with `--answer`. **Not done** (design decisions, see the audit): a requirement marked blocked (changes the REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE contract; free-text answer), an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar-only; resolve-after-the-fact), a subagent's question. Headless needs-input audit (Anthropic 2026 trends report, trend 4: agents that learn when to ask for help): list every path where a headless run needs an operator decision (steering that negates a requirement, a requirement marked blocked, a denied approval, a verification-file edit) and give each the same resumable outcome, a stable `needs_input` reason carrying the question and resumable with the answer; build only the missing glue | each path ends BLOCKED with a machine-readable question and resumes with an answer; no path guesses | S | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:73:| VC-10 | Steering semantic judge (optional; evidence in `docs/research/steering-judgment/`): a judge provider interface (none by default, the configured gateway, or an explicit opt-in third party) asked, for each live requirement, whether the steering message withdraws, defers or changes it; conflict (the existing operator decision) if the regex hits or `withdraws` >= 0.7 whatever `defers` says, advisory note between 0.3 and 0.7, `defers` and `changes` informational only; an unavailable judge records `semanticJudge: unavailable` and keeps the regex decision; the steer stays held from the model until the operator decides and no text goes to a third party unless opted in | **gated**: build only after a larger evaluation (at least 100 judgments from real steering messages, with the owner's consent, labelled blind, on a fresh held-out split) shows union recall of at least 0.8 with at most 5% false positives on the non-withdrawals, and after the synchronous path can hold a steer while a judge answers (the pilot gateway judge took a median of about 17 s per message) | M | P2 (v1.2.2, gated) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:75:### CS: containment and security\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:79:| CS-1 | Containment by default: use the existing bubblewrap path by default when available (fall back to review mode with a visible notice when not); keep `--sandbox none` | decision D1; default flips only with an opt-out and a release-notes line; doctor reports the effective containment | M | P1 **Done 2026-11-17 (D1: default stays opt-in):** doctor reports the effective containment and how to enable it (`cli/src/doctor.ts`). |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:80:| CS-2 | Hard stop for shell writes to verification files: after a `run_command`, compare the verification-file set against the pre-command snapshot and refuse completion (or revert, per decision D2) when it changed | a `sed -i Makefile` run ends BLOCKED with the diff; ordinary commands unaffected | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:81:| CS-3 | Repeated-call detector: identical tool name and arguments N times in a window ends or escalates (Cline 3/5, Gemini CLI 5/10 as reference), independent of timing | the existing \"timing-only\" limitation removed; scenario in CERI | S | P1 **Done 2026-11-17:** detector merged earlier (`cli/src/core/repeated_calls.ts`, `cli/test/repeated_calls.test.ts`); the CERI scenario `repeated-call-01` is active (26 active, 0 pending): five identical `read_file` calls with other reads interleaved end the real agent turn BLOCKED `TOOL_CALL_LOOP`, a nine-file varied sequence is never stopped. Mutation check: with the hard stop disabled in `core/run_turn.ts` the scenario fails (turn COMPLETED, no `repeatedCall`). The \"timing-only\" wording refers to the key auto-repeat check in the v1.1.4 ledger, a different mechanism, and is unchanged. |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:85:| CS-7 | Prompt-injection scanner (decision D5, included): deterministic checks, with no new runtime dependency, on untrusted content entering the model (tool results, fetched pages, MCP responses, file contents, diagnostics): instruction-like imperatives aimed at the agent, hidden or bidirectional Unicode, fake system or tool-call markup, encoded payloads. A hit labels the block as suspected injection, fences it, and raises an approval for any action that follows it in the same step; it never silently deletes content. Reference: Goose `security/scanner.rs` (ML-based; ours is deterministic and its limits are stated) | a labelled corpus (public injection samples plus benign code and docs) with a measured detection rate and false-positive rate reported in the PR; request-shape snapshot shows the fence; the notes state it is a heuristic, not a guarantee | M | P1 **Done 2026-11-17** (`cli/src/core/injection_scanner.ts`, gate in `agent.ts`; corpus `cli/test/fixtures/injection_corpus.json`; measured: tuned set 54/64 detected, 5/45 false positives; held-out 15/30 detected, 2/24 false positives; evasion probes 0/12; operator approval applies only while an operator is present). |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:86:| CS-8 | Egress inspector (D5): inspect outbound requests and tool arguments to external destinations for secrets and workspace-private data using the existing redaction table (MT-3) and `core/egress.ts` classification; ask or deny per policy, and record the decision. Reference: Goose `security/egress_inspector.rs` | test: a tool call that sends a `.env` value to an external host is refused; internal and loopback hosts unaffected | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:91:### WM: weak-model robustness\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:100:### CX: context\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:108:| CX-5 | Post-compaction invariant: after every compaction, every gating requirement of the pinned ledger (`seedRequirements`) is present in the request, or the turn blocks with a named reason (owns CERI `compaction-03`). Covers each rung of the `admitContext` ladder (recent turns 2, 1, 0 against detail budgets 6000 down to 0) and `compact --llm`; the requirement rows are never omitted or truncated, so a ledger too big for the window blocks the turn (`CONTEXT_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED` naming the requirements), which the code already did; only the verbatim objective text has the whole-or-named deferral (`compaction-02`) | a test walks every rung and asserts the ledger survives; a forced zero-budget rung with an over-limit ledger blocks; scenario `compaction-03` active | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:114:### PR: providers (decision D9: every cloud provider configurable)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:135:### RD: reach and distribution\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:139:| RD-1 | macOS arm64 release artifact (then x86_64): build, reproducibility check, install script support, README | decision D6 (runner cost); smoke on a macOS runner; checksum and attestation as for Linux | L | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:142:| RD-4 | GitHub Action wrapper around `nexus run` with a verification-gated exit code | decision D11; example workflow in `examples/` | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:143:| RD-5 | ACP server mode for IDE hosts (references: Goose `crates/goose/src/acp`, Cline `apps/cli/src/acp`) | decision D11; a protocol conformance test | L | P2 (superseded by v1.3 AC-1; see docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:144:| RD-6 | Session fork (reference: Cline `core/src/session/fork-metadata.ts`) | fork test: two divergent continuations, both resumable | M | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL. Done: `SessionManager.forkSession(sourceId, {atMessage?, newId?})` (`cli/src/session.ts`) and `/fork [message-count]` in the TUI catalogue and the REPL. The source is only read (tests compare its record, message log and archive byte for byte after a fork and after the fork continues). The cut backs up to the last complete tool exchange. Compactions whose summary is still in the kept conversation keep their record and archive lines (copied verbatim, so `auditCompactionsOnResume` verifies the fork); a compaction whose summary the cut removed is left out of the fork (otherwise resume would undo it into history the operator did not choose). The fork records `forkedFrom`. Test: two divergent continuations, both resumable and both auditing clean. Decisions: the objective ledger is carried only by a fork of the whole conversation (a truncated fork never reached the source's later state); worktree binding, in-flight operation, provider recovery and last turn are not carried. Update 2026-11-18: the start flag is done: `nexus run --fork-session <task> [--fork-at <n>]` (task store, as `--resume`), `nexus repl --fork-session <id>` and `nexus --fork-session <id>` (opens the REPL, not the TUI: the TUI binary is not given a session to open). Flag errors (unknown source, bad `--fork-at`, `--fork-session` with `--resume`) exit 2. Forking a session bound to a worktree is REFUSED, by design: the binding names one checkout and a fork would share it (either session's merge or discard would pull it from under the other); `/fork` refuses while a worktree is active. Not done: carrying a worktree binding (needs a decision on creating a second checkout), keeping `forkedFrom` after the fork's own turns are saved, and an automated test of the REPL `/fork` worktree refusal (the TUI slash path is tested).\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:147:### PC: process and CI\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:151:| PC-1 | CI wall time: run `linux-x86_64` in parallel with `test-matrix` where its work does not need it, split the CLI suite into shards, cache toolchains; update the stale \"12–20 minutes\" comment (decision D10: required check names and runner minutes) | median PR CI under 40 minutes with the same required-check semantics; measured over 20 runs | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:156:| PC-6 | Dependency hygiene: `commander` major bump if safe, review the two Dependabot PRs, keep the no-deprecated gate **Done 2026-11-18 (no code change):** `cli/package.json` and the lockfile are on commander 14.0.3 (PR #557). Commander 15.1.0 needs Node 22.12.0 and the supported floor is the major only (22), so it stays deferred until the floor moves; PR #530 (commander 15) was closed on 2026-11-16 with `@dependabot ignore this major version`. `gh pr list --state open --author app/dependabot` returns no open PRs, so there are none to merge or close (the rest of the Dependabot history is merged or closed). `check_deprecated_lock.mjs` passes on all five lockfiles. | gate green | S | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:159:### MT: maintainability\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:165:| MT-3 | **Done 2026-11-18** (decision table: `docs/v1.2.1/REDACTION_VARIANTS.md`; shared `redactPlain`, one `safeTerminal` use, one `SANITIZATION_FAILED`; variants that differ on purpose kept and written down; no test edits). One redaction table and one terminal-safe helper: collapse the 17 `redact*` and 4 terminal-safe variants; generate the Rust `redact.rs` patterns from the same table or test parity between them | a parity test fails when one side adds a pattern | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:167:### DH: docs and hygiene\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:174:| DH-4 | Issues reviewed 2026-11-15 (D8): #2, #5, #7 and #49 closed as superseded or completed with comments naming where each requirement went (MQ-1/MQ-2, PC-4, DH-6, merged PR #33); #1 (held-out corpus) and #6 (independent continuity acceptance) kept open and labelled v1.2.1. **Done 2026-11-17:** `docs/v1.1.0/STATUS.md` marked historical, the `FINDINGS.md` header corrected, the stale `v1.0.0` label removed from #1; the deferral review is below | tracker has no stale v1.0.0 issue; the review table is in this file | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:175:| DH-5 | Finish the ledger: read `docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/` and `docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md` and add anything not yet in `GAP_ANALYSIS.md`. **Done 2026-11-16:** both read in full; GA-44 to GA-56 are open or unverified rows, GA-57 to GA-59 record what current `main` already fixes. Unit candidates for the owner, not applied: a small S unit for GA-44 (`patch_file` hunk placement, stale-content guard) and GA-45 (search truncation disclosure), GA-46 and GA-47 (milestone and in-flight-operation state), GA-49 (verify MCP tool execution), and GA-55 (tag signature verification); GA-54 needs an owner decision | gap register updated with sources | S | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:176:| DH-6 | Decide whether to switch `scripts/check_requirements.mjs` to enforce mode (the one open fragment of closed issue #7) and, if yes, bring the ledger rows to their accepted statuses **Decided and done 2026-11-18: enforce.** `--enforce` passes on a clean `origin/main` (123 rows, 12 deferred to exact-RC qualification by `ACCEPTANCE.md`, which is the designed outcome, so no ledger row needed changing). The `test-matrix` job in `ci.yml` now runs it in enforce mode right after checkout (enforce requires a clean worktree). The `linux-x86_64` call runs after a build and stays in report mode so build output cannot dirty the tree. Exact-RC enforcement with a qualification record stays a release step. | decision recorded; gate behaviour matches it | S | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:178:## 5. Sequencing and release split\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:180:Capacity warning. After the 2026-11-15 decisions the unit list is about three times what v1.2.0 carried, and v1.2.0 took a week of agent work with a 70-minute CI cycle. All of it cannot be one release without cutting corners that this project does not cut. Decision D12 (accepted 2026-11-15): **v1.2.1 = Phase 0 + Phase 1 + Phase 2; v1.2.2 = Phase 3 and the native provider protocols.** Anything not merged with green gates rolls with a dated note in this file; a unit is never half-merged to hit a date.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:186:Phase 2: PR-4, PR-5, PR-12; CS-7, CS-8 (D5); MQ-7 slices B and C; CS-1 and CS-2 (defaults per D1 and D2: CS-1 stays opt-in unless the default is changed by a later decision); CX-2, CX-3, VC-1, RD-3, RD-4, MT-3, WM-3, MQ-4, PC-3, DH-3, DH-4, DH-6, CX-7, CS-11, VC-9, PC-7.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:188:Phase 3 (v1.2.2): native protocols PR-6 (Gemini), PR-7 (Bedrock), PR-8 (Vertex), PR-10; CS-9, CS-10, VC-8, RD-7 (added 2026-11-15 from the old-backlog review); MQ-7 slices D, E, F, G; RD-1 (macOS arm64; D6 default is Linux x86_64 only, so this waits for a decision), MT-1, MT-2, VC-2, VC-3, VC-6, VC-7, CX-4, CX-6, RD-2, RD-5, RD-6, CS-5, CS-6, PC-4, PC-5, PC-6, MQ-8 (needs MQ-1 and MQ-2), VC-10 (gated on a larger evaluation).\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:192:## 6. Owner decisions\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:203:| D6 | Platform priority and CI cost | default accepted: Linux x86_64 only for v1.2.1; RD-1 waits for a later decision |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:205:| D8 | Relevance of old deferrals and issues | **reviewed and applied** (2026-11-15): issues #2, #5, #7, #49 closed with comments, #1 and #6 kept open and labelled v1.2.1; the five recommended drops approved (DISC-005, cgroup containment, split-record schema, hosted/multi-tenant, autonomous tool generation); deferral table below |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:211:### Deferral relevance review (D8)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:226:**Dropped (owner approved 2026-11-15; each reopens only on the stated condition):**\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:247:| macOS, arm64, musl (DEC-12, REQ:262) | RD-1, RD-2 | gated on D6 (Linux x86_64 only for v1.2.1; a later decision) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:256:Every row above now carries a decision (2026-11-15). The two sources left open on 2026-11-15, `docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/` and `docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md`, were read in full on 2026-11-16 (DH-5): the qualification files describe about 60 findings against an October build, most of them already fixed on current `main` (grouped in `GAP_ANALYSIS.md` GA-57 to GA-59, with grep evidence); 13 remain open or unverified (GA-44 to GA-56), mostly P2 and P3 with no unit; `READINESS.md` is a v1.0 status log whose gaps are covered by GA-01, GA-29, DH-6 and the new GA-53 to GA-56. No unit was added or re-prioritised by this review; the candidates are listed in the DH-5 row.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:258:## 7. What \"externally qualified\" means here\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:269:## 8. Risks\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:277:| Self-run numbers overclaim | section 7 definitions; wording reviewed against decision D3 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:278:| Scope creep from the deferred verification set | cut lines in section 5; P2 rolls without ceremony |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:281:## 9. Evidence still missing\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:285:## Release review follow-ups (2026-11-17)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:289:## Release disposition (2026-11-17)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:291:Status of every unit that is not marked done above, decided under the owner's standing delegation (D1 to D12) when the release candidate was cut. A unit that is not here is either done (see its row) or a P2 unit that rolls to the next release without ceremony (section 5). Each deferral names its reason so it can be reopened.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:299:| PC-1 | **Not met; deferred to v1.2.2.** Measured: the required checks of this release's PRs took about 37 to 45 minutes. | Test sharding and toolchain caching were not done; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is in place. |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:303:| CS-9, CS-10, VC-8, VC-10, RD-5, RD-7, MT-2, PC-5 | Already deferred in their rows (v1.2.2 or superseded by v1.3). | |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:3:What the v1.2.1 plan ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md)) did not finish, with the reason and the first step for each. Nothing here is scheduled by date; the owner chooses what ships. Units marked **needs** cannot be finished by writing code alone.\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:5:## Needs a resource or an owner decision\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:9:| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | Containers, a pinned task subset, a model-cost budget (decisions D3, D4) | Pick the subset and budget; build the adapter against a mock gateway first. No external measurement is published for v1.2.1. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:11:| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | A macOS runner and an arm64 or musl runner (decision D6, runner cost) | Decide runner cost; reuse `release.yml` packaging |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:14:## Partly done: what is left\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:18:| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations (`/requirements obligate|minimise|retire|reference`), replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording for file tools and the shell; exact-test result binding in the verifier output | Still open: a shell edit to a test file larger than 64 KiB, or outside the 20000-entry scan, is not recorded; weakening is recorded, never blocked. Runner output can still be fabricated by a workspace-controlled test command, so an output line is evidence of a report, not proof the test body executed. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:20:| VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:24:| RD-6 | `SessionManager.forkSession` and `/fork [n]` (source never mutated, compaction archive verified on resume); `--fork-session`/`--fork-at` on `run`, `repl` and the top level; a worktree-bound session is refused | Carrying a worktree binding into a fork (today it is refused: a fork would share the checkout; needs a decision on making a second checkout); `forkedFrom` is lost once the fork's own turns are saved; the REPL `/fork` worktree refusal has no automated test (the `--fork-session` flag is done: run, repl, top level) |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:27:| MQ-7 | Slices B and C; D–G implemented in PR #627 (pending review and CI) | D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker), G (bounded campaigns) are available for review; see PR #627. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:31:## Landed after this list was first written (2026-11-18)\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:39:## Not started / deferred in their rows\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:43:## Release-note items for the next release\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:49:## Process lessons from v1.2.1\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":35640}},"started_at_ms":1795148476567,"completed_at_ms":1795148476567}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:21:16.701Z","ordinal":29800,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1f72f-f11d-7602-8334-77bdf5d0dc54","call_id":"call_56086a1c9edf437bb06c86270e2114de","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:3:Status: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes, at the owner's request (\"draft the v1.3 platform track as recommended\"). It follows v1.2.1 and v1.2.2 ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md), decision D12). Nothing here is a claim that Nexus Code is frontier-grade, and no unit starts without the decisions in section 7.\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:5:## 1. Purpose and theme\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:14:Non-goals: a marketplace, a desktop or mobile app, a Nexus-specific VS Code extension (ACP clients already exist), hosted or multi-tenant operation (dropped 2026-11-15, decision D8), plugin auto-update, any new runtime dependency without a decision.\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:16:## 2. Evidence and its limits\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:24:## 3. Principles\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:30:5. **Explicit trust.** Project-defined hooks, plugins and policy run only after a trust decision keyed by a content hash; a changed file asks again.\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:32:7. **No new runtime dependency** without a decision. ACP is plain JSON-RPC; hash chains and ed25519 signing use `node:crypto`.\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:35:## 4. Workstreams and units\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:39:### PF: protocol and embedding foundation\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:43:| PF-1 | Publish the client protocol: JSON Schema generated from `cli/src/protocol.ts`, a checked-in copy, a TS/Rust parity test replacing hand-sync, capability negotiation in `hello` / `initialize` (version range, `experimental` flag), `docs/PROTOCOL.md`; wire-level conformance fixtures (reference: opencode `packages/cli/test/acp/wire-fixture.ts`) | schema diff fails CI when a type changes without a version decision; a recorded session replays through the fixture harness | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:46:| PF-4 | Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar | an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:48:### AC: ACP and IDE access\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:53:| AC-2 | ACP conformance: fixture suite plus runs against two real ACP clients (decision D14), recorded as fixtures; no live model | both clients complete the basic flow; fixtures committed | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:57:### VR: Nexus as the verification layer\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:62:| VR-2 | Expose verification as an MCP tool and an ACP extension so another harness can call Nexus to check its own work (`serve-mcp` today exposes workspace tools, not verification); the caller can read the verdict but cannot change it | an external client's session gets a verdict and a receipt id; tampering with the request cannot raise the verdict | M | P1 (decision D20) |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:64:### BG: background and scheduled runs\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:68:| BG-1 | Detached runner: a user-level job runner that owns durable operations so a run survives its CLI process (decision D15: a documented `systemd --user` unit versus a self-daemonizing mode); foreground mode unchanged | kill the CLI, the run continues and resumes cleanly; the SI-7 kill test passes against the runner | L | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:69:| BG-2 | Schedules: a spec (trigger `cron`, `every` or `once`; objective ledger id; `max_rounds`; timeout; policy `read-only` or `worktree-write`; optional deterministic wake `check` command with hard limits, reference opencode `schedule/check.ts:23-39`: 30 s, 20 tool calls, 32 KB, cannot create schedules or worktrees) stored beside operations (`next_due_at`, `remaining_runs`, `last_outcome` as in opencode `core/src/schedule/sql.ts`); `nexus schedule add\\|list\\|rm\\|run-now`; each run in a fresh worktree; unattended flag in every receipt; no allow-always; delivery is a receipt plus a pointer file | a scheduled run on a seeded failing verifier ends BLOCKED and says so; an unattended run asking for approval is denied, not blocked forever; missed runs are reported, not silently skipped | L | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:70:| BG-3 | Event triggers (file change, webhook) | deferred: needs a trust and abuse model first | M | P2 (v1.4) |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:72:### GV: governance\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:76:| GV-1 | Managed policy with fixed precedence **system > user > project**: `/etc/nexus/policy.toml` (path per decision D16), `~/.config/nexus/policy.toml`, then the existing `.nexus-security.json`; restrict-only semantics (deny, require a human, disable auto/yolo, allowlist MCP servers and plugins, set verification-path modes); the effective tier is printed beside every decision; a parse error in the system or user tier **fails closed**; a project policy file is hashed and a changed one asks again (reference: Gemini CLI tiers, MATCH/MISMATCH/NEW integrity check and system settings paths; opencode managed policy; Codex `requirements.toml`) | a project policy cannot loosen a system policy; a corrupted system policy refuses to start with a clear message instead of failing open; precedence table covered by tests | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:77:| GV-2 | Audit export: `nexus audit export <task\\|session>` writes a bundle (session export, receipts, evidence, verification attempts, flight log excerpt) with a manifest of SHA-256 hashes and a hash chain, optionally signed with an operator-provided ed25519 key (`node:crypto`, decision D17); `nexus audit verify` | a bundle with one altered byte fails verification and names the file; an unsigned bundle verifies by hash only and says so | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:82:### HK: hooks and plugins\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:86:| HK-1 | Declarative command hooks: events `pre-tool`, `post-tool`, `pre-verify`, `post-verify`, `permission-request`, `session-start`, `session-end`, `stop`, `pre-compact`; JSON on stdin (`session_id`, `cwd`, `hook_event_name`, `tool_name`, `tool_input`) and on stdout (`decision`, `reason`, `hookSpecificOutput`), exit 2 blocks with stderr as the reason, a timeout, a scrubbed environment (reuse `buildMcpEnvironment`), results written to the receipt. A hook may only deny or tighten arguments, never grant (Gemini CLI also ignores `allow` from lower tiers). Project-defined hooks need a trust decision keyed by a hash of the command (reference: Gemini CLI `trusted_hooks.json`, goose `hooks/mod.rs`, Codex hook trust). Handler types: command only (no peer shipped http, prompt or subagent handlers in what was read) | a hook that returns `allow` for a denied action has no effect; a changed project hook asks again; the contract is documented so existing hook scripts of that shape transfer | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:88:| HK-3 | Per-plugin trust instead of per-directory, and run workspace-defined plugin hooks as subprocesses rather than in-process (a trusted workspace today runs every `.js` file in it with full process authority) | decision D18; migration note for existing plugins | M | P2 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:90:### PA: parallel agents and goals\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:94:| PA-1 | Parallel run visibility: `nexus operation watch` (CLI view first) showing units, worktrees and owners; a worktree owner id recorded in the receipt (reference: Codex `worktree` crate `owner` and `bind_thread`); a concurrency cap tied to a CPU and memory budget instead of a count only | two parallel units show their owners and states; the cap refuses a third when the budget is exhausted | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:98:## 5. Sequencing\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:107:## 6. Release gates\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:117:| G-7 | Notes keep the framing: \"ledger closed, qualification pending\" unless an external result (E2 per the v1.2.1 plan §7) exists |\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:119:## 7. Decisions needed\ndocs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:133:## 8. Risks\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:3:Status: PLAN, written 2026-11-15 against `main` plus the v1.2.0-rc.1 notes. Evidence and the full gap register are in [`GAP_ANALYSIS.md`](GAP_ANALYSIS.md); gap ids (GA-nn) refer to it. Owner decisions are recorded in section 6 (answered 2026-11-15) and nothing marked \"decision\" starts without one.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:5:## 1. Purpose and theme\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:15:Non-goals: new verification features beyond the deferred SI-5/DV-3/DV-4 set, new providers, a hosted product, any claim of frontier-grade.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:17:## 2. Rules for this release\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:19:- Every unit is test-first, one PR, with local Code / Security / Slop reviews on the exact head before merge (prompts: `memory/entire-agent-review-prompts.md`), required CI green at the exact head, and honest framing in notes: \"ledger closed, qualification pending\" until an external result exists.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:20:- No new runtime dependency without an owner decision. No change to tags (immutable). Dependabot PRs roll in.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:21:- A behaviour change that can break an existing user (containment default, concurrency validation, shell-write hard stop) is a decision (section 6), a release-notes line, and an opt-out.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:25:## 3. Release gates\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:32:| G-2 | Every P0 unit merged; every P1 unit merged or carries a dated owner deferral |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:40:## 4. Workstreams and units\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:44:### MQ: measure and qualify\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:51:| MQ-3 | Real-project smoke: a nightly job that runs a scripted task on pinned copies of representative repositories (a JS project with `node_modules`, a Makefile project, a Python project, a Rust project, and the owner's own repositories where licensing allows) and fails when `task_completed` errors, hangs or ends UNVERIFIED for an avoidable reason. This is the test that would have caught the v1.1.4 every-completion error before release | job red on a seeded regression (a repo whose verifier cannot be bound must complete as REGRESSION_FREE). **Done 2026-11-17** (branch `v1/mq3`): `scripts/real_project_smoke.mjs`, `cli/test/real_project_smoke.test.ts`, nightly `.github/workflows/real-project-smoke.yml` (not required), `docs/REAL_PROJECT_SMOKE.md`. Covers a JS project with `node_modules`, a Makefile project with 140 referenced files, a 24-deep tree and a pytest project; red when a completion errors, hangs or is not REGRESSION_FREE/VERIFIED; mutation-checked against a reintroduced unbindable-verifier block. **Does not cover:** a live model (the gateway is a scripted mock), real third-party or owner repositories (all synthetic), a Rust project, sandbox, worktree or resume modes | M | P0 **First hosted run 2026-11-18 (schedule, main 3c54e601): 3 of 4 repositories passed, the pytest one failed on a setup defect (pytest installed with `pip install --user` was invisible to the isolated HOME); fixed, rerun to be recorded.** **Validated on a hosted runner 2026-11-18 (manual run 44314195365 on a7c07e74: all four repositories REGRESSION_FREE) after the pytest setup fix.** |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:53:| MQ-5 | CERI baseline identity bound to evidence (audit F02): baseline manifest, model digest, sampling and configuration recorded and verified when a comparison is made; a comparison without bound baselines is labelled non-certifying (decision D7, included) | a comparison against an edited baseline fails; test with a swapped manifest | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:55:| MQ-7 | Audit Slices B–G, decide each as a unit or a dated deferral, then build in this order: B evidence lifecycle, C verified baselines and paired comparisons (needed for MQ-2 to be called certifying), E request-context manifests, D installed-evaluator adapter, F recovery reference checker, G bounded campaigns (D7). B and C are P1; D, E, F, G are P2 and may roll | each slice either merged with tests or carries a dated deferral in this file. **2026-11-17:** slices B and C built (branch `v1/mq7bc`; builds on MQ-5/MQ-6): B adds resolved (realpath) containment of evidence paths, validated run ids (reserved names, length, case-only collisions) and the written same-user limitation in `docs/CERI_EVIDENCE.md`; C adds a fixture identity, `mismatchedFields` naming every differing identity field, `loadVerifiedBaseline`/`compareToVerifiedBaseline`, and removes the caller flag as a route to CONFIRMED_REGRESSION (a verified earlier run is required). **Deferred 2026-11-17 (D7):** slices D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker) and G (bounded campaigns) roll to the next release (v1.2.2) per D7; not started | L | P1/P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:60:### VC: verification completeness (the v1.2.0 deferrals)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:66:| VC-3 | **Done 2026-11-18.** Lineage (parent attempt, transition hash of the two candidate identities: not a content diff), the authorisation gate for a named reference (`/requirements reference`) and the behavioural differential (`/requirements differential <R> --inputs <file>`) are built. The reference is an operator-declared command; the candidate is the objective's latest verifier command; each declared input (JSON array of strings, at most 50, each at most 64 KiB) goes to both on stdin through the sandbox with no network, 30 s per run, output capped at 1 MiB; outputs are normalised with `normalizeOutcomeText` and compared with the exit code. A timeout, error or over-cap run is inconclusive for that input, never agreement. The result is finite agreement on the tested inputs (`agreementStatement`), never equivalence; there is no model-facing path. DV-4 attempt lineage (parent attempt, diff hash) and behavioural differential against a declared reference only when an authorised requirement names one (the Anthropic 2026 trends report's flagship long run, Rakuten's seven-hour single run at 99.9% numerical accuracy against a reference method, succeeded because a reference existed: the reference is what lets a long run stay autonomous. Raising this to P1 if MQ-1 shows reference-checkable tasks are common is an owner decision, not made here) | baseline-defect correction not blocked when an authoritative requirement contradicts the baseline; finite-case agreement never reported as equivalence | M | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:67:| VC-4 | **Done 2026-11-17:** the only pending scenario, `cumulative-change-03`, is active (pending count 0); `compaction-03` became active with CX-5. Close the two pending CERI scenarios once VC-1 lands; assign an owner to every scenario in `semantic_integrity` | pending count 0 or each pending carries a unit id | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:72:| VC-9 | **Partly done 2026-11-18** (audit: `docs/v1.2.1/VC-9-needs-input-audit.md`; glue: `cli/src/core/needs_input.ts`, `--answer`). Done: tool approval (write, exec, egress), verification-file edit, verifier and type-check approval end BLOCKED / NEEDS_INPUT with a question and resume with `--answer`. **Not done** (design decisions, see the audit): a requirement marked blocked (changes the REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE contract; free-text answer), an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar-only; resolve-after-the-fact), a subagent's question. Headless needs-input audit (Anthropic 2026 trends report, trend 4: agents that learn when to ask for help): list every path where a headless run needs an operator decision (steering that negates a requirement, a requirement marked blocked, a denied approval, a verification-file edit) and give each the same resumable outcome, a stable `needs_input` reason carrying the question and resumable with the answer; build only the missing glue | each path ends BLOCKED with a machine-readable question and resumes with an answer; no path guesses | S | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:73:| VC-10 | Steering semantic judge (optional; evidence in `docs/research/steering-judgment/`): a judge provider interface (none by default, the configured gateway, or an explicit opt-in third party) asked, for each live requirement, whether the steering message withdraws, defers or changes it; conflict (the existing operator decision) if the regex hits or `withdraws` >= 0.7 whatever `defers` says, advisory note between 0.3 and 0.7, `defers` and `changes` informational only; an unavailable judge records `semanticJudge: unavailable` and keeps the regex decision; the steer stays held from the model until the operator decides and no text goes to a third party unless opted in | **gated**: build only after a larger evaluation (at least 100 judgments from real steering messages, with the owner's consent, labelled blind, on a fresh held-out split) shows union recall of at least 0.8 with at most 5% false positives on the non-withdrawals, and after the synchronous path can hold a steer while a judge answers (the pilot gateway judge took a median of about 17 s per message) | M | P2 (v1.2.2, gated) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:75:### CS: containment and security\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:79:| CS-1 | Containment by default: use the existing bubblewrap path by default when available (fall back to review mode with a visible notice when not); keep `--sandbox none` | decision D1; default flips only with an opt-out and a release-notes line; doctor reports the effective containment | M | P1 **Done 2026-11-17 (D1: default stays opt-in):** doctor reports the effective containment and how to enable it (`cli/src/doctor.ts`). |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:80:| CS-2 | Hard stop for shell writes to verification files: after a `run_command`, compare the verification-file set against the pre-command snapshot and refuse completion (or revert, per decision D2) when it changed | a `sed -i Makefile` run ends BLOCKED with the diff; ordinary commands unaffected | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:81:| CS-3 | Repeated-call detector: identical tool name and arguments N times in a window ends or escalates (Cline 3/5, Gemini CLI 5/10 as reference), independent of timing | the existing \"timing-only\" limitation removed; scenario in CERI | S | P1 **Done 2026-11-17:** detector merged earlier (`cli/src/core/repeated_calls.ts`, `cli/test/repeated_calls.test.ts`); the CERI scenario `repeated-call-01` is active (26 active, 0 pending): five identical `read_file` calls with other reads interleaved end the real agent turn BLOCKED `TOOL_CALL_LOOP`, a nine-file varied sequence is never stopped. Mutation check: with the hard stop disabled in `core/run_turn.ts` the scenario fails (turn COMPLETED, no `repeatedCall`). The \"timing-only\" wording refers to the key auto-repeat check in the v1.1.4 ledger, a different mechanism, and is unchanged. |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:85:| CS-7 | Prompt-injection scanner (decision D5, included): deterministic checks, with no new runtime dependency, on untrusted content entering the model (tool results, fetched pages, MCP responses, file contents, diagnostics): instruction-like imperatives aimed at the agent, hidden or bidirectional Unicode, fake system or tool-call markup, encoded payloads. A hit labels the block as suspected injection, fences it, and raises an approval for any action that follows it in the same step; it never silently deletes content. Reference: Goose `security/scanner.rs` (ML-based; ours is deterministic and its limits are stated) | a labelled corpus (public injection samples plus benign code and docs) with a measured detection rate and false-positive rate reported in the PR; request-shape snapshot shows the fence; the notes state it is a heuristic, not a guarantee | M | P1 **Done 2026-11-17** (`cli/src/core/injection_scanner.ts`, gate in `agent.ts`; corpus `cli/test/fixtures/injection_corpus.json`; measured: tuned set 54/64 detected, 5/45 false positives; held-out 15/30 detected, 2/24 false positives; evasion probes 0/12; operator approval applies only while an operator is present). |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:86:| CS-8 | Egress inspector (D5): inspect outbound requests and tool arguments to external destinations for secrets and workspace-private data using the existing redaction table (MT-3) and `core/egress.ts` classification; ask or deny per policy, and record the decision. Reference: Goose `security/egress_inspector.rs` | test: a tool call that sends a `.env` value to an external host is refused; internal and loopback hosts unaffected | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:91:### WM: weak-model robustness\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:100:### CX: context\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:108:| CX-5 | Post-compaction invariant: after every compaction, every gating requirement of the pinned ledger (`seedRequirements`) is present in the request, or the turn blocks with a named reason (owns CERI `compaction-03`). Covers each rung of the `admitContext` ladder (recent turns 2, 1, 0 against detail budgets 6000 down to 0) and `compact --llm`; the requirement rows are never omitted or truncated, so a ledger too big for the window blocks the turn (`CONTEXT_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED` naming the requirements), which the code already did; only the verbatim objective text has the whole-or-named deferral (`compaction-02`) | a test walks every rung and asserts the ledger survives; a forced zero-budget rung with an over-limit ledger blocks; scenario `compaction-03` active | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:114:### PR: providers (decision D9: every cloud provider configurable)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:135:### RD: reach and distribution\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:139:| RD-1 | macOS arm64 release artifact (then x86_64): build, reproducibility check, install script support, README | decision D6 (runner cost); smoke on a macOS runner; checksum and attestation as for Linux | L | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:142:| RD-4 | GitHub Action wrapper around `nexus run` with a verification-gated exit code | decision D11; example workflow in `examples/` | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:143:| RD-5 | ACP server mode for IDE hosts (references: Goose `crates/goose/src/acp`, Cline `apps/cli/src/acp`) | decision D11; a protocol conformance test | L | P2 (superseded by v1.3 AC-1; see docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:144:| RD-6 | Session fork (reference: Cline `core/src/session/fork-metadata.ts`) | fork test: two divergent continuations, both resumable | M | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL. Done: `SessionManager.forkSession(sourceId, {atMessage?, newId?})` (`cli/src/session.ts`) and `/fork [message-count]` in the TUI catalogue and the REPL. The source is only read (tests compare its record, message log and archive byte for byte after a fork and after the fork continues). The cut backs up to the last complete tool exchange. Compactions whose summary is still in the kept conversation keep their record and archive lines (copied verbatim, so `auditCompactionsOnResume` verifies the fork); a compaction whose summary the cut removed is left out of the fork (otherwise resume would undo it into history the operator did not choose). The fork records `forkedFrom`. Test: two divergent continuations, both resumable and both auditing clean. Decisions: the objective ledger is carried only by a fork of the whole conversation (a truncated fork never reached the source's later state); worktree binding, in-flight operation, provider recovery and last turn are not carried. Update 2026-11-18: the start flag is done: `nexus run --fork-session <task> [--fork-at <n>]` (task store, as `--resume`), `nexus repl --fork-session <id>` and `nexus --fork-session <id>` (opens the REPL, not the TUI: the TUI binary is not given a session to open). Flag errors (unknown source, bad `--fork-at`, `--fork-session` with `--resume`) exit 2. Forking a session bound to a worktree is REFUSED, by design: the binding names one checkout and a fork would share it (either session's merge or discard would pull it from under the other); `/fork` refuses while a worktree is active. Not done: carrying a worktree binding (needs a decision on creating a second checkout), keeping `forkedFrom` after the fork's own turns are saved, and an automated test of the REPL `/fork` worktree refusal (the TUI slash path is tested).\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:147:### PC: process and CI\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:151:| PC-1 | CI wall time: run `linux-x86_64` in parallel with `test-matrix` where its work does not need it, split the CLI suite into shards, cache toolchains; update the stale \"12–20 minutes\" comment (decision D10: required check names and runner minutes) | median PR CI under 40 minutes with the same required-check semantics; measured over 20 runs | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:156:| PC-6 | Dependency hygiene: `commander` major bump if safe, review the two Dependabot PRs, keep the no-deprecated gate **Done 2026-11-18 (no code change):** `cli/package.json` and the lockfile are on commander 14.0.3 (PR #557). Commander 15.1.0 needs Node 22.12.0 and the supported floor is the major only (22), so it stays deferred until the floor moves; PR #530 (commander 15) was closed on 2026-11-16 with `@dependabot ignore this major version`. `gh pr list --state open --author app/dependabot` returns no open PRs, so there are none to merge or close (the rest of the Dependabot history is merged or closed). `check_deprecated_lock.mjs` passes on all five lockfiles. | gate green | S | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:159:### MT: maintainability\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:165:| MT-3 | **Done 2026-11-18** (decision table: `docs/v1.2.1/REDACTION_VARIANTS.md`; shared `redactPlain`, one `safeTerminal` use, one `SANITIZATION_FAILED`; variants that differ on purpose kept and written down; no test edits). One redaction table and one terminal-safe helper: collapse the 17 `redact*` and 4 terminal-safe variants; generate the Rust `redact.rs` patterns from the same table or test parity between them | a parity test fails when one side adds a pattern | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:167:### DH: docs and hygiene\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:174:| DH-4 | Issues reviewed 2026-11-15 (D8): #2, #5, #7 and #49 closed as superseded or completed with comments naming where each requirement went (MQ-1/MQ-2, PC-4, DH-6, merged PR #33); #1 (held-out corpus) and #6 (independent continuity acceptance) kept open and labelled v1.2.1. **Done 2026-11-17:** `docs/v1.1.0/STATUS.md` marked historical, the `FINDINGS.md` header corrected, the stale `v1.0.0` label removed from #1; the deferral review is below | tracker has no stale v1.0.0 issue; the review table is in this file | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:175:| DH-5 | Finish the ledger: read `docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/` and `docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md` and add anything not yet in `GAP_ANALYSIS.md`. **Done 2026-11-16:** both read in full; GA-44 to GA-56 are open or unverified rows, GA-57 to GA-59 record what current `main` already fixes. Unit candidates for the owner, not applied: a small S unit for GA-44 (`patch_file` hunk placement, stale-content guard) and GA-45 (search truncation disclosure), GA-46 and GA-47 (milestone and in-flight-operation state), GA-49 (verify MCP tool execution), and GA-55 (tag signature verification); GA-54 needs an owner decision | gap register updated with sources | S | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:176:| DH-6 | Decide whether to switch `scripts/check_requirements.mjs` to enforce mode (the one open fragment of closed issue #7) and, if yes, bring the ledger rows to their accepted statuses **Decided and done 2026-11-18: enforce.** `--enforce` passes on a clean `origin/main` (123 rows, 12 deferred to exact-RC qualification by `ACCEPTANCE.md`, which is the designed outcome, so no ledger row needed changing). The `test-matrix` job in `ci.yml` now runs it in enforce mode right after checkout (enforce requires a clean worktree). The `linux-x86_64` call runs after a build and stays in report mode so build output cannot dirty the tree. Exact-RC enforcement with a qualification record stays a release step. | decision recorded; gate behaviour matches it | S | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:178:## 5. Sequencing and release split\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:180:Capacity warning. After the 2026-11-15 decisions the unit list is about three times what v1.2.0 carried, and v1.2.0 took a week of agent work with a 70-minute CI cycle. All of it cannot be one release without cutting corners that this project does not cut. Decision D12 (accepted 2026-11-15): **v1.2.1 = Phase 0 + Phase 1 + Phase 2; v1.2.2 = Phase 3 and the native provider protocols.** Anything not merged with green gates rolls with a dated note in this file; a unit is never half-merged to hit a date.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:186:Phase 2: PR-4, PR-5, PR-12; CS-7, CS-8 (D5); MQ-7 slices B and C; CS-1 and CS-2 (defaults per D1 and D2: CS-1 stays opt-in unless the default is changed by a later decision); CX-2, CX-3, VC-1, RD-3, RD-4, MT-3, WM-3, MQ-4, PC-3, DH-3, DH-4, DH-6, CX-7, CS-11, VC-9, PC-7.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:188:Phase 3 (v1.2.2): native protocols PR-6 (Gemini), PR-7 (Bedrock), PR-8 (Vertex), PR-10; CS-9, CS-10, VC-8, RD-7 (added 2026-11-15 from the old-backlog review); MQ-7 slices D, E, F, G; RD-1 (macOS arm64; D6 default is Linux x86_64 only, so this waits for a decision), MT-1, MT-2, VC-2, VC-3, VC-6, VC-7, CX-4, CX-6, RD-2, RD-5, RD-6, CS-5, CS-6, PC-4, PC-5, PC-6, MQ-8 (needs MQ-1 and MQ-2), VC-10 (gated on a larger evaluation).\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:192:## 6. Owner decisions\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:203:| D6 | Platform priority and CI cost | default accepted: Linux x86_64 only for v1.2.1; RD-1 waits for a later decision |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:205:| D8 | Relevance of old deferrals and issues | **reviewed and applied** (2026-11-15): issues #2, #5, #7, #49 closed with comments, #1 and #6 kept open and labelled v1.2.1; the five recommended drops approved (DISC-005, cgroup containment, split-record schema, hosted/multi-tenant, autonomous tool generation); deferral table below |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:211:### Deferral relevance review (D8)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:226:**Dropped (owner approved 2026-11-15; each reopens only on the stated condition):**\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:247:| macOS, arm64, musl (DEC-12, REQ:262) | RD-1, RD-2 | gated on D6 (Linux x86_64 only for v1.2.1; a later decision) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:256:Every row above now carries a decision (2026-11-15). The two sources left open on 2026-11-15, `docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/` and `docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md`, were read in full on 2026-11-16 (DH-5): the qualification files describe about 60 findings against an October build, most of them already fixed on current `main` (grouped in `GAP_ANALYSIS.md` GA-57 to GA-59, with grep evidence); 13 remain open or unverified (GA-44 to GA-56), mostly P2 and P3 with no unit; `READINESS.md` is a v1.0 status log whose gaps are covered by GA-01, GA-29, DH-6 and the new GA-53 to GA-56. No unit was added or re-prioritised by this review; the candidates are listed in the DH-5 row.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:258:## 7. What \"externally qualified\" means here\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:269:## 8. Risks\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:277:| Self-run numbers overclaim | section 7 definitions; wording reviewed against decision D3 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:278:| Scope creep from the deferred verification set | cut lines in section 5; P2 rolls without ceremony |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:281:## 9. Evidence still missing\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:285:## Release review follow-ups (2026-11-17)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:289:## Release disposition (2026-11-17)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:291:Status of every unit that is not marked done above, decided under the owner's standing delegation (D1 to D12) when the release candidate was cut. A unit that is not here is either done (see its row) or a P2 unit that rolls to the next release without ceremony (section 5). Each deferral names its reason so it can be reopened.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:299:| PC-1 | **Not met; deferred to v1.2.2.** Measured: the required checks of this release's PRs took about 37 to 45 minutes. | Test sharding and toolchain caching were not done; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is in place. |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:303:| CS-9, CS-10, VC-8, VC-10, RD-5, RD-7, MT-2, PC-5 | Already deferred in their rows (v1.2.2 or superseded by v1.3). | |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:3:What the v1.2.1 plan ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md)) did not finish, with the reason and the first step for each. Nothing here is scheduled by date; the owner chooses what ships. Units marked **needs** cannot be finished by writing code alone.\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:5:## Needs a resource or an owner decision\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:9:| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | Containers, a pinned task subset, a model-cost budget (decisions D3, D4) | Pick the subset and budget; build the adapter against a mock gateway first. No external measurement is published for v1.2.1. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:11:| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | A macOS runner and an arm64 or musl runner (decision D6, runner cost) | Decide runner cost; reuse `release.yml` packaging |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:14:## Partly done: what is left\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:18:| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations (`/requirements obligate|minimise|retire|reference`), replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording for file tools and the shell; exact-test result binding in the verifier output | Still open: a shell edit to a test file larger than 64 KiB, or outside the 20000-entry scan, is not recorded; weakening is recorded, never blocked. Runner output can still be fabricated by a workspace-controlled test command, so an output line is evidence of a report, not proof the test body executed. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:20:| VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:24:| RD-6 | `SessionManager.forkSession` and `/fork [n]` (source never mutated, compaction archive verified on resume); `--fork-session`/`--fork-at` on `run`, `repl` and the top level; a worktree-bound session is refused | Carrying a worktree binding into a fork (today it is refused: a fork would share the checkout; needs a decision on making a second checkout); `forkedFrom` is lost once the fork's own turns are saved; the REPL `/fork` worktree refusal has no automated test (the `--fork-session` flag is done: run, repl, top level) |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:27:| MQ-7 | Slices B and C; D–G implemented in PR #627 (pending review and CI) | D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker), G (bounded campaigns) are available for review; see PR #627. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:31:## Landed after this list was first written (2026-11-18)\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:39:## Not started / deferred in their rows\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:43:## Release-note items for the next release\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:49:## Process lessons from v1.2.1\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","create_time":1795148476.7013462}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":13150,"sampled_through_sequence":13147,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
