{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:21:28.770Z","ordinal":29817,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_05793ea695c2aa2c016affcac6d7bc87d2b5770ad069872e2e","status":"completed","call_id":"call_489871b67b9e419aa6ee3347bcf21d78","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"git show origin/v1/v1.2x-backlog-reconcile:docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md | sed -n '5,32p'; git status --short --branch; git -C /home/mike/nexus-wt/invariants status --short --branch\",\"workdir\":\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":4000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","create_time":1795148486.8451917}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":13158,"sampled_through_sequence":13156,"client_authored":false}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:21:28.784Z","ordinal":29818,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","session_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","root_turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","response_id":"resp_05793ea695c2aa2c016affcac492bc87d287e64f8e9a87a971","usage":{"input_tokens":52328,"cached_input_tokens":48896,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":212,"reasoning_output_tokens":99,"total_tokens":52540},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":4207124,"cached_input_tokens":4160256,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":10518,"reasoning_output_tokens":4777,"total_tokens":4217642},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":624781194,"cached_input_tokens":617801472,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1339552,"reasoning_output_tokens":706957,"total_tokens":626120746}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:21:29.016Z","ordinal":29819,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-1d8c3593-c002-43bd-8cfc-441b8ff8dec2","process_id":"12601","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git show origin/v1/v1.2x-backlog-reconcile:docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md | sed -n '5,32p'; git status --short --branch; git -C /home/mike/nexus-wt/invariants status --short --branch"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git show origin/v1/v1.2x-backlog-reconcile:docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md | sed -n '5,32p'; git status --short --branch; git -C /home/mike/nexus-wt/invariants status --short --branch"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"## Needs a resource or an owner decision\n\n| Unit | Not done | Needs | First step |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | Containerized benchmark environment, an owner-selected pinned subset, and a concrete inference-spend ceiling. D3/D4 already decide the publication rule (publish nothing before a result) and measurement class (small self-run E1); they do not set the actual subset or spend cap. | Build the adapter against a mock gateway; before live inference, select the subset and approve its spend ceiling. |\n| MQ-4 | Scheduled gateway conformance: the job script, claim protocol and alert path are built and tested (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`) | A local scheduler on the host that can reach the gateway (a timer unit example is documented, nothing is installed) and a first live run with a prepared config | Install the timer (via Ansible if the host is managed that way), keep a `last-good-result.json` baseline, run once by hand |\n| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | A macOS runner and an arm64 or musl runner. D6 currently accepts Linux x86_64 only; expanding supported release targets requires a new owner go/no-go and runner-cost decision. | If expanding platforms, decide which target and runner cost; reuse `release.yml` packaging. |\n| PC-1 | Median PR CI under 40 minutes (measured 37 to 45 minutes) | Time on the runners | Shard the CLI suite and cache toolchains; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is already in place |\n\n## Partly done: what is left\n\n| Unit | Done in v1.2.1 / since | Left |\n|---|---|---|\n| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations (`/requirements obligate|minimise|retire|reference`), replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording for file tools and the shell; exact-test result binding in the verifier output | Still open: a shell edit to a test file larger than 64 KiB, or outside the 20000-entry scan, is not recorded; weakening is recorded, never blocked. Runner output can still be fabricated by a workspace-controlled test command, so an output line is evidence of a report, not proof the test body executed. |\n| VC-6 | **Done for configured project coverage**: every discovered TypeScript, Rust, Go, and configured Python typecheck project is checked; missing configured tools report `UNAVAILABLE`; baseline deltas distinguish introduced from pre-existing diagnostics. Evidence: PR #631 and `cli/test/vc6_typecheck_gaps.test.ts` (discovery, multiple configs, bounded discovery, nested Rust, unavailable tools, shell changes). | Still open: resumed tasks do not retain a trustworthy pre-change typecheck baseline; Cargo is excluded from the baseline run because it is not cheap. These limits must remain visible in the verifier result and completion policy. |\n| VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\n| CX-4 | Opt-in masking (`NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS`) with ids resolved through `read_archive` | A default threshold (not shown safe); a dedicated id-read tool; measurement of masking on real tasks |\n| CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\n| CX-7 | **Done**: trusted workspaces load all non-empty instruction files in precedence order; imports are constrained by real-path containment; `~/` and absolute paths are supported when they resolve inside the trusted workspace. Evidence: `cli/test/project_rules_imports.test.ts` (including untrusted-workspace, cycle, depth-bound, and symlink escape cases). |\n| RD-6 | **Done for the accepted single-checkout fork behavior**: `SessionManager.forkSession` and `/fork [n]` preserve the source and compaction integrity; `--fork-session`/`--fork-at` work on `run`, `repl`, and top-level launch; worktree-bound forks are refused before creating or saving a fork. `forkedFrom` is persisted on later session saves. Evidence: `cli/test/session_fork.test.ts` and `cli/test/session_fork_start.test.ts`, including the REPL `/fork` worktree-refusal test. | Carrying a worktree binding would require creating a second checkout; this remains an owner design decision and is not required for the safe refusal behavior. |\n| CS-6 | Test `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts`, weekly non-required workflow `lsp-real-servers.yml`, `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`; typescript-language-server and pyright validated locally (pass; mutation-checked); fixed the missing `--stdio` for TS/JS/Python | The workflow has not yet run on a hosted runner; rust-analyzer and gopls cases are written but have run nowhere (not installed on the dev host), so treat them as unvalidated; hover, definition, the trust and sandbox gates and multi-file projects are not exercised against real servers; versions of rust-analyzer and gopls are not pinned by the test |\n| MT-3 | One `redactPlain` and one `safeTerminal` use in place of 7 and 3 inline copies (part 1); part 2: a characterization test pins 23 variants over a shared corpus, `formatSafeSidecarText` now calls `safeTerminal`, and every remaining variant has a row with the reason it differs in `docs/v1.2.1/REDACTION_VARIANTS.md` | Nothing planned. The audit found only one more behaviour-preserving collapse; the rest differ in bytes (escape stripping, classifier or no classifier, control handling order, credential-key replacement). Module-private variants (`ceri` `redactedJson`, `safeTerminalText`, `flat`, `redactDeep`) are not in the characterization test |\n| MQ-7 | **Done**: slices B–G are implemented and merged in PR #627. Slices D (installed-evaluator binding), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery-reference integrity), and G (bounded campaigns) have focused coverage in `cli/test/ceri_mq7dg.test.ts`; B/C coverage is in `cli/test/ceri_mq7bc.test.ts`. | No remaining v1.2.2 implementation item identified in this unit. |\n| CS-11 | Deterministic loop-pattern transcripts against the real agent turn and the measured table in `docs/LOOP_PATTERNS.md`; the pattern 2b gap (a command failing with only volatile text changing) closed by context-anchored volatile-text patterns, measured on an 86-pair labelled corpus (same-failure merged 6 of 38 to 34 of 38, different-failure merged 0 of 48 before and after) | Four same-failure pairs with no context word to anchor on (digit-less random suffix, unanchored counter, queue depth, short sha), left unmerged on purpose |\n| WM-3 | All five gateway aliases measured (single runs) | A reasoning-aware pass rule for aliases with reasoning off (the runner marks them `passed: false` for the hybrid-reasoning check); repeats, to see run-to-run variation |\n\n## Landed after this list was first written (2026-11-18)\n\n## main...origin/main\n## v1/invariants-audit...origin/main [ahead 35, behind 171]\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":7846640}},"started_at_ms":1795148489007,"completed_at_ms":1795148489015}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:21:29.171Z","ordinal":29820,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1f730-21d3-7fa3-9296-b13df3dd17f8","call_id":"call_489871b67b9e419aa6ee3347bcf21d78","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.4 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"## Needs a resource or an owner decision\n\n| Unit | Not done | Needs | First step |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | Containerized benchmark environment, an owner-selected pinned subset, and a concrete inference-spend ceiling. D3/D4 already decide the publication rule (publish nothing before a result) and measurement class (small self-run E1); they do not set the actual subset or spend cap. | Build the adapter against a mock gateway; before live inference, select the subset and approve its spend ceiling. |\n| MQ-4 | Scheduled gateway conformance: the job script, claim protocol and alert path are built and tested (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`) | A local scheduler on the host that can reach the gateway (a timer unit example is documented, nothing is installed) and a first live run with a prepared config | Install the timer (via Ansible if the host is managed that way), keep a `last-good-result.json` baseline, run once by hand |\n| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | A macOS runner and an arm64 or musl runner. D6 currently accepts Linux x86_64 only; expanding supported release targets requires a new owner go/no-go and runner-cost decision. | If expanding platforms, decide which target and runner cost; reuse `release.yml` packaging. |\n| PC-1 | Median PR CI under 40 minutes (measured 37 to 45 minutes) | Time on the runners | Shard the CLI suite and cache toolchains; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is already in place |\n\n## Partly done: what is left\n\n| Unit | Done in v1.2.1 / since | Left |\n|---|---|---|\n| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations (`/requirements obligate|minimise|retire|reference`), replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording for file tools and the shell; exact-test result binding in the verifier output | Still open: a shell edit to a test file larger than 64 KiB, or outside the 20000-entry scan, is not recorded; weakening is recorded, never blocked. Runner output can still be fabricated by a workspace-controlled test command, so an output line is evidence of a report, not proof the test body executed. |\n| VC-6 | **Done for configured project coverage**: every discovered TypeScript, Rust, Go, and configured Python typecheck project is checked; missing configured tools report `UNAVAILABLE`; baseline deltas distinguish introduced from pre-existing diagnostics. Evidence: PR #631 and `cli/test/vc6_typecheck_gaps.test.ts` (discovery, multiple configs, bounded discovery, nested Rust, unavailable tools, shell changes). | Still open: resumed tasks do not retain a trustworthy pre-change typecheck baseline; Cargo is excluded from the baseline run because it is not cheap. These limits must remain visible in the verifier result and completion policy. |\n| VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\n| CX-4 | Opt-in masking (`NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS`) with ids resolved through `read_archive` | A default threshold (not shown safe); a dedicated id-read tool; measurement of masking on real tasks |\n| CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\n| CX-7 | **Done**: trusted workspaces load all non-empty instruction files in precedence order; imports are constrained by real-path containment; `~/` and absolute paths are supported when they resolve inside the trusted workspace. Evidence: `cli/test/project_rules_imports.test.ts` (including untrusted-workspace, cycle, depth-bound, and symlink escape cases). |\n| RD-6 | **Done for the accepted single-checkout fork behavior**: `SessionManager.forkSession` and `/fork [n]` preserve the source and compaction integrity; `--fork-session`/`--fork-at` work on `run`, `repl`, and top-level launch; worktree-bound forks are refused before creating or saving a fork. `forkedFrom` is persisted on later session saves. Evidence: `cli/test/session_fork.test.ts` and `cli/test/session_fork_start.test.ts`, including the REPL `/fork` worktree-refusal test. | Carrying a worktree binding would require creating a second checkout; this remains an owner design decision and is not required for the safe refusal behavior. |\n| CS-6 | Test `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts`, weekly non-required workflow `lsp-real-servers.yml`, `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`; typescript-language-server and pyright validated locally (pass; mutation-checked); fixed the missing `--stdio` for TS/JS/Python | The workflow has not yet run on a hosted runner; rust-analyzer and gopls cases are written but have run nowhere (not installed on the dev host), so treat them as unvalidated; hover, definition, the trust and sandbox gates and multi-file projects are not exercised against real servers; versions of rust-analyzer and gopls are not pinned by the test |\n| MT-3 | One `redactPlain` and one `safeTerminal` use in place of 7 and 3 inline copies (part 1); part 2: a characterization test pins 23 variants over a shared corpus, `formatSafeSidecarText` now calls `safeTerminal`, and every remaining variant has a row with the reason it differs in `docs/v1.2.1/REDACTION_VARIANTS.md` | Nothing planned. The audit found only one more behaviour-preserving collapse; the rest differ in bytes (escape stripping, classifier or no classifier, control handling order, credential-key replacement). Module-private variants (`ceri` `redactedJson`, `safeTerminalText`, `flat`, `redactDeep`) are not in the characterization test |\n| MQ-7 | **Done**: slices B–G are implemented and merged in PR #627. Slices D (installed-evaluator binding), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery-reference integrity), and G (bounded campaigns) have focused coverage in `cli/test/ceri_mq7dg.test.ts`; B/C coverage is in `cli/test/ceri_mq7bc.test.ts`. | No remaining v1.2.2 implementation item identified in this unit. |\n| CS-11 | Deterministic loop-pattern transcripts against the real agent turn and the measured table in `docs/LOOP_PATTERNS.md`; the pattern 2b gap (a command failing with only volatile text changing) closed by context-anchored volatile-text patterns, measured on an 86-pair labelled corpus (same-failure merged 6 of 38 to 34 of 38, different-failure merged 0 of 48 before and after) | Four same-failure pairs with no context word to anchor on (digit-less random suffix, unanchored counter, queue depth, short sha), left unmerged on purpose |\n| WM-3 | All five gateway aliases measured (single runs) | A reasoning-aware pass rule for aliases with reasoning off (the runner marks them `passed: false` for the hybrid-reasoning check); repeats, to see run-to-run variation |\n\n## Landed after this list was first written (2026-11-18)\n\n## main...origin/main\n## v1/invariants-audit...origin/main [ahead 35, behind 171]\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","create_time":1795148489.1718347}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":13159,"sampled_through_sequence":13156,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:21:29.175Z","ordinal":29821,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"token_count","info":{"total_token_usage":{"input_tokens":619155932,"cached_input_tokens":612196224,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1263750,"reasoning_output_tokens":706957,"total_tokens":620419682},"last_token_usage":{"input_tokens":52328,"cached_input_tokens":48896,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":212,"reasoning_output_tokens":99,"total_tokens":52540},"last_usage_includes_reasoning":true,"model_context_window":258400},"rate_limits":{"limit_id":"codex","limit_name":null,"primary":{"used_percent":9.0,"window_minutes":300,"resets_at":1795163068},"secondary":{"used_percent":22.0,"window_minutes":10080,"resets_at":1795294616},"credits":{"has_credits":false,"unlimited":false,"balance":"0"},"individual_limit":null,"spend_control_reached":null,"plan_type":"plus","rate_limit_reached_type":null}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:21:39.744Z","ordinal":29822,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affcaca464487d28457cc4c79f06662","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Most remaining items need a choice or an external resource; **none require a second reviewer**. The “add another maintainer” path is not applicable to your single-user repo, and lack of one should not hold up publication.\n\n**The immediate work is publication of three open PRs.** Their required checks are still pending in the latest check I saw; once green, the project’s merge process can proceed. The backlog reconciliation PR also updates which items are already done, so that’s the best current tracker.\n\n### Decisions or resources still needed for v1.2.x\n\n- **MQ-1/MQ-2 benchmark:** choose a pinned task subset and an inference-spend ceiling. The benchmark adapter can be built against a mock gateway before spending on live runs.\n- **MQ-4 scheduled gateway checks:** install a host scheduler that can reach the gateway, prepare its configuration, and run it once.\n- **RD-1/RD-2 additional release platforms:** decide whether to add macOS and/or arm64/musl support and fund/provide the corresponding runners. Linux x86_64 remains the accepted target meanwhile.\n- **PC-1 faster CI:** no owner decision; it needs engineering time for sharding and caching, plus runner measurements.\n- **CX-6 live provider validation:** Anthropic API access is needed for live cache validation; Bedrock access for its cache-point support. These are optional enhancements, not prerequisites to publish the current work.\n- **WM-3 gateway measurements:** gateway access and repeat runs are needed to evaluate the reasoning-off aliases.\n- **RD-6 worktree-bound forks:** no decision is needed to ship the safe existing behavior, which refuses these forks. Creating a separate checkout for each fork would need a later design choice.\n\nItems such as VC-2’s large-file/scope limits, VC-9’s remaining needs-input cases, and parts of CX-4/CX-7 are engineering work or deliberate limits, rather than external resources you must obtain before publishing.\n\n### Claude’s outstanding work\n\nThe Claude candidate is still separate and behind `origin/main`; it should not be merged wholesale. Much of its work has already been incorporated or superseded. The remaining candidate notes need reconciliation against current main, especially the review-contract changes, while avoiding files claimed by the active P10 work. I would treat that as a separate implementation/reconciliation task, not as a reason to delay the open PRs.\n\nI checked the local `main` state: it is clean and matches `origin/main` at `f873787a`."}],"phase":"final_answer"},"content_token_count":521,"started_at_ms":1795148490411,"completed_at_ms":1795148499744}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:21:39.751Z","ordinal":29823,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","status":"completed","id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affcaca464487d28457cc4c79f06662","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Most remaining items need a choice or an external resource; **none require a second reviewer**. The “add another maintainer” path is not applicable to your single-user repo, and lack of one should not hold up publication.\n\n**The immediate work is publication of three open PRs.** Their required checks are still pending in the latest check I saw; once green, the project’s merge process can proceed. The backlog reconciliation PR also updates which items are already done, so that’s the best current tracker.\n\n### Decisions or resources still needed for v1.2.x\n\n- **MQ-1/MQ-2 benchmark:** choose a pinned task subset and an inference-spend ceiling. The benchmark adapter can be built against a mock gateway before spending on live runs.\n- **MQ-4 scheduled gateway checks:** install a host scheduler that can reach the gateway, prepare its configuration, and run it once.\n- **RD-1/RD-2 additional release platforms:** decide whether to add macOS and/or arm64/musl support and fund/provide the corresponding runners. Linux x86_64 remains the accepted target meanwhile.\n- **PC-1 faster CI:** no owner decision; it needs engineering time for sharding and caching, plus runner measurements.\n- **CX-6 live provider validation:** Anthropic API access is needed for live cache validation; Bedrock access for its cache-point support. These are optional enhancements, not prerequisites to publish the current work.\n- **WM-3 gateway measurements:** gateway access and repeat runs are needed to evaluate the reasoning-off aliases.\n- **RD-6 worktree-bound forks:** no decision is needed to ship the safe existing behavior, which refuses these forks. Creating a separate checkout for each fork would need a later design choice.\n\nItems such as VC-2’s large-file/scope limits, VC-9’s remaining needs-input cases, and parts of CX-4/CX-7 are engineering work or deliberate limits, rather than external resources you must obtain before publishing.\n\n### Claude’s outstanding work\n\nThe Claude candidate is still separate and behind `origin/main`; it should not be merged wholesale. Much of its work has already been incorporated or superseded. The remaining candidate notes need reconciliation against current main, especially the review-contract changes, while avoiding files claimed by the active P10 work. I would treat that as a separate implementation/reconciliation task, not as a reason to delay the open PRs.\n\nI checked the local `main` state: it is clean and matches `origin/main` at `f873787a`.","annotations":[],"logprobs":[]}],"phase":"final_answer","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","create_time":1795148490.2781184,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":13160,"sampled_through_sequence":13159,"retained_source":{"id":{"message_id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affcaca464487d28457cc4c79f06662","turn_id":"01a1f72c-56d0-7a60-917e-bc2e070d3543","role":"assistant"},"revision":"retained_01a1f730-4b27-7f92-b64f-5c3cf83486b8","complete":true},"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":760}}
