{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:23:37.318Z","ordinal":29942,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-f4d4bc21-209c-42e6-8257-eba555fe5397","process_id":"38787","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '101,110p' docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md; rg -n \"cached|cache\" docs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json docs/v1.2.1 docs/evidence/v1.2.1 --glob '*.md' --glob '*.json'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '101,110p' docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md","name":"PLAN.md","path":"docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md"},{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'cached|cache' docs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json docs/v1.2.1 docs/evidence/v1.2.1 --glob '*.md' --glob '*.json'","query":"cached|cache","path":"wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CX-1 | Label every injected summary block (including `compact --llm`) as untrusted data with its source (G-SI-6) | request-shape snapshot (TI-1) updated; test with an injected instruction in a summary | S | P1 |\n| CX-2 | Compaction state stored separately from the canonical transcript and validated by a hash of the covered prefix on resume (reference: Cline `sdk/ARCHITECTURE.md:643-665`); the record names the covered event ids, the summary and where it is inserted, so a compaction can be audited and undone (reference: OpenHands `Condensation` event, docs 2026-11-15) | resume with a corrupted compaction file falls back to the full transcript; the record lists the covered ids | M | P1 |\n| CX-3 | Compaction probe: after summarising, a second bounded call checks that the summary keeps every gating requirement (reference: Gemini CLI `chatCompressionService.ts:770`); reject and fall back otherwise. Scope: the `compact --llm` path and any future summarising path only; the default ladder (`admitContext`) is deterministic and writes no summary, so CX-5 alone covers it | closes the check `compaction-03` expects on the summarising path, together with CX-5 | M | P1 |\n| CX-4 | Tool-output masking above a token threshold, with the full output retrievable by id (references: Gemini CLI `toolOutputMaskingService.ts:25`, Cline cached tool results). A masked output stays in the evidence store, and the independent reviewer's package (requirements, scope, diff, verifier receipts) does not depend on conversation context, so masking cannot remove evidence (read in `independent_review.ts`, 2026-11-15) | request-shape snapshot shows the mask marker; the id resolves; the reviewer package is identical before and after masking | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL, opt-in. Done: `NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS=<n>` masks, in the request only, a tool result above n estimated tokens once the model has acted on it (a later assistant message exists); the marker names a content id and the full output resolves with `read_archive` query `tool-output:<id>` (live conversation, then archive). Never masked: results not yet acted on, `read_archive`/`read_findings`/`read_objective` results, the pinned ledger, findings and durable records (system messages). Stored conversation and sessions are unchanged. `cli/src/core/tool_output_mask.ts`, `cli/test/tool_output_mask.test.ts` (request-shape marker, id resolves, ledger intact, off by default). The reviewer package is built from the diff and verifier receipts (`operation_runner_execution.ts` `reviewEvidence`), not the conversation, and the review module does not import masking; the test pins that, but it is a structural guard, not an end-to-end review run. Decision: default off, because safe-by-default would need a measured threshold on real tasks (masking can cost the model a retrieval step); a retrieved output enters the conversation whole and is not masked again. Not done: a dedicated id-read tool (the existing archive tool is reused), a default threshold, masking measured on the eval set.\n| CX-5 | Post-compaction invariant: after every compaction, every gating requirement of the pinned ledger (`seedRequirements`) is present in the request, or the turn blocks with a named reason (owns CERI `compaction-03`). Covers each rung of the `admitContext` ladder (recent turns 2, 1, 0 against detail budgets 6000 down to 0) and `compact --llm`; the requirement rows are never omitted or truncated, so a ledger too big for the window blocks the turn (`CONTEXT_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED` naming the requirements), which the code already did; only the verbatim objective text has the whole-or-named deferral (`compaction-02`) | a test walks every rung and asserts the ledger survives; a forced zero-budget rung with an over-limit ledger blocks; scenario `compaction-03` active | S | P1 |\n| CX-6 | Prompt caching: request cache hints for providers that support them; measure cached-token share | share reported in the matrix | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL. Done: Anthropic `cache_control` breakpoints (system prompt, pinned ledger) behind the provider's declared `promptCache` capability and the operator's `NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`; request-shape test shows the marker; the usage summary reports the cached share (`cached N of M input tokens (P%)`). Not done: default-on (needs live Anthropic verification, none made), Bedrock `cachePoint`, operator `providers.json` declaration of the capability, a cached share in the provider matrix doc. OpenAI and Gemini cache implicitly and need no hint.\n| CX-7 | Project instruction files: expand `@path` imports in `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CODEX.md` and `.nexusrules` (today `getProjectRules` in `cli/src/git.ts` loads the first file raw, so a `CLAUDE.md` holding only `@AGENTS.md` puts that literal text in the prompt); bounded depth, cycle-safe, confined to the workspace, expanded only in a trusted workspace, and report which files were loaded (references: the Claude Code `@AGENTS.md` import convention, the usage guide of `agent-rules-books`) | `CLAUDE.md` containing `@AGENTS.md` loads the AGENTS content once; an import outside the workspace, or in an untrusted workspace, is not read; a cycle stops at the depth bound; the loaded-file list is shown | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: DONE as scoped. `getProjectRules` expands `@path` imports (new `cli/src/project_rules.ts`) only for a trusted workspace; real-path confinement, regular files only, 64 KiB per file and 256 KiB total, depth 5, each file read once (cycle-safe); the heading lists loaded files. Tests: `cli/test/project_rules_imports.test.ts`. Not covered: it still loads only the first non-empty file (AGENTS.md wins over CLAUDE.md); `@~/` and absolute paths are not imports; no UI beyond the prompt heading shows the list.\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json:17:      \"cacheBypass\": true,\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json:90:      \"cacheBypass\": true,\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json:162:      \"cacheBypass\": true,\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/MQ-4-first-live-run.md:11:- Runner: installed v1.2.1 and `scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs` invoking the unchanged `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.mjs` fixture, 200 attempts per alias (50 per tool), temperature 0, max output 8192, 1200 ms request interval, cache bypass enabled.\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.rc.2.json:17:      \"cacheBypass\": true,\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.rc.2.json:90:      \"cacheBypass\": true,\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/G1.1-4-rc.2.md:6:- Provider: the shared gateway only (`gateway-openai-chat`, `temperature 0`, `max_tokens 8192`, response cache bypassed on every request, 1200 ms request interval, up to 5 rate-limit retries at 30 s; none were needed). Runner: `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.mjs` from a clean checkout of the candidate commit, unmodified, under the `GATEWAY` claim (claimed and released, logged).\ndocs/v1.2.1/GAP_ANALYSIS.md:125:| GA-20 | Prompt caching not requested (no `cache_control` found) | inventory | P2 | CX-6 |\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json:17:      \"cacheBypass\": true,\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json:90:      \"cacheBypass\": true,\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json:162:      \"cacheBypass\": true,\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/mq4-conformance-first-live-run.json:17:      \"cacheBypass\": true,\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/mq4-conformance-first-live-run.json:90:      \"cacheBypass\": true,\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:97:| WM-3 | Extend the G1.1-4 matrix to every alias the gateway serves, with pacing and cache-bypass checks unchanged | per-alias table in evidence | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:107:| CX-4 | Tool-output masking above a token threshold, with the full output retrievable by id (references: Gemini CLI `toolOutputMaskingService.ts:25`, Cline cached tool results). A masked output stays in the evidence store, and the independent reviewer's package (requirements, scope, diff, verifier receipts) does not depend on conversation context, so masking cannot remove evidence (read in `independent_review.ts`, 2026-11-15) | request-shape snapshot shows the mask marker; the id resolves; the reviewer package is identical before and after masking | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL, opt-in. Done: `NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS=<n>` masks, in the request only, a tool result above n estimated tokens once the model has acted on it (a later assistant message exists); the marker names a content id and the full output resolves with `read_archive` query `tool-output:<id>` (live conversation, then archive). Never masked: results not yet acted on, `read_archive`/`read_findings`/`read_objective` results, the pinned ledger, findings and durable records (system messages). Stored conversation and sessions are unchanged. `cli/src/core/tool_output_mask.ts`, `cli/test/tool_output_mask.test.ts` (request-shape marker, id resolves, ledger intact, off by default). The reviewer package is built from the diff and verifier receipts (`operation_runner_execution.ts` `reviewEvidence`), not the conversation, and the review module does not import masking; the test pins that, but it is a structural guard, not an end-to-end review run. Decision: default off, because safe-by-default would need a measured threshold on real tasks (masking can cost the model a retrieval step); a retrieved output enters the conversation whole and is not masked again. Not done: a dedicated id-read tool (the existing archive tool is reused), a default threshold, masking measured on the eval set.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:109:| CX-6 | Prompt caching: request cache hints for providers that support them; measure cached-token share | share reported in the matrix | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL. Done: Anthropic `cache_control` breakpoints (system prompt, pinned ledger) behind the provider's declared `promptCache` capability and the operator's `NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`; request-shape test shows the marker; the usage summary reports the cached share (`cached N of M input tokens (P%)`). Not done: default-on (needs live Anthropic verification, none made), Bedrock `cachePoint`, operator `providers.json` declaration of the capability, a cached share in the provider matrix doc. OpenAI and Gemini cache implicitly and need no hint.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:116:Audit of 2026-11-15 (`cli/src/providers.ts:46-116`): six hard-coded provider ids (`openrouter`, `openai`, `anthropic`, `chatgpt`, `copilot`, `compatible`); a closed protocol set (`openai-chat` inline in `client.ts`, `openai-responses`, `anthropic-messages` under `cli/src/protocols/`); one unnamed `compatible` slot with no extra headers, no query parameters (so Azure's `?api-version=` breaks), no custom auth header name, no static model list; model lists only from `GET {base}/models`; no per-model capability or price data (context defaults to 65,536 when a cloud provider advertises none); `docs/CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md:165-167` lists Gemini, Azure OpenAI, Bedrock and Vertex as not implemented. Reference data: models.dev (MIT, `sst/models.dev`, 334 provider files, about 10,600 model files; `https://models.dev/api.json`). About 290 of its providers are plain OpenAI-compatible with a base URL and an environment-variable key; per-model `limit.context/input/output`, `capabilities.tools.choice` (the forced-tool modes a model accepts), `capabilities.reasoning.effort`, cache and `cost` fields are present.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:122:| PR-3 | models.dev snapshot: an offline generator script turns the models.dev data into `cli/src/providers.generated.ts` (provider ids, base URLs and env names for the OpenAI-compatible set; per-model context, output, tool-choice modes, reasoning effort, cache and price), dated and schema-validated, with no runtime network fetch; an explicit `nexus models refresh` writes a local cache that overrides the snapshot | table validates in CI; the snapshot date is shown by `nexus providers`; the cache is used only when the operator ran refresh | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:131:| PR-12 | Docs and ops: rewrite `docs/CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `nexus providers` shows source (built-in, snapshot, user file, cache), doctor checks for configured providers | docs match behaviour; doctor test | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:151:| PC-1 | CI wall time: run `linux-x86_64` in parallel with `test-matrix` where its work does not need it, split the CLI suite into shards, cache toolchains; update the stale \"12–20 minutes\" comment (decision D10: required check names and runner minutes) | median PR CI under 40 minutes with the same required-check semantics; measured over 20 runs | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:287:The Code, Security and Slop reviews of the v1.2.1 diff (prompts as the Entire agents ran them) found two medium security issues, one low gate defect and a handful of slop items; those are fixed in the release-review PR. Findings accepted as work for the next release, not done here because they change no behaviour and each touches working code: the `openai-chat` entry in `WIRE_PROTOCOLS` has dead `build`/`parse`/`stream` members; `readChatStream` is a third SSE reader beside `readSse` and `readEventStream`; generated tool-call ids in `openai_chat.ts` use `Math.random`; `encodeFrame` is production code used only by tests; `usage.ts` keeps `reserved` and `reservedBy` as two maps; `boundary_rules.ts` rebuilds glob regexes per call and labels any added `NOT NULL` column as breaking; the CERI library has no shipped consumer; `agent.ts` still lists the external-tool names in four places; `models_snapshot.ts` computes the cache path itself instead of sharing `modelsCachePath`; the injection gate covers only the step after a flagged result and scans the first 200,000 characters of a result.\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/G1.1-4-v1.2.1-other-aliases.md:6:- Provider: the shared gateway only (`gateway-openai-chat`, `temperature 0`, `max_tokens 8192`, response cache bypassed on every request, 1200 ms interval; no throttled retries). Runner: `eval/tool_grammar_matrix.mjs` from a clean checkout of the candidate commit, unmodified, under the `GATEWAY` claim (claimed and released, logged).\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":55917}},"started_at_ms":1795148617317,"completed_at_ms":1795148617318}}
