{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:23:43.420Z","ordinal":29949,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-f2bafdd2-7883-4821-abd1-899bae4d70a2","process_id":"18003","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n -i \"provider matrix|cached share|cached.*share|cache.*share\" docs --glob '*.md' --glob '*.json' --glob '!docs/reviews/evidence/**' --glob '!docs/evidence/**' | head -120; sed -n '109,115p' docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md; rg --files docs/v1.2.2 docs/evidence/v1.2.1 | sed -n '1,120p'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n -i 'provider matrix|cached share|cached.*share|cache.*share' docs --glob '*.md' --glob '*.json' --glob '!docs/reviews/evidence/**' --glob '!docs/evidence/**'","query":"provider matrix|cached share|cached.*share|cache.*share","path":"docs"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '109,115p' docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md","name":"PLAN.md","path":"docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md"},{"type":"list_files","cmd":"rg --files docs/v1.2.2 docs/evidence/v1.2.1","path":"v1.2.2"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"docs/v1.1.4/FINDINGS.md:96:| A4a (WP-1316 supported domain) | P2 | **OPEN, accepted limitation, for the owner at release review.** Three Entire review rounds each fixed one exotic git state and exposed the next, so per-state handling was replaced by one predicate, `snapshotSupport` (`cli/src/core/specialist_inputs.ts`), applied by `candidateDiff` and by the debugger snapshot (`snapshotCandidate`). **Supported:** a stage-0 index of files and symlinks (every `ls-files -v` tag `H`); no sparse checkout; `core.filemode` and `core.symlinks` true; `core.ignorecase` not true; no gitlink (submodule); no unresolved merge; a workspace at the repository root; SHA-1 objects; every path valid UTF-8; no `filter=` attribute and no `.gitattributes` over 1 MiB or unreadable. Exercised by the sweep in `cli/test/specialist_inputs_domain.test.ts` and not refused: detached HEAD, linked worktrees, untracked, ignored and nested-repository paths (in a plain checkout), intent-to-add entries, renames and copies, symlinks, a merge or cherry-pick resolved in the index. **Refused:** each state above that is not supported. **Checkout trust:** both builders run git only after `trustedGitArgs` (`verifyCheckout`) and against the pinned `--git-dir`/`--work-tree`, so a unit worktree whose `.git` pointer was replaced, or that holds a planted repository (a nested repository included), is refused and nothing of it is read or copied. **What each consumer sees on a refusal:** `security_reviewer` runs without the diff and is told why (`candidate diff unavailable: ...`); `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` fall back to a plain `git diff` against the base (`plainCandidateDiff`: private base-tree index, protected paths and protected content left out, no driver run, same checkout trust) and are told it is a plain diff, so a domain refusal does not block the review (an untrusted checkout, a driver refusal, a path count over 2,000, a timeout or any other git failure still blocks; the plain diff refuses (explicit error) when any path in the base tree, index or untracked listing is not valid UTF-8, even an unrelated one, and does not show a force-added ignored new file); a `failure_debugger` cannot be given a snapshot of a refused candidate, so the unit fails with the reason and an operator retry (fix the state, then retry) rather than debugging a wrong tree. A failed rebuild of a stale snapshot leaves the old worktree and branch in place. **Protected-content comparison (new files vs protected sources):** sound only for regular, readable files within the size bound; a protected path that is a symlink (never dereferenced), a special file, a directory in its place, oversized or unreadable means 'cannot compare' and is treated like a protected change: every new file is omitted and counted (`omittedNew`). While a readable protected source exists, every NEW symlink or other non-regular file is omitted too (a link to a directory is followed by `git diff --no-index`). `mv .env notes.txt` of an ignored, untracked `.env` is not detectable (the source is gone from disk and was never tracked): only credential redaction guards it. Protected sources are read through a no-follow descriptor whose real path is checked inside the repository on Linux (a parent directory swapped for a link after the check is refused); on other platforms that window is narrowed, not closed. Out of scope: encoded or re-formatted copies (base64, quoting, values only) and lines under 8 characters; hard links are regular files and are compared. Residual: lines pasted into a file that already existed (status M) are never line-compared; a commented-out secret line is not matched; in the plain diff for a subdirectory workspace the protected-source listings are relative to that subdirectory; `.nexus` links and files over the bound are left out by design; the snapshot does not run the comparison. The plain review diff keeps the real index's staged removals (`git rm --cached` of a file that stays on disk). **Known gaps, not claims of coverage:** the snapshot (`add -A`) and the diff leave out ignored files (`node_modules`, build output, `.env`), so a failure that depends on them is not reproduced; a `-diff` attribute can push the diff over its size limit (reported as too large); git without `GIT_ATTR_SOURCE` silently ignores it (the snapshot's `--path-format=absolute` is the only option here that makes an old git error), so a worktree `.gitattributes` driver would apply there and is caught only by the `filter=` refusal and `check-attr`; the predicate is evaluated once and the listings run after it, so a concurrent index change in a shared (non-isolated) checkout can slip between them; a partial clone is protected from lazy fetch only through `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH` (a newer git; older git ignores it); an unreadable file or an old git fails closed with a raw git message. SHA-256 refusal is tested for the diff only. A finding outside this list is still possible. |\ndocs/v1.1.4/TUI-AUDIT-STATUS.md:19:| D-11 | major | FIXED | `eced1af8` | `cli/test/model_picker_session_endpoint.test.ts` (9 cases: session endpoint listed and current marked, same-as-appliance gives one row, cloud provider gives none, no credential/userinfo/query in label, ids or error, per-endpoint cached fallback, count/length/control-character bounds, timeout and abort, `selectModel` for the session row, sidecar `provider.cancel`); Rust `live_view::tests::the_picker_lists_the_session_endpoints_models_and_marks_the_current_one_at_every_width` (40/60/100 cols), `live_state::tests::closing_the_picker_while_models_are_still_being_listed_cancels_the_fetch` | When the session runs on an endpoint that is neither the appliance nor a cloud provider (`-e`, or the environment), the catalog gains a first row `This session (host:port)` with that endpoint's own `/v1/models`, and `current` points at it, so the picker marks `●` the model in use. Selecting from it keeps the session's endpoint and key and changes only the model (`provider: \"session\"`; refused if a cloud provider became current; not saved to the recent list). Lists are bounded (8 s timeout, 1000 entries, ids at most 200 printable characters, controls removed) and cancellable: a new `models.list`, `provider.cancel` with the list's request id (sent when the picker is closed with Esc while it still says `Listing models`) and shutdown abort the fetch and send nothing; the label is host and port only; error text goes through `scrubUrls` and `redactSecrets`. No protocol change: `session` is an ordinary provider id in the existing `models.catalog` and `model.select`; an older TUI shows it as another provider, an older sidecar never sends it. Model ids with a control, bidi, zero-width or line-separator character are dropped whole (never cleaned into another id), and every provider and model string the picker draws goes through the approval character set (`<U+202E>` etc.; test `the_picker_draws_no_invisible_or_direction_changing_character_from_a_provider`). Limits: once an appliance or cloud model is chosen the `-e` session row disappears until restart (`setInferenceTarget` mutates the shared config); a `-e` session with `fallbackEndpoints` may list a fallback's models under the primary's label; the response body size is not capped (bounded only by the 8 s timeout, pre-existing); the appliance row still shows `could not list models` when nothing listens at its default address (honest: it is a different endpoint); the picker does not mark which endpoint a cloud-routed `-e` goes to beyond the existing `local`/`cloud` word; an env-configured endpoint was not captured in the pty (same code path as `-e`). Captures: `docs/evidence/v1.1.4/tui/D-11/` |\ndocs/v1.1.4/PLAN.md:89:- **Per-turn matching:** score the operator's prompt against each trusted skill's name and description with a local lexical ranker (no model call, no new dependency). When the top scores clear a threshold, add the top 3 to 5 with full descriptions as a system note placed immediately after the newest operator message, never in `messages[0]` and never at a fixed early index. The note is appended to the conversation once, when that operator message arrives, and stays where it was written; it is not re-inserted per request. That keeps every request an append-only extension of the previous one, so the local prefix cache survives each new prompt. This placement differs from the pinned objective, which `requestMessages` (`cli/src/agent.ts`, around line 2711) inserts at index 1 of each request whenever the ledger must be pinned: copying that pattern would put a per-prompt note at the start of the conversation and invalidate the cache on every operator message. The note is bounded by its own share of the window.\ndocs/CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md:280:and killed after 15 s. Tokens are cached and refreshed one minute before expiry; concurrent requests share one refresh;\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:109:| CX-6 | Prompt caching: request cache hints for providers that support them; measure cached-token share | share reported in the matrix | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL. Done: Anthropic `cache_control` breakpoints (system prompt, pinned ledger) behind the provider's declared `promptCache` capability and the operator's `NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`; request-shape test shows the marker; the usage summary reports the cached share (`cached N of M input tokens (P%)`). Not done: default-on (needs live Anthropic verification, none made), Bedrock `cachePoint`, operator `providers.json` declaration of the capability, a cached share in the provider matrix doc. OpenAI and Gemini cache implicitly and need no hint.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:264:**RC and remaining acceptance:** Public [v1.0.0-rc.10](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/releases/tag/v1.0.0-rc.10) remains an unaccepted prerelease from source `16ee8ca3643824741b6da67936b41f262414fdf0`, not the current main source. PR #265's merged OpenRouter evidence covers one pinned-model verification and one synthetic small task. Issue [#1](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/issues/1) remains open without custodian acceptance; no held-out task payload was accessed or run. Installed exact-RC qualification, standalone/provider matrix coverage, T2/T3/T4, the complete terminal matrix, and independent exact-RC acceptance remain incomplete. No stable `v1.0.0` tag or release is published. Existing publication authorization remains gated on every mandatory acceptance gate.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:751:5. **Stage 4 — Installed-RC qualification:** Install the workflow-produced `v1.0.0-rc.1` package via the documented installer and execute `ACCEPTANCE.md` §§4–8 (standalone/provider matrix, CI/scripted/manual terminal matrix, T2 40×5 held-out comparison, T4 9×5 fault matrix, T3 8×5 long-task matrix, install matrix, SPEC-09 §3.2 docs checklist, and §8 independent acceptance). Archive all evidence under `docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/v1.0.0-rc.1/`.\ndocs/ARCHITECTURE.md:64:   - *Filesystem:* the host root is bound read-only (`--ro-bind / /`, remounted read-only) with a fresh `/dev`, `/proc` and an empty `/tmp`; the workspace is bound writable. `$HOME` is replaced by an empty tmpfs, so `~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, `~/.gnupg`, `~/.config/nexus` and browser profiles do not exist inside. Only toolchain directories are bound back read-only: `~/.cargo`, `~/.rustup`, `~/.nvm`, `~/.local/bin`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cache/pip`, `~/go/pkg`, the `PATH` entries that lie under `$HOME` (with their `<prefix>` for a `.../bin` entry), and the paths the operator lists in `NEXUS_SANDBOX_HOME_READONLY` (comma-separated; operator environment or `config.env` only, never a project `.env`). A candidate that is, contains or sits inside a credential location (the same list plus `~/.config`, `~/.local/share`, `~/.cache` as whole directories) is refused, including through a symlink. `~/.cargo/credentials` and `~/.cargo/credentials.toml` are masked with `/dev/null`. `/run/user/<uid>` (ssh/gpg agents, user bus) is an empty tmpfs, and `/run/docker.sock` and `/var/run/docker.sock` are masked. The workspace bind is applied on top of the masked `$HOME`, so when the workspace is `$HOME`, or contains a credential path, those paths are overlaid (a tmpfs for a directory, `/dev/null` for a file) after the workspace bind; they appear empty. `nexus doctor` (`sandbox.confinement`) lists the effective mounts, masks and refusals.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-10-v1-qualification-and-evaluation.md:82:7. **REQ-QUAL-107. Standalone and provider matrix.** The live standalone set (a T2 subset of ≥ 10 tasks plus a T4 subset) is run through the gateway-stripping proxy in its plain profile and its emulated-server profiles (DEC-16). `providers verify` is run live for each provider listed as supported (DEC-06). Receipts are archived.\ndocs/research/context-engineering/TURN-SPECIFIC-CONTEXT-ENGINEERING-HYPOTHESIS.md:151:Q1 Context sensitivity of `engineering/b0|lead|deep` as a function of length (and of distractor load). Q2 Gateway prefix-cache behaviour and whether `usage` can be forwarded. Q3 Right fingerprint granularity for applicability (git candidate identity vs per-path hashes vs per-symbol). Q4 Who sets intent (event, phase, model). Q5 Whether retrieval should be tool-visible to the model or planner-only. Q6 How `/team` roles and cognitive routing (planner/coder models) share or partition the store. Q7 Store location/retention vs the existing irreversible-redaction policy. Q8 What fraction of real tasks exceed the window at all (the corpus suggests few).\ndocs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md:56:| WP-1107 — GBNF tool grammar | Generate tool-schema grammars and integrate supported provider paths. | Commit `1518780d` adds the bounded schema compiler. Commits through `0d506762` connect strict function schemas on the **vLLM request path only**; llama.cpp and Ollama requests keep their native tool schemas (no grammar or format constraint), and the hybrid reasoning grammars in the roadmap are not implemented. The owner-approved full v1.1.2 scope supersedes the older deferral in `ROADMAP-REVIEW.md` §2.5. | **REDUCED SCOPE DELIVERED; SOURCE GATES PASS; INDEPENDENT REVIEW REQUEST CHANGES, FINDINGS WAIVED; LIVE PROVIDER MATRIX NOT MEASURED** | Open items are listed in [the release notes](v1.1.2.md#provider-handling-wp-1107): no expiry for the server-kind cache, `X-Task-Class` can reach a replaced non-gateway server, null normalisation gaps, request counting can turn off, matrix provenance not bound to checkout content. G1.1-4 was not measured. |\ndocs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md:95:**Not done in this record.** No release package for the final stable version was built here, installed, or acceptance-tested (the package rows below are for earlier source). The supported-provider matrix (G1.1-4) was not run. Release-level PTY evidence and installed trace capture on the final artifact were not collected.\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:22:| CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\ndocs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md:50:| REQ-QUAL-107 | ACC-PRV-STANDALONE, ACC-PRV-LIVE | Installed-RC standalone and provider matrix |\ndocs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md:150:### 4.3 Provider matrix (E5)\ndocs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md:335:- [ ] §4 standalone and provider matrix: all rows PASS.\ndocs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md:47:| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n| CX-6 | Prompt caching: request cache hints for providers that support them; measure cached-token share | share reported in the matrix | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL. Done: Anthropic `cache_control` breakpoints (system prompt, pinned ledger) behind the provider's declared `promptCache` capability and the operator's `NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`; request-shape test shows the marker; the usage summary reports the cached share (`cached N of M input tokens (P%)`). Not done: default-on (needs live Anthropic verification, none made), Bedrock `cachePoint`, operator `providers.json` declaration of the capability, a cached share in the provider matrix doc. OpenAI and Gemini cache implicitly and need no hint.\n| CX-7 | Project instruction files: expand `@path` imports in `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CODEX.md` and `.nexusrules` (today `getProjectRules` in `cli/src/git.ts` loads the first file raw, so a `CLAUDE.md` holding only `@AGENTS.md` puts that literal text in the prompt); bounded depth, cycle-safe, confined to the workspace, expanded only in a trusted workspace, and report which files were loaded (references: the Claude Code `@AGENTS.md` import convention, the usage guide of `agent-rules-books`) | `CLAUDE.md` containing `@AGENTS.md` loads the AGENTS content once; an import outside the workspace, or in an untrusted workspace, is not read; a cycle stops at the depth bound; the loaded-file list is shown | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: DONE as scoped. `getProjectRules` expands `@path` imports (new `cli/src/project_rules.ts`) only for a trusted workspace; real-path confinement, regular files only, 64 KiB per file and 256 KiB total, depth 5, each file read once (cycle-safe); the heading lists loaded files. Tests: `cli/test/project_rules_imports.test.ts`. Not covered: it still loads only the first non-empty file (AGENTS.md wins over CLAUDE.md); `@~/` and absolute paths are not imports; no UI beyond the prompt heading shows the list.\n\nNot planned in the CX group: telling the model its remaining token budget (Anthropic context-management post, \"context awareness\"). No such notice exists today; with local models the context limit is an estimate, so a precise number could mislead. Revisit only with a measurement from MQ-8.\n\n### PR: providers (decision D9: every cloud provider configurable)\n\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/G1.1-4-v1.2.1-other-aliases.md\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json.sha256\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/MQ-4-first-live-run.md\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/stable-artifact-verification.md\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.rc.2.json\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/G1.1-4-rc.2.md\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/mq4-conformance-first-live-run.json.sha256\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.rc.2.json.sha256\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/mq4-conformance-first-live-run.json\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":44711}},"started_at_ms":1795148623419,"completed_at_ms":1795148623420}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:23:43.554Z","ordinal":29950,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1f732-2ec2-7150-8947-ed894a51b0a7","call_id":"call_890e3a52003a4db0a2b5e142c32718c0","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 4663)\nTotal output lines: 35\n\ndocs/v1.1.4/FINDINGS.md:96:| A4a (WP-1316 supported domain) | P2 | **OPEN, accepted limitation, for the owner at release review.** Three Entire review rounds each fixed one exotic git state and exposed the next, so per-state handling was replaced by one predicate, `snapshotSupport` (`cli/src/core/specialist_inputs.ts`), applied by `candidateDiff` and by the debugger snapshot (`snapshotCandidate`). **Supported:** a stage-0 index of files and symlinks (every `ls-files -v` tag `H`); no sparse checkout; `core.filemode` and `core.symlinks` true; `core.ignorecase` not true; no gitlink (submodule); no unresolved merge; a workspace at the repository root; SHA-1 objects; every path valid UTF-8; no `filter=` attribute and no `.gitattributes` over 1 MiB or unreadable. Exercised by the sweep in `cli/test/specialist_inputs_domain.test.ts` and not refused: detached HEAD, linked worktrees, untracked, ignored and nested-repository paths (in a plain checkout), intent-to-add entries, renames and copies, symlinks, a merge or cherry-pick resolved in the index. **Refused:** each state above that is not supported. **Checkout trust:** both builders run git only after `trustedGitArgs` (`verifyCheckout`) and against the pinned `--git-dir`/`--work-tree`, so a unit worktree whose `.git` pointer was replaced, or that holds a planted repository (a nested repository included), is refused and nothing of it is read or copied. **What each consumer sees on a refusal:** `security_reviewer` runs without the diff and is told why (`candidate diff unavailable: ...`); `reviewer` and `acceptance_reviewer` fall back to a plain `git diff` against the base (`plainCandidateDiff`: private base-tree index, protected paths and protected content left out, no driver run, same checkout trust) and are told it is a plain diff, so a domain refusal does not block the review (an untrusted checkout, a driver refusal, a path count over 2,000, a timeout or any other git failure still blocks; the plain diff refuses (explicit error) when any path in the base tree, index or untracked listing is not valid UTF-8, even an unrelated one, and does not show a force-added ignored new file); a `failure_debugger` cannot be given a snapshot of a refused candidate, so the unit fails with the reason and an operator retry (fix the state, then retry) rather than debugging a wrong tree. A failed rebuild of a stale snapshot leaves the old worktree and branch in place. **Protected-content comparison (new files vs protected sources):** sound only for regular, readable files within the size bound; a protected path that is a symlink (never dereferenced), a special file, a directory in its place, oversized or unreadable means 'cannot compare' and is treated like a protected change: every new file is omitted and counted (`omittedNew`). While a readable protected source exists, every NEW symlink or other non-regular file is omitted too (a link to a directory is followed by `git diff --no-index`). `mv .env notes.txt` of an ignored, untracked `.env` is not detectable (the source is gone from disk and was never tracked): only credential redaction guards it. Protected sources are read through a no-follow descriptor whose real path is checked inside the repository on Linux (a parent directory swapped for a link after the check is refused); on other platforms that window is narrowed, not closed. Out of scope: encoded or re-formatted copies (base64, quoting, values only) and lines under 8 characters; hard links are regular files and are compared. Residual: lines pasted into a file that already existed (status M) are never line-compared; a commented-out secret line is not matched; in the plain diff for a subdirectory workspace the protected-source listings are relative to that subdirectory; `.nexus` links and files over the bound are left out by design; the snapshot does not run the comparison. The plain review diff keeps the real index's staged removals (`git rm --cached` of a file that stays on disk). **Known gaps, not claims of coverage:** the snapshot (`add -A`) and the diff leave out ignored files (`node_modules`, build output, `.env`), so a failure that depends on them is not reproduced; a `-diff` attribute can push the diff over its size limit (reported as too large); git without `GIT_ATTR_SOURCE` silently ignores it (the snapshot's `--path-format=absolute` is the only option here that makes an old git error), so a worktree `.gitattributes` driver would apply there and is caught only by the `filter=` refusal and `check-attr`; the predicate is evaluated once and the listings run after it, so a concurrent index change in a shared (non-isolated) checkout can slip between them; a partial clone is protected from lazy fetch only through `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH` (a newer git; older git ignores it); an unreadable file or an old git fails closed with a raw git message. SHA-256 refusal is tested for the diff only. A finding outside this list is still possible. |\ndocs/v1.1.4/TUI-AUDIT-STATUS.md:19:| D-11 | major | FIXED | `eced1af8` | `cli/test/model_picker_session_endpoint.test.ts` (9 cases: session endpoint listed and current marked, same-as-appliance gives one row, cloud provider gives none, no credential/userinfo/query in label, ids or error, per-endpoint cached fallback, count/length/control-character bounds, timeout and abort, `selectModel` for the session row, sidecar `provider.cancel`); Rust `live_view::tests::the_picker_lists_the_session_endpoints_models_and_marks_the_current_one_at_every_width` (40/60/100 cols), `live_state::tests::closing_the_picker_while_models_are_still_being_listed_cancels_the_fetch` | When the session runs on an endpoint that is neither the appliance nor a cloud provider (`-e`, or the environment), the catalog gains a first row `This session (host:port)` with that endpoint's own `/v1/models`, and `current` points at it, so the picker marks `●` the model in use. Selecting from it keeps the session's endpoint and key and changes only the model (`provider: …1663 tokens truncated…its emulated-server profiles (DEC-16). `providers verify` is run live for each provider listed as supported (DEC-06). Receipts are archived.\ndocs/research/context-engineering/TURN-SPECIFIC-CONTEXT-ENGINEERING-HYPOTHESIS.md:151:Q1 Context sensitivity of `engineering/b0|lead|deep` as a function of length (and of distractor load). Q2 Gateway prefix-cache behaviour and whether `usage` can be forwarded. Q3 Right fingerprint granularity for applicability (git candidate identity vs per-path hashes vs per-symbol). Q4 Who sets intent (event, phase, model). Q5 Whether retrieval should be tool-visible to the model or planner-only. Q6 How `/team` roles and cognitive routing (planner/coder models) share or partition the store. Q7 Store location/retention vs the existing irreversible-redaction policy. Q8 What fraction of real tasks exceed the window at all (the corpus suggests few).\ndocs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md:56:| WP-1107 — GBNF tool grammar | Generate tool-schema grammars and integrate supported provider paths. | Commit `1518780d` adds the bounded schema compiler. Commits through `0d506762` connect strict function schemas on the **vLLM request path only**; llama.cpp and Ollama requests keep their native tool schemas (no grammar or format constraint), and the hybrid reasoning grammars in the roadmap are not implemented. The owner-approved full v1.1.2 scope supersedes the older deferral in `ROADMAP-REVIEW.md` §2.5. | **REDUCED SCOPE DELIVERED; SOURCE GATES PASS; INDEPENDENT REVIEW REQUEST CHANGES, FINDINGS WAIVED; LIVE PROVIDER MATRIX NOT MEASURED** | Open items are listed in [the release notes](v1.1.2.md#provider-handling-wp-1107): no expiry for the server-kind cache, `X-Task-Class` can reach a replaced non-gateway server, null normalisation gaps, request counting can turn off, matrix provenance not bound to checkout content. G1.1-4 was not measured. |\ndocs/releases/v1.1.2-qualification.md:95:**Not done in this record.** No release package for the final stable version was built here, installed, or acceptance-tested (the package rows below are for earlier source). The supported-provider matrix (G1.1-4) was not run. Release-level PTY evidence and installed trace capture on the final artifact were not collected.\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:22:| CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\ndocs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md:50:| REQ-QUAL-107 | ACC-PRV-STANDALONE, ACC-PRV-LIVE | Installed-RC standalone and provider matrix |\ndocs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md:150:### 4.3 Provider matrix (E5)\ndocs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md:335:- [ ] §4 standalone and provider matrix: all rows PASS.\ndocs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md:47:| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n| CX-6 | Prompt caching: request cache hints for providers that support them; measure cached-token share | share reported in the matrix | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL. Done: Anthropic `cache_control` breakpoints (system prompt, pinned ledger) behind the provider's declared `promptCache` capability and the operator's `NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`; request-shape test shows the marker; the usage summary reports the cached share (`cached N of M input tokens (P%)`). Not done: default-on (needs live Anthropic verification, none made), Bedrock `cachePoint`, operator `providers.json` declaration of the capability, a cached share in the provider matrix doc. OpenAI and Gemini cache implicitly and need no hint.\n| CX-7 | Project instruction files: expand `@path` imports in `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CODEX.md` and `.nexusrules` (today `getProjectRules` in `cli/src/git.ts` loads the first file raw, so a `CLAUDE.md` holding only `@AGENTS.md` puts that literal text in the prompt); bounded depth, cycle-safe, confined to the workspace, expanded only in a trusted workspace, and report which files were loaded (references: the Claude Code `@AGENTS.md` import convention, the usage guide of `agent-rules-books`) | `CLAUDE.md` containing `@AGENTS.md` loads the AGENTS content once; an import outside the workspace, or in an untrusted workspace, is not read; a cycle stops at the depth bound; the loaded-file list is shown | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: DONE as scoped. `getProjectRules` expands `@path` imports (new `cli/src/project_rules.ts`) only for a trusted workspace; real-path confinement, regular files only, 64 KiB per file and 256 KiB total, depth 5, each file read once (cycle-safe); the heading lists loaded files. Tests: `cli/test/project_rules_imports.test.ts`. Not covered: it still loads only the first non-empty file (AGENTS.md wins over CLAUDE.md); `@~/` and absolute paths are not imports; no UI beyond the prompt heading shows the list.\n\nNot planned in the CX group: telling the model its remaining token budget (Anthropic context-management post, \"context awareness\"). No such notice exists today; with local models the context limit is an estimate, so a precise number could mislead. Revisit only with a measurement from MQ-8.\n\n### PR: providers (decision D9: every cloud provider configurable)\n\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/G1.1-4-v1.2.1-other-aliases.md\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json.sha256\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/MQ-4-first-live-run.md\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/stable-artifact-verification.md\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.rc.2.json\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/G1.1-4-rc.2.md\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/mq4-conformance-first-live-run.json.sha256\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.v1.2.1.wm3.json\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/wp-1107-provider-matrix.rc.2.json.sha256\ndocs/evidence/v1.2.1/mq4-conformance-first-live-run.json\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781","create_time":1795148623.5542874}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":13215,"sampled_through_sequence":13212,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
