h a different `\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e...\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003ctools\u003e\nDeferred tool namespaces:\n- mcp__codex_apps__chatgpt_space: Read, search, create, and edit pages and spaces in ChatGPT Space, within your current account.\n- mcp__codex_apps__codex_document_control: Use Codex Document Control to find connected document sessions, inspect the tools supported by a selected session, and execute one supported tool against that session. Call `list_document_sessions` first to choose the intended connected session, c...\n- mcp__codex_apps__github: Access repositories, issues, and pull requests. Required for some features such as Codex\n- mcp__codex_apps__gmail: Gmail tools for label counts, searching and reading emails/threads/attachments, reviewing drafts, and explicit mail changes like send, draft, forward, archive, Trash, and label actions.\n- mcp__codex_apps__hotline: Look up local helpline information for the user based on country inferred from the conversation. You must use this tool before providing a suicide or self-harm helpline; do not use web search or guess.\n- mcp__codex_apps__pets: Create and manage the user's animated companion pets inside ChatGPT Work mode. Use only for ChatGPT Pets, not real-world animal advice, generic pet images, or pets in other apps.\n- mcp__codex_apps__plugin_management: Manage plugins, settings, permissions, and connections. Prefer available built-in tools or connected plugins when they fit the task. Proactively search for plugins when an external app, account, or service would materially help, even if the user d...\n- mcp__codex_apps__safety_settings: For ChatGPT Parental Controls (your child or teen's settings, features, Study Mode, quiet hours, family setup) and Trusted Contact (setup, status, privacy). Read account state first. Before updates, read the child's controls; prepare only can_upda...\n- mcp__codex_apps__search_service: Tool for accessing the internet.\n- mcp__codex_apps__sites: Use Sites to build or modify websites, including landing pages, portfolios, dashboards, portals, trackers, hubs, and internal tools. For repository-based sites, use Sites skills for local implementation, source preparation, and artifact packaging....\n- mcp__mcp_agent_mail: You are the MCP Agent Mail coordination server. Provide message routing, coordination tooling, and project context to cooperating agents. Outputs are JSON by default; pass format='toon' (or set MCP_AGENT_MAIL_OUTPUT_FORMAT=toon) to receive {format...\n- mcp__mcp_search: # Work state: your to-do lists and working state\n- mcp__rea: REA provides reverse-engineering tools for local artifacts, native binaries, managed code, browser pages, and runtimes. Use the tool that directly answers the question; discover targets or inspect inventory only when needed. Tool results include i...\n- mcp__xerj: XERJ tools proxy to a running XERJ engine (ES-compatible). Use xerj_search for retrieval — pass `query` as a plain string for definition-first code search (ranks the defining file first, returns the matching passage instead of whole files, and add...\n\u003c/tools\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003crecommended_plugins\u003e\nHere is a list of plugins that are available but not installed.\n\n- Dropbox (app-69b31dc2110c8191b8b47dc98fe5a052@openai-curated-remote)\n- Box (box@openai-curated-remote)\n- Codex Security (codex-security@openai-curated-remote)\n- Figma (figma@openai-curated-remote)\n- Google Calendar (google-calendar@openai-curated-remote)\n- Google Drive (google-drive@openai-curated-remote)\n- Linear (linear@openai-curated-remote)\n- Notion (notion@openai-curated-remote)\n- OpenAI Developers (openai-developers@openai-curated-remote)\n- Outlook Calendar (outlook-calendar@openai-curated-remote)\n- Outlook Email (outlook-email@openai-curated-remote)\n- SharePoint (sharepoint@openai-curated-remote)\n- Slack (slack@openai-curated-remote)\n- Teams (teams@openai-curated-remote)\n\u003c/recommended_plugins\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a1f751-0cfe-74f1-a869-67f31366a940","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["generic.developer_instructions","memories.instructions","host_skills.instructions","permissions.instructions","collaboration_mode.instructions","tools.deferred_namespaces","plugins.recommendations"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"from_additional_requirements":false,"type":"developer_instructions"}},{"provenance":{"hook":false,"type":"harness"}},{"provenance":{"type":"skills"}},{"provenance":{"hook":false,"type":"harness"}},{"provenance":{"hook":false,"type":"harness"}},{"provenance":{"namespace":"tool_search","type":"tool"}},{"provenance":{"hook":false,"type":"harness"}}],"turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_role\u003eYou are `/root`, the primary agent in a team of agents collaborating to fulfill the user's goals.\n\nAt the start of your turn, you are the active agent.\nYou can spawn sub-agents to handle subtasks, and those sub-agents can spawn their own sub-agents.\nAll agents in the team, including the agents that you can assign tasks to, are equally intelligent and capable, and have access to the same set of tools.\n\nYou can use `spawn_agent` to create a new agent, `followup_task` to give an existing agent a new task and trigger a turn, and `send_message` to pass a message to a running agent without triggering a turn.\n`send_message` calls may be read by a human, so ensure they are legible. Always put proper spaces between words and/or numbers.\nChild agents can also spawn their own sub-agents.\nYou can decide how much context you want to propagate to your sub-agents with the `fork_turns` parameter.\n\nYou will receive messages in the analysis channel in the form:\n```\nMessage Type: MESSAGE | FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: \u003crecipient\u003e\nSender: \u003cauthor\u003e\nPayload:\n\u003cpayload text\u003e\n```\nThey may be addressed as to=/root\n\nNote that collaboration tools cannot be called from inside `functions.exec`. Call `spawn_agent`, `send_message`, `followup_task`, `wait_agent`, `interrupt_agent`, and `list_agents` only as direct tool calls using the recipient shown in their tool definitions, such as `to=functions.collaboration.spawn_agent`, since they are intentionally absent from the `functions.exec` `tools.*` namespace. Available tools in `functions.exec` are explicitly described with a `tools` namespace in the developer message.\n\nAll agents share the same directory. In detail:\n- All agents have access to the same container and filesystem as you.\n- All agents use the same current working directory.\n- As a result, edits made by one agent are immediately visible to all other agents.\n\nWhen calling `wait_agent`, prefer longer waits (minutes) to avoid busy polling.\n\nThere are 4 available concurrency slots, meaning that up to 4 agents can be active at once, including you.\n\nFull-history forks (`fork_turns` omitted or `\"all\"`) inherit the parent model and reasoning effort and do not accept overrides. Only set `model` or `reasoning_effort` when explicitly requested by the user, applicable `AGENTS.md` instructions, or skill instructions; when doing so, set `fork_turns` to `\"none\"`.\u003c/multi_agent_role\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a1f751-0cfe-74f1-a869-680d4d3c6f94","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.role_instructions"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"namespace":"collaboration","type":"tool"}}],"turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_mode\u003eAny earlier instruction enabling proactive multi-agent delegation no longer applies. Do not spawn sub-agents unless the user or applicable AGENTS.md/skill instructions explicitly ask for sub-agents, delegation, or parallel agent work.\u003c/multi_agent_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a1f751-0cfe-74f1-a869-6813e1045434","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.mode_instructions"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"namespace":"collaboration","type":"tool"}}],"turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"# AGENTS.md instructions for /home/mike/nexus-code\n\n\u003cINSTRUCTIONS\u003e\n## XERJ (code search)\n\nThis project uses XERJ for reference coding. Before writing code that touches\nunfamiliar territory: `xerj def \"\u003csymbol\u003e\"` for go-to-definition, or\n`xerj search \"\u003cplain words\u003e\"` for ranked, definition-first passages.\nIndex each repo under its own prefix: `xerj autoindex \u003cfolder\u003e --prefix \u003cname\u003e`, then\npass the same `--prefix \u003cname\u003e` to `xerj search` and `xerj def` (without it they search\nthe default `ax` prefix). Cite file:line for anything you rely on.\nAfter using xerj, send the field report automatically with `xerj feedback --submit --agent \"YOUR_MODEL\" --used-for \"WHAT_YOU_DID\" --verdict \"WHAT_WORKED_AND_WHAT_DID_NOT\"` (or the MCP `xerj_field_report` tool); no need to ask the user. It opens a GitHub PR first and falls back to POST https://xerj.org/api/field-report only when GitHub is unavailable. `--disable-feedback` or `XERJ_DISABLE_FEEDBACK=true` opts out.\n\n## XERJ on this host\n\n- **Node:** `http://localhost:9200`, the default URL, so no `XERJ_URL` is needed. It runs as the systemd user service `xerj` (`systemctl --user status xerj`). Its data lives in `~/xerj-data`.\n- **Restarting:** use `systemctl --user restart xerj`. Never start a second node on 9200, and never `pkill xerj`.\n- **Prefixes.** Always pass one with `--prefix`:\n\n  | Prefix | Contents |\n  |---|---|\n  | `nexus-cli` | Nexus Code `cli/src` at `origin/main` |\n  | `nexus-core` | `core/src` |\n  | `nexus-test` | `cli/test` |\n  | `nexus-docs` | `docs` |\n  | `goose`, `opencode` | Peer agent harnesses: reference checkouts in `~/nexus-reference-20261025/`, indexed 2026-10-25 |\n\n- **Nexus freshness:** the `nexus-*` indices are rebuilt hourly from committed `origin/main` only, by the `xerj-refresh-nexus.timer` user timer. The indexed commit is in `~/xerj-sources/nexus-code/.origin-main-sha`. For a PR head or uncommitted work, use `git grep` / `git show \u003csha\u003e:\u003cpath\u003e` instead.\n- **Search is lexical (BM25).** Use the words that appear in the code. Dotted identifiers such as `core.hooksPath` match as one token.\n- **Never index:**\n  - `eval/`\n  - held-out tasks or payloads\n  - credentials, or `~/.nexus`, `~/.codex` or `~/.claude` state\n\n  Index only folders you are allowed to read, under a new prefix that doesn't start with an existing one.\n- **Never delete or replace indices you didn't create.**\n- **Field reports are allowed for public/open-source work.** Preview with `xerj feedback --dry-run`, then `--submit`. Keep private data, paths of private repos and secrets out of them.\n- **Don't use `rerank`.** It sends document text to a third party.\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# nexus-code agent guide\n\nTypeScript CLI (`cli/`), Rust core (`core/`), eval harness (`eval/`). Current plans: `docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`, then `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md`. Per-release records live in `docs/releases/` and `docs/evidence/`.\n\n## Build and test\n- Build: `make build` (core, then `tsc` in `cli/`). Everything: `make test`; the CI-equivalent run: `make test-ci`. Lint the core with `cargo clippy`; `cli/` has no lint script, `npm --prefix cli run build` type-checks it.\n- One TypeScript test file: `cd cli \u0026\u0026 HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/\u003cname\u003e.test.ts`. A throwaway `HOME` is mandatory: a test that touched the real `HOME` once overwrote `~/.config/nexus/config.env`.\n- Done = the affected test files pass, plus `tsc` for TypeScript changes. CI is the arbiter; a failure that appears only locally comes from host state first (an unbuilt `cli/dist` or Rust core, a stray `/tmp/.git`), so rule that out before calling it a defect.\n- Test-runner or process-handling changes run on Node 22 and 24: the two emit different `node:test` events.\n- Rust builds use an isolated `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated changes.\n- Entire CLI: export `ENTIRE_TOKEN_STORE=file` before any `entire` command. The Entire gate no longer counts toward merging.\n\n## Reference coding with XERJ (required)\n- Before writing non-trivial code that has a peer-project analogue (feature, protocol, parser, worktree or provider handling, tool, test harness), read what mature projects do: query the local XERJ node, then record what you took, borrowed or rejected in the PR's \"Reference coding\" section. Exempt: trivial edits, doc-only changes, fixes fully specified by a failing test.\n- Node, commands, corpora, licence and privacy rules, the field report, and the fallback when XERJ is unavailable (\"XERJ unavailable: \u003creason\u003e\" plus `git grep`): `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md`. Read it when you start that first query.\n\n## Concurrency, claims and release authority\n- Several agents can work here: follow `docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md`. Claim a slice or hot file under `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` before editing; one worktree and branch per slice (`~/nexus-wt/`), never `~/nexus-code` itself; isolate `HOME` for every test or probe; stop a process by its own pid, never `pkill -f`; check `git log` for foreign commits before each commit.\n- Take the `GATEWAY` claim for any inference run; the key comes from the environment (`~/.config/nexus/config.env`) and is never printed. The gateway is the only live provider.\n- Push, tag and publish only on an explicit owner instruction; release authority is the owner's.\n\n## Merging and releasing\nBias to correct: a green PR is not a green `main`, and a pushed tag cannot be taken back.\n- When merging: required checks are `test-matrix (22)`, `test-matrix (24)`, `linux-x86_64`; read each check's conclusion (empty or in-progress is not success). Merge with a merge commit, `gh pr merge \u003cn\u003e --merge --match-head-commit \u003cfull-sha\u003e`. PRs that touch one subsystem merge one at a time, each followed by the CI run on `main`: two PRs green alone can break `main` together.\n- When a PR's checks look wrong after `main` moved: PR checks run on GitHub's merge commit, so confirm the run checked out the current `merge_commit_sha` (`gh api repos/\u003cowner\u003e/\u003crepo\u003e/pulls/\u003cn\u003e --jq .merge_commit_sha`); a stale one tests old content.\n- When cutting a stable release: make a version-only commit directly on the accepted RC (`node scripts/check-version-only-diff.mjs \u003crc-ref\u003e \u003cstable-ref\u003e`), land it on `main` through a merge-commit PR, and only then tag, because `release.yml` requires the tagged commit to be an ancestor of `main`. Version tags are immutable: re-run a failed run, or move to the next RC or patch. Then follow `docs/RELEASE_PROCESS.md`.\n- When reporting a gate or a result: state it as measured (\"ledger closed, qualification pending\"), record failed runs unaltered, and make no frontier-grade claim without external qualification.\n- Before tagging or merging, check: required checks green at the exact head? CI on `main` green after the previous merge? Tagged commit an ancestor of `main`? Claims match the evidence?\n\n\u003c/INSTRUCTIONS\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cenvironment_context\u003e\n  \u003ccwd\u003e/home/mike/nexus-code\u003c/cwd\u003e\n  \u003cshell\u003ezsh\u003c/shell\u003e\n  \u003ccurrent_date\u003e2026-11-20\u003c/current_date\u003e\n  \u003ctimezone\u003eEtc/UTC\u003c/timezone\u003e\n  \u003cfilesystem\u003e\u003cworkspace_roots\u003e\u003croot\u003e/home/mike/nexus-code\u003c/root\u003e\u003croot\u003e/home/mike/.codex/visualizations/2026/11/18/01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76\u003c/root\u003e\u003c/workspace_roots\u003e\u003cpermission_profile type=\"disabled\"\u003e\u003cfile_system type=\"unrestricted\" /\u003e\u003c/permission_profile\u003e\u003c/filesystem\u003e\n\u003c/environment_context\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a1f751-0cfe-74f1-a869-68245dde7b71","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["agents_md.instructions","environments.environment_context"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"agents_md"}},{"provenance":{"hook":false,"type":"harness"}}],"turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"This is FOSS software.. paid infrastructure is NOT going to be in the future either... unless someone decides to donate it.\n","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a1f734-ddb2-7420-b7a9-2905ebeff515","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"user"}}],"create_time":1795148799.4101117,"turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},"role":"user","type":"message"}],"replacement_history_metadata":[{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":13733,"sampled_through_sequence":13727},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":13734,"sampled_through_sequence":13727},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":3,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":false,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f090-d234-7f71-924c-1bd1e08d282e","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f090-bc71-74b2-9880-7a0da806a08f"},"revision":"retained_01a1f090-d23a-7433-944c-15615175ee70"},"sampled_through_sequence":0,"user_input_order":0},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":8,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f091-cc7b-73d3-9d5d-066d526a3244","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f090-bc71-74b2-9880-7a0da806a08f"},"revision":"retained_01a1f091-cc7c-7682-b2e1-c06c325b7ea1"},"sampled_through_sequence":0,"user_input_order":5},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":479,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f155-9cae-7bc2-8a18-bc5d175b9b32","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},"revision":"retained_01a1f155-9cae-7bc2-8a18-bc76a40ee8c6"},"sampled_through_sequence":0,"user_input_order":21},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":513,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f157-a8c8-7cb2-9315-8b1dee7bf10e","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},"revision":"retained_01a1f157-a8c9-7693-92c1-dfaf164934f9"},"sampled_through_sequence":507,"user_input_order":28},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":516,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f157-cc3e-7ee3-ba5d-dceedad2cb6f","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},"revision":"retained_01a1f157-cc3e-7ee3-ba5d-dd08a4022032"},"sampled_through_sequence":507,"user_input_order":29},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":705,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f163-bf57-7600-9fbf-add3cebc467b","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},"revision":"retained_01a1f163-bf58-7030-a6c3-8a22e3139455"},"sampled_through_sequence":702,"user_input_order":40},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":718,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f164-8e61-7a91-9f28-96461f806306","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},"revision":"retained_01a1f164-8e61-7a91-9f28-96659116b7d1"},"sampled_through_sequence":713,"user_input_order":43},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":770,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f16a-71f3-72c1-a20a-76dfab87cc65","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},"revision":"retained_01a1f16a-71f3-72c1-a20a-76f5f51bafaf"},"sampled_through_sequence":766,"user_input_order":47},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":1029,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f17b-9528-7821-ba5d-152bc8bebfbd","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},"revision":"retained_01a1f17b-9528-7821-ba5d-15441addf0f1"},"sampled_through_sequence":1025,"user_input_order":57},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":1046,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f17c-3c3b-7682-8a75-c7095b3fa903","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},"revision":"retained_01a1f17c-3c3b-7682-8a75-c7297d291b05"},"sampled_through_sequence":1042,"user_input_order":59},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":1087,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f17d-ae71-7102-8136-484c8efc5c1a","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},"revision":"retained_01a1f17d-ae72-7a23-b2a6-00cd916d46a7"},"sampled_through_sequence":1085,"user_input_order":63},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":1105,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f17e-45a5-7ba3-b12f-fe6766d88539","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},"revision":"retained_01a1f17e-45a5-7ba3-b12f-fe85aa0d73f4"},"sampled_through_sequence":1101,"user_input_order":65},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":1547,"mcp_attribution":{"sources":[{"first_turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_field_report"}],"status":"complete"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f273-6fa3-7f60-be6b-90025d2a38db","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f273-5cdc-7020-9e8c-3d1f502c8bcc"},"revision":"retained_01a1f273-6fa3-7f60-be6b-902449ba72b6"},"sampled_through_sequence":1542,"user_input_order":74},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":11494,"mcp_attribution":{"sources":[{"first_turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_field_report"},{"first_turn_id":"01a1f3c7-83d5-7590-b34e-54cabca7785a","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_corpus_feedback"},{"first_turn_id":"01a1f552-0afa-72c2-9d7b-074a70278233","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_search"}],"status":"complete"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f55b-a866-7d70-962f-078742fcce96","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f552-0afa-72c2-9d7b-074a70278233"},"revision":"retained_01a1f55b-a866-7d70-962f-07a14311954d"},"sampled_through_sequence":11488,"user_input_order":697},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":13242,"mcp_attribution":{"sources":[{"first_turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_field_report"},{"first_turn_id":"01a1f3c7-83d5-7590-b34e-54cabca7785a","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_corpus_feedback"},{"first_turn_id":"01a1f552-0afa-72c2-9d7b-074a70278233","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_search"}],"status":"complete"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f733-604d-76b1-bd1c-72d9a20adf4b","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},"revision":"retained_01a1f733-604d-76b1-bd1c-72f1078f8a4c"},"sampled_through_sequence":13238,"user_input_order":764},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":13735,"sampled_through_sequence":13727},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":13736,"sampled_through_sequence":13727},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":13737,"sampled_through_sequence":13727},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":13738,"sampled_through_sequence":13727},{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":13273,"mcp_attribution":{"sources":[{"first_turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_field_report"},{"first_turn_id":"01a1f3c7-83d5-7590-b34e-54cabca7785a","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_corpus_feedback"},{"first_turn_id":"01a1f552-0afa-72c2-9d7b-074a70278233","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_search"}],"status":"complete"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a1f734-ddb2-7420-b7a9-2905ebeff515","role":"user","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},"revision":"retained_01a1f734-ddb2-7420-b7a9-2920cf688417"},"sampled_through_sequence":13267,"user_input_order":766},{"client_authored":false,"compaction_model_hash":"3000","history_sequence":13739,"mcp_attribution":{"sources":[{"first_turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_field_report"},{"first_turn_id":"01a1f3c7-83d5-7590-b34e-54cabca7785a","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_corpus_feedback"},{"first_turn_id":"01a1f552-0afa-72c2-9d7b-074a70278233","server_name":"xerj","tool_name":"xerj_search"}],"status":"complete"},"sampled_through_sequence":13727}],"resume_metadata":{"dynamic_tools":{"thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","tools":[]},"last_started_turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781","multi_agent_version":"v2","previous_turn_settings":{"comp_hash":"3000","model":"gpt-6-luna","realtime_active":false},"turn_attribution":{"initiating_agent_path":null,"parent_turn_id":null,"root_turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781","turn_trigger":"goal"}},"retained_context":{"assistant_messages":[{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affcec6f9a887d29e4feb368019bebd","order":783,"phase":"commentary","revision":"retained_01a1f73f-be6a-7652-8a07-7703516a0688","text":"PR #643’s Linux runner is now in the reproducible packaging step; GitHub does not expose its live logs until that step finishes. The two older jobs are still in the same install step. I’m preserving the runs rather than treating an elapsed timeout as a failed check.","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affcf42abb087d2b57d31a34ee583fe","order":786,"phase":"commentary","revision":"retained_01a1f741-a25a-70d1-a897-513c1ddfd39a","text":"I found a narrower, safe VC-6 improvement: when no trusted type-check baseline exists, diagnostics in untouched files can currently be labelled `PREEXISTING` even though Nexus cannot know whether they predate the task. I’ll make that case explicit and blocking for enforced checks, while leaving clean results and known pre-existing diagnostics with an actual baseline unchanged. This avoids trusting mutable saved state.","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affcfb30d4487d2b61594e4404222ce","order":787,"phase":"commentary","revision":"retained_01a1f743-59fb-7d92-ada3-906db8c94657","text":"The change is intentionally narrow: a resumed task with compiler errors in untouched files now reports `UNAVAILABLE` and blocks enforced verification unless a trusted baseline exists. It still allows clean type-check results, and it doesn’t treat remembered diagnostics as trustworthy. I’m installing the CLI’s locked dependencies in the isolated worktree and will run the focused VC-6 tests plus TypeScript compilation.","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affd050295487d2b39330ef3d0ffde3","order":788,"phase":"commentary","revision":"retained_01a1f745-bde8-72d1-a9c4-df3109d30fb6","text":"The first test run exposed a setup issue: the isolated worktree had TypeScript dependencies but not the Rust core binary, so every simulated tool call failed at the core handshake. That invalidated the test results; it did not exercise the change. I’m rebuilding the core in this worktree before rerunning.","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affd1f1f0a487d2bfbb6417698410c9","order":789,"phase":"commentary","revision":"retained_01a1f74c-1eb8-7de0-91f8-49daafdd265f","text":"The resumed-task test confirmed the existing independent-verifier gate blocks completion first: without a trusted pre-change verifier baseline, the task ends `NOT_ESTABLISHED / VERIFIER_FAILED` before the type-check result is used. My proposed extra type-check block would therefore duplicate an existing fail-closed control, so I’m dropping that implementation rather than adding redundant state or tests.","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affd20b6dcc87d2be54a5413036009c","order":790,"phase":"commentary","revision":"retained_01a1f74c-827f-7ca2-b4d3-7ce6f65c4de3","text":"PR #643’s required checks are now green, including Linux packaging and the Node 22/24 suites. Its separate Entire gate failed, but the repository instructions say that gate no longer blocks merging. I’m merging #643 at its verified exact head, then I’ll wait for CI on the resulting `main` before proceeding to the next PR.","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affd235745887d284d505ab85df68e0","order":791,"phase":"commentary","revision":"retained_01a1f74d-280a-76d2-a5a7-a69c7dcdeeff","text":"#643 is merged at `07ce137b`, and the root checkout is fast-forwarded and clean. I’m removing its now-finished local worktree; I’ll retain the branches and worktrees for #639, #642, Claude’s candidate, and the newer orchestration work until each is resolved. CI for the new `main` commit has started, so I’m waiting for that result before refreshing another PR.","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_05793ea695c2aa2c016affd2f9963887d2bc4fe1ae0df9ff97","order":794,"phase":"commentary","revision":"retained_01a1f750-2583-7600-b84c-90ecf37e7572","text":"#642’s checks started against the old base, so they won’t be valid for merging after #643 changed `main`. Its worktree is clean and the branch contains only the VC-2 commits plus earlier main merges. I’m rebasing that PR onto the merged head now so its required checks can evaluate the current content; I’ll still wait for CI on `main` before merging it.","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"}],"assistant_messages_incomplete":true,"incomplete":false,"next_order":795,"user_messages":[{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_01a1f17c-3c3b-7682-8a75-c7095b3fa903","order":59,"revision":"retained_01a1f17c-3c3b-7682-8a75-c7297d291b05","text":"this is a single user repo at the moment.\n","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_01a1f17d-ae71-7102-8136-484c8efc5c1a","order":63,"revision":"retained_01a1f17d-ae72-7a23-b2a6-00cd916d46a7","text":"I don't have anyone else to review\\.. and I've only been met with requests for payment by asking... so not going to keep going down that road. It will remain a single user repo until something changes down the road.\n","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_01a1f17e-45a5-7ba3-b12f-fe6766d88539","order":65,"revision":"retained_01a1f17e-45a5-7ba3-b12f-fe85aa0d73f4","text":"nor should that hold up work being published.\n","turn_id":"01a1f155-7ab7-7bc2-bc69-eefbc04e4519"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_01a1f273-6fa3-7f60-be6b-90025d2a38db","order":74,"revision":"retained_01a1f273-6fa3-7f60-be6b-902449ba72b6","text":"is there anything left on the v1.2.x track that isn't yet complete?\n","turn_id":"01a1f273-5cdc-7020-9e8c-3d1f502c8bcc"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_01a1f276-f125-7ef2-8170-a28dc04b0e2c","order":78,"revision":"retained_01a1f276-f136-7e91-8244-67d42287b73d","text":"\u003ccodex_internal_context source=\"user_goal\"\u003e\nUser set the goal: \"clean up local workspace. Sync with origin. Clean up old worktrees that are no longer required. Then, complete all remaining v1.2.x work that is outstanding.\"\nUser set goal status: \"active\".\n\u003c/codex_internal_context\u003e","turn_id":"auto-compact-2"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_01a1f55b-a866-7d70-962f-078742fcce96","order":697,"revision":"retained_01a1f55b-a866-7d70-962f-07a14311954d","text":"what decisions and resources are still required? \n","turn_id":"01a1f552-0afa-72c2-9d7b-074a70278233"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_01a1f733-604d-76b1-bd1c-72d9a20adf4b","order":764,"revision":"retained_01a1f733-604d-76b1-bd1c-72f1078f8a4c","text":"\u003csend_user_message_question_reply\u003e\n[{\"questionItemId\":\"[\\\"request_user_input_async\\\",\\\"call_447a258d7ec24c24b82047be6dd93164\\\",0]\",\"question\":\"MQ-1/2 benchmarks, MQ-4 scheduler, RD-1/2 runners, and CX-6 live provider checks: defer work needing paid infrastructure, or provide access/budget?\",\"answer\":\"Defer paid infrastructure for v1.2.x (Recommended)\"},{\"questionItemId\":\"[\\\"request_user_input_async\\\",\\\"call_447a258d7ec24c24b82047be6dd93164\\\",1]\",\"question\":\"RD-6 worktree-bound session forks: keep refusing for now, or create a separate checkout for each fork?\",\"answer\":\"Implement what is best for the users of the client.\"}]\n\u003c/send_user_message_question_reply\u003e\n","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"},{"complete":true,"message_id":"msg_01a1f734-ddb2-7420-b7a9-2905ebeff515","order":766,"revision":"retained_01a1f734-ddb2-7420-b7a9-2920cf688417","text":"This is FOSS software.. paid infrastructure is NOT going to be in the future either... unless someone decides to donate it.\n","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781"}],"user_messages_incomplete":true,"verified_answers":[]},"window_id":"01a1f751-0cfe-74f1-a869-67c99c55d6f6","window_number":24}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T04:57:26.532Z","ordinal":31094,"type":"world_state","payload":{"full":true,"state":{"agents_md":{"directory":"/home/mike/nexus-code","text":"## XERJ (code search)\n\nThis project uses XERJ for reference coding. Before writing code that touches\nunfamiliar territory: `xerj def \"<symbol>\"` for go-to-definition, or\n`xerj search \"<plain words>\"` for ranked, definition-first passages.\nIndex each repo under its own prefix: `xerj autoindex <folder> --prefix <name>`, then\npass the same `--prefix <name>` to `xerj search` and `xerj def` (without it they search\nthe default `ax` prefix). Cite file:line for anything you rely on.\nAfter using xerj, send the field report automatically with `xerj feedback --submit --agent \"YOUR_MODEL\" --used-for \"WHAT_YOU_DID\" --verdict \"WHAT_WORKED_AND_WHAT_DID_NOT\"` (or the MCP `xerj_field_report` tool); no need to ask the user. It opens a GitHub PR first and falls back to POST https://xerj.org/api/field-report only when GitHub is unavailable. `--disable-feedback` or `XERJ_DISABLE_FEEDBACK=true` opts out.\n\n## XERJ on this host\n\n- **Node:** `http://localhost:9200`, the default URL, so no `XERJ_URL` is needed. It runs as the systemd user service `xerj` (`systemctl --user status xerj`). Its data lives in `~/xerj-data`.\n- **Restarting:** use `systemctl --user restart xerj`. Never start a second node on 9200, and never `pkill xerj`.\n- **Prefixes.** Always pass one with `--prefix`:\n\n  | Prefix | Contents |\n  |---|---|\n  | `nexus-cli` | Nexus Code `cli/src` at `origin/main` |\n  | `nexus-core` | `core/src` |\n  | `nexus-test` | `cli/test` |\n  | `nexus-docs` | `docs` |\n  | `goose`, `opencode` | Peer agent harnesses: reference checkouts in `~/nexus-reference-20261025/`, indexed 2026-10-25 |\n\n- **Nexus freshness:** the `nexus-*` indices are rebuilt hourly from committed `origin/main` only, by the `xerj-refresh-nexus.timer` user timer. The indexed commit is in `~/xerj-sources/nexus-code/.origin-main-sha`. For a PR head or uncommitted work, use `git grep` / `git show <sha>:<path>` instead.\n- **Search is lexical (BM25).** Use the words that appear in the code. Dotted identifiers such as `core.hooksPath` match as one token.\n- **Never index:**\n  - `eval/`\n  - held-out tasks or payloads\n  - credentials, or `~/.nexus`, `~/.codex` or `~/.claude` state\n\n  Index only folders you are allowed to read, under a new prefix that doesn't start with an existing one.\n- **Never delete or replace indices you didn't create.**\n- **Field reports are allowed for public/open-source work.** Preview with `xerj feedback --dry-run`, then `--submit`. Keep private data, paths of private repos and secrets out of them.\n- **Don't use `rerank`.** It sends document text to a third party.\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# nexus-code agent guide\n\nTypeScript CLI (`cli/`), Rust core (`core/`), eval harness (`eval/`). Current plans: `docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`, then `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md`. Per-release records live in `docs/releases/` and `docs/evidence/`.\n\n## Build and test\n- Build: `make build` (core, then `tsc` in `cli/`). Everything: `make test`; the CI-equivalent run: `make test-ci`. Lint the core with `cargo clippy`; `cli/` has no lint script, `npm --prefix cli run build` type-checks it.\n- One TypeScript test file: `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts`. A throwaway `HOME` is mandatory: a test that touched the real `HOME` once overwrote `~/.config/nexus/config.env`.\n- Done = the affected test files pass, plus `tsc` for TypeScript changes. CI is the arbiter; a failure that appears only locally comes from host state first (an unbuilt `cli/dist` or Rust core, a stray `/tmp/.git`), so rule that out before calling it a defect.\n- Test-runner or process-handling changes run on Node 22 and 24: the two emit different `node:test` events.\n- Rust builds use an isolated `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated changes.\n- Entire CLI: export `ENTIRE_TOKEN_STORE=file` before any `entire` command. The Entire gate no longer counts toward merging.\n\n## Reference coding with XERJ (required)\n- Before writing non-trivial code that has a peer-project analogue (feature, protocol, parser, worktree or provider handling, tool, test harness), read what mature projects do: query the local XERJ node, then record what you took, borrowed or rejected in the PR's \"Reference coding\" section. Exempt: trivial edits, doc-only changes, fixes fully specified by a failing test.\n- Node, commands, corpora, licence and privacy rules, the field report, and the fallback when XERJ is unavailable (\"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" plus `git grep`): `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md`. Read it when you start that first query.\n\n## Concurrency, claims and release authority\n- Several agents can work here: follow `docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md`. Claim a slice or hot file under `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` before editing; one worktree and branch per slice (`~/nexus-wt/`), never `~/nexus-code` itself; isolate `HOME` for every test or probe; stop a process by its own pid, never `pkill -f`; check `git log` for foreign commits before each commit.\n- Take the `GATEWAY` claim for any inference run; the key comes from the environment (`~/.config/nexus/config.env`) and is never printed. The gateway is the only live provider.\n- Push, tag and publish only on an explicit owner instruction; release authority is the owner's.\n\n## Merging and releasing\nBias to correct: a green PR is not a green `main`, and a pushed tag cannot be taken back.\n- When merging: required checks are `test-matrix (22)`, `test-matrix (24)`, `linux-x86_64`; read each check's conclusion (empty or in-progress is not success). Merge with a merge commit, `gh pr merge <n> --merge --match-head-commit <full-sha>`. PRs that touch one subsystem merge one at a time, each followed by the CI run on `main`: two PRs green alone can break `main` together.\n- When a PR's checks look wrong after `main` moved: PR checks run on GitHub's merge commit, so confirm the run checked out the current `merge_commit_sha` (`gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<n> --jq .merge_commit_sha`); a stale one tests old content.\n- When cutting a stable release: make a version-only commit directly on the accepted RC (`node scripts/check-version-only-diff.mjs <rc-ref> <stable-ref>`), land it on `main` through a merge-commit PR, and only then tag, because `release.yml` requires the tagged commit to be an ancestor of `main`. Version tags are immutable: re-run a failed run, or move to the next RC or patch. Then follow `docs/RELEASE_PROCESS.md`.\n- When reporting a gate or a result: state it as measured (\"ledger closed, qualification pending\"), record failed runs unaltered, and make no frontier-grade claim without external qualification.\n- Before tagging or merging, check: required checks green at the exact head? CI on `main` green after the previous merge? Tagged commit an ancestor of `main`? Claims match the evidence?\n"},"apps_instructions":false,"cloud_skills":{"version":1,"entries":{},"roots":{},"usage":{"tokens":0,"characters":0},"instructionsSent":false,"omitDescriptions":false,"allocation":{"totalBudget":{"tokens":5440},"cloudLimit":4080,"cloudCatalogFingerprint":[213,61,24,194,50,18,234,123,99,0,89,75,184,155,206,96,33,143,110,255,43,157,98,139,140,196,45,62,121,187,213,171]}},"collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","model":"gpt-6-luna","instructions":"afcdf1ff905fb4e61ff4d6d89af089dbda3d5e4e"},"context_window_guidance":"","environments":{"environments":{"local":{"cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","status":"available","shell":"zsh"}},"current_date":"2026-11-20","timezone":"Etc/UTC","filesystem":"<filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/home/mike/nexus-code</root><root>/home/mike/.codex/visualizations/2026/11/18/01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"disabled\"><file_system type=\"unrestricted\" /></permission_pro