{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T06:40:35.507Z","ordinal":32832,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-1f9742b9-97b6-46d9-870b-343ec6de1690","process_id":"11558","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n 'three|multi.file|real servers|not exercised|Still open|unvalidated' cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md; git diff --check; git diff -- docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/v1-2x-backlog-reconcile","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rg -n 'three|multi.file|real servers|not exercised|Still open|unvalidated' cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md; git diff --check; git diff -- docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts:22:  unvalidated?: string;\ncli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts:146:  // An unvalidated case still runs and reports; it fails the run only when NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE names it.\ncli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts:147:  const todo = c.unvalidated && !required(c.id) ? c.unvalidated : false;\ncli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts:213:// Navigation (hover and definition) against the same real servers: a function defined in one file and called in another.\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:3:`cli/src/lsp.ts` (the `lsp_diagnostics`, `lsp_hover` and `lsp_definition` tools) is tested in the ordinary suite against `cli/test/fixtures/fake_lsp_server.mjs`. `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts` also runs the product's own `LspBridgeManager` against real servers on fixture projects written to a temporary directory. Navigation cases use a three-source-file import/re-export chain (or equivalent package-level call chain) and require hover and definition to resolve through that project. The `lsp-real-servers` workflow runs it weekly (Monday 04:17 UTC) and on demand. It is not a required check.\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:5:Per server the test asserts three things: a file with one deliberate error yields `status: \"ok\"` with an error diagnostic whose message names it; a clean file yields `status: \"ok\"` with an empty list (a published report, which is not `unavailable`); and a manager whose server cannot start yields `unavailable`.\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:33:- A larger real project: navigation uses three source files, but there is no large workspace-wide diagnostics fixture, incremental-edit sequence, or server crash/restart scenario.\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:35:- Hosted-runner result (manual run 44323291710 on `3732d911`, 2026-11-18): **typescript-language-server, pyright and gopls passed** (a deliberate error reported, a clean file a real empty report, a broken server `unavailable`), which also confirms on real servers that the default launch needs `--stdio`. **rust-analyzer failed**: the product reported \"not installed or failed to start\" although the rustup proxy answered `--version` in the test's probe. The cause is not diagnosed (it needs a host with the rust-analyzer component to reproduce); the Rust case therefore runs as a `todo` (reported, not failing the job) unless `NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE` names it, and the workflow requires `typescript,python,go`. A manual run of that change on its branch (run 44323915622) completed successfully: 5 pass, 0 fail, 1 todo (Rust).\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:38:- **Three-file navigation extension:** hosted run [44606102675](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/actions/runs/44606102675) on commit `99c6049f` (2026-11-19) passed all 10 tests with `NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE=typescript,python,go,rust`: all four real servers passed the diagnostics and navigation cases, with 0 failures, skips, or todos. This is a small three-source-file chain, not evidence for large-project behavior.\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:39:- **Hover and definition (CS-6 follow-up):** each real server is asked, through the product, to hover and go to the definition through a three-source-file project (positions are 1-based, as `lsp_hover` and `lsp_definition` take them); the call is retried until the case timeout because servers answer only after analysis, and the definition must point into the defining file. This checks a small import/re-export chain; it does not establish behavior on large projects.\ndiff --git a/docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md b/docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md\nindex 907923ac..c71375ec 100644\n--- a/docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md\n+++ b/docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md\n@@ -2,26 +2,32 @@\n \n What the v1.2.1 plan ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md)) did not finish, with the reason and the first step for each. Nothing here is scheduled by date; the owner chooses what ships. Units marked **needs** cannot be finished by writing code alone.\n \n-## Needs a resource or an owner decision\n+## Out of scope without donated capacity; no paid infrastructure is planned\n \n-| Unit | Not done | Needs | First step |\n+| Unit | Not done | Constraint | If donated or otherwise available at no cost |\n |---|---|---|---|\n-| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | Containerized benchmark environment, an owner-selected pinned subset, and a concrete inference-spend ceiling. D3/D4 already decide the publication rule (publish nothing before a result) and measurement class (small self-run E1); they do not set the actual subset or spend cap. | Build the adapter against a mock gateway; before live inference, select the subset and approve its spend ceiling. |\n-| MQ-4 | Scheduled gateway conformance: the job script, claim protocol and alert path are built and tested (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`) | A local scheduler on the host that can reach the gateway (a timer unit example is documented, nothing is installed) and a first live run with a prepared config | Install the timer (via Ansible if the host is managed that way), keep a `last-good-result.json` baseline, run once by hand |\n-| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | A macOS runner and an arm64 or musl runner. D6 currently accepts Linux x86_64 only; expanding supported release targets requires a new owner go/no-go and runner-cost decision. | If expanding platforms, decide which target and runner cost; reuse `release.yml` packaging. |\n-| PC-1 | Median PR CI under 40 minutes (measured 37 to 45 minutes) | Time on the runners | Shard the CLI suite and cache toolchains; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is already in place |\n+| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | No paid inference or benchmark service. D3/D4 already decide the publication rule (publish nothing before a result) and measurement class (small self-run E1); the pinned subset remains an owner choice. | Build or retain the adapter against a mock gateway. Run a live subset only on donated capacity or an available no-cost local endpoint; otherwise keep live results unpublished and the work out of scope. |\n+| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | No paid runners. D6 currently accepts Linux x86_64 only; expanding release targets needs suitable donated or otherwise no-cost hardware and an owner go/no-go. | If suitable hardware is donated or already available at no cost, choose the target and reuse `release.yml`; otherwise keep Linux x86_64 as the supported target. |\n+| CX-6 | Live Anthropic/Bedrock cache validation and provider-matrix updates | No paid API usage. The existing cache option remains opt-in; no claim of live provider validation is made. | Validate only with donated credits or an already available no-cost account; otherwise leave live validation out of scope. |\n+\n+## No-cost work that remains actionable\n+\n+| Unit | Not done | First step |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| MQ-4 | Scheduled gateway conformance: the job script, claim protocol and alert path are built and tested (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`); the local scheduler and first live run are not installed or evidenced. | Check whether a user scheduler and gateway configuration are already available. If so, add the timer and record one run; do not make paid infrastructure a prerequisite. |\n+| PC-1 | Median PR CI under 40 minutes (measured 37 to 45 minutes) | Measure the current required checks, then shard the CLI suite and cache toolchains if it reduces elapsed time; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is already in place. |\n \n ## Partly done: what is left\n \n | Unit | Done in v1.2.1 / since | Left |\n |---|---|---|\n-| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations (`/requirements obligate|minimise|retire|reference`), replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording for file tools and the shell; exact-test result binding in the verifier output | Still open: a shell edit to a test file larger than 64 KiB, or outside the 20000-entry scan, is not recorded; weakening is recorded, never blocked. Runner output can still be fabricated by a workspace-controlled test command, so an output line is evidence of a report, not proof the test body executed. |\n+| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations, replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording and exact-test result binding. PR #642 adds fail-closed handling for shell edits to oversized verification files and incomplete bounded scans. | In `verificationPaths.mode=block`, the relevant uncertain shell edit is refused before side effects. Remaining limitation: workspace-controlled test commands can fabricate their own output, so a reported passing line is not proof the test body executed. |\n | VC-6 | **Done for configured project coverage**: every discovered TypeScript, Rust, Go, and configured Python typecheck project is checked; missing configured tools report `UNAVAILABLE`; baseline deltas distinguish introduced from pre-existing diagnostics. Evidence: PR #631 and `cli/test/vc6_typecheck_gaps.test.ts` (discovery, multiple configs, bounded discovery, nested Rust, unavailable tools, shell changes). | Still open: resumed tasks do not retain a trustworthy pre-change typecheck baseline; Cargo is excluded from the baseline run because it is not cheap. These limits must remain visible in the verifier result and completion policy. |\n | VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\n | CX-4 | Opt-in masking (`NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS`) with ids resolved through `read_archive` | A default threshold (not shown safe); a dedicated id-read tool; measurement of masking on real tasks |\n | CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\n | CX-7 | **Done**: trusted workspaces load all non-empty instruction files in precedence order; imports are constrained by real-path containment; `~/` and absolute paths are supported when they resolve inside the trusted workspace. Evidence: `cli/test/project_rules_imports.test.ts` (including untrusted-workspace, cycle, depth-bound, and symlink escape cases). |\n-| RD-6 | **Done for the accepted single-checkout fork behavior**: `SessionManager.forkSession` and `/fork [n]` preserve the source and compaction integrity; `--fork-session`/`--fork-at` work on `run`, `repl`, and top-level launch; worktree-bound forks are refused before creating or saving a fork. `forkedFrom` is persisted on later session saves. Evidence: `cli/test/session_fork.test.ts` and `cli/test/session_fork_start.test.ts`, including the REPL `/fork` worktree-refusal test. | Carrying a worktree binding would require creating a second checkout; this remains an owner design decision and is not required for the safe refusal behavior. |\n+| RD-6 | **Done for the safe single-checkout fork behavior**: forks preserve the source and compaction integrity; worktree-bound forks are refused before creating or saving a fork; `forkedFrom` persists. The owner selected the refusal behavior as safer than implicitly creating a second checkout that could omit uncommitted state. Evidence: `cli/test/session_fork.test.ts` and `cli/test/session_fork_start.test.ts`. | No remaining decision for v1.2.x. |\n | CS-6 | Test `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts`, weekly non-required workflow `lsp-real-servers.yml`, `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`; typescript-language-server and pyright validated locally (pass; mutation-checked); fixed the missing `--stdio` for TS/JS/Python | The workflow has not yet run on a hosted runner; rust-analyzer and gopls cases are written but have run nowhere (not installed on the dev host), so treat them as unvalidated; hover, definition, the trust and sandbox gates and multi-file projects are not exercised against real servers; versions of rust-analyzer and gopls are not pinned by the test |\n | MT-3 | One `redactPlain` and one `safeTerminal` use in place of 7 and 3 inline copies (part 1); part 2: a characterization test pins 23 variants over a shared corpus, `formatSafeSidecarText` now calls `safeTerminal`, and every remaining variant has a row with the reason it differs in `docs/v1.2.1/REDACTION_VARIANTS.md` | Nothing planned. The audit found only one more behaviour-preserving collapse; the rest differ in bytes (escape stripping, classifier or no classifier, control handling order, credential-key replacement). Module-private variants (`ceri` `redactedJson`, `safeTerminalText`, `flat`, `redactDeep`) are not in the characterization test |\n | MQ-7 | **Done**: slices B–G are implemented and merged in PR #627. Slices D (installed-evaluator binding), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery-reference integrity), and G (bounded campaigns) have focused coverage in `cli/test/ceri_mq7dg.test.ts`; B/C coverage is in `cli/test/ceri_mq7bc.test.ts`. | No remaining v1.2.2 implementation item identified in this unit. |\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":681062582}},"started_at_ms":1795156834825,"completed_at_ms":1795156835507}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T06:40:35.628Z","ordinal":32833,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1f7af-7d2c-75a0-9b8c-ed383534a335","call_id":"call_bc5d360a78924aebae8312aad9c97c6d","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 3455)\nTotal output lines: 55\n\ncli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts:22:  unvalidated?: string;\ncli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts:146:  // An unvalidated case still runs and reports; it fails the run only when NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE names it.\ncli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts:147:  const todo = c.unvalidated && !required(c.id) ? c.unvalidated : false;\ncli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts:213:// Navigation (hover and definition) against the same real servers: a function defined in one file and called in another.\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:3:`cli/src/lsp.ts` (the `lsp_diagnostics`, `lsp_hover` and `lsp_definition` tools) is tested in the ordinary suite against `cli/test/fixtures/fake_lsp_server.mjs`. `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts` also runs the product's own `LspBridgeManager` against real servers on fixture projects written to a temporary directory. Navigation cases use a three-source-file import/re-export chain (or equivalent package-level call chain) and require hover and definition to resolve through that project. The `lsp-real-servers` workflow runs it weekly (Monday 04:17 UTC) and on demand. It is not a required check.\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:5:Per server the test asserts three things: a file with one deliberate error yields `status: \"ok\"` with an error diagnostic whose message names it; a clean file yields `status: \"ok\"` with an empty list (a published report, which is not `unavailable`); and a manager whose server cannot start yields `unavailable`.\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:33:- A larger real project: navigation uses three source files, but there is no large workspace-wide diagnostics fixture, incremental-edit sequence, or server crash/restart scenario.\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:35:- Hosted-runner result (manual run 44323291710 on `3732d911`, 2026-11-18): **typescript-language-server, pyright and gopls passed** (a deliberate error reported, a clean file a real empty report, a broken server `unavailable`), which also confirms on real servers that the default launch needs `--stdio`. **rust-analyzer failed**: the product reported \"not installed or failed to start\" although the rustup proxy answered `--version` in the test's probe. The cause is not diagnosed (it needs a host with the rust-analyzer component to reproduce); the Rust case therefore runs as a `todo` (reported, not failing the job) unless `NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE` names it, and the workflow requires `typescript,python,go`. A manual run of that change on its branch (run 44323915622) completed successfully: 5 pass, 0 fail, 1 todo (Rust).\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:38:- **Three-file navigation extension:** hosted run [44606102675](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/actions/runs/44606102675) on commit `99c6049f` (2026-11-19) passed all 10 tests with `NEXUS_LSP_REQUIRE=typescript,python,go,rust`: all four real servers passed the diagnostics and navigation cases, with 0 failures, skips, or todos. This is a small three-source-file chain, not evidence for large-project behavior.\ndocs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md:39:- **Hover and definition (CS-6 follow-up):** each real server is asked, through the product, to hover and go to the definition through a three-source-file project (positions are 1-based, as `lsp_hover` and `lsp_definition` take them); the call is retried until the case timeout because servers answer only after analysis, and the definition must point into the defining file. This checks a small import/re-export chain; it does not establish behavior on large projects.\ndiff --git a/docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md b/docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md\nindex 907923ac..c71375ec 100644\n--- a/docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md\n+++ b/docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md\n@@ -2,26 +2,32 @@\n \n What the v1.2.1 plan ([`docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`](../v1.2.1/PLAN.md)) did not finish, with the reason and the first step for each. Nothing here is scheduled by date; the owner chooses what ships. Units marked **needs** cannot be finished by writing code alone.\n \n-## Needs a resource or an owner decision\n+## Out of scope without donated capacity; no paid infrastructure is planned\n \n-| Unit | Not done | Needs | First step |\n+| Unit | Not done | Constraint | If donated or otherwise available at no cost |\n |---|---|---|---|\n-| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | Containerized benchmark environment, an owner-selected pinned subset, and a concrete inference-spend ceiling. D3/D4 already decide the publication rule (publish nothing before a result) and measurement class (small self-run E1); they do not set the actual subset or spend cap. | Build the adapter against a mock gateway; before live inference, select the subset and approve its spend ceiling. |\n-| MQ-4 | Scheduled gateway conformance: the job script, claim protocol and alert path are built and tested (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`) | A local scheduler on the host that can reach the gateway (a timer unit example is documented, nothing is installed) and a first live run with a prepared config | Install the timer (via Ansible if the host is managed that way), keep a `last-good-result.json` baseline, run once by hand |\n-| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | A macOS runner and an arm64 or musl runner. D6 currently accepts Linux x86_64 only; expanding supported release targets requires a new owner go/no-go and runner-cost decision. | If expanding platforms, decide which target and runner cost; reuse `release.yml` packaging. |\n-| PC-1 | Median PR CI under 40 minutes (measured 37 to 45 minutes) | Time on the runners | Shard the CLI suite and cache toolchains; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is already in place |\n+| MQ-1, MQ-2 | Benchmark adapter (Harbor / Terminal-Bench) and the false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | No paid inference or benchmark service. D3/D4 already decide the publication rule (publish nothing before a result) and m…455 tokens truncated…VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations (`/requirements obligate|minimise|retire|reference`), replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording for file tools and the shell; exact-test result binding in the verifier output | Still open: a shell edit to a test file larger than 64 KiB, or outside the 20000-entry scan, is not recorded; weakening is recorded, never blocked. Runner output can still be fabricated by a workspace-controlled test command, so an output line is evidence of a report, not proof the test body executed. |\n+| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations, replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording and exact-test result binding. PR #642 adds fail-closed handling for shell edits to oversized verification files and incomplete bounded scans. | In `verificationPaths.mode=block`, the relevant uncertain shell edit is refused before side effects. Remaining limitation: workspace-controlled test commands can fabricate their own output, so a reported passing line is not proof the test body executed. |\n | VC-6 | **Done for configured project coverage**: every discovered TypeScript, Rust, Go, and configured Python typecheck project is checked; missing configured tools report `UNAVAILABLE`; baseline deltas distinguish introduced from pre-existing diagnostics. Evidence: PR #631 and `cli/test/vc6_typecheck_gaps.test.ts` (discovery, multiple configs, bounded discovery, nested Rust, unavailable tools, shell changes). | Still open: resumed tasks do not retain a trustworthy pre-change typecheck baseline; Cargo is excluded from the baseline run because it is not cheap. These limits must remain visible in the verifier result and completion policy. |\n | VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\n | CX-4 | Opt-in masking (`NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS`) with ids resolved through `read_archive` | A default threshold (not shown safe); a dedicated id-read tool; measurement of masking on real tasks |\n | CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\n | CX-7 | **Done**: trusted workspaces load all non-empty instruction files in precedence order; imports are constrained by real-path containment; `~/` and absolute paths are supported when they resolve inside the trusted workspace. Evidence: `cli/test/project_rules_imports.test.ts` (including untrusted-workspace, cycle, depth-bound, and symlink escape cases). |\n-| RD-6 | **Done for the accepted single-checkout fork behavior**: `SessionManager.forkSession` and `/fork [n]` preserve the source and compaction integrity; `--fork-session`/`--fork-at` work on `run`, `repl`, and top-level launch; worktree-bound forks are refused before creating or saving a fork. `forkedFrom` is persisted on later session saves. Evidence: `cli/test/session_fork.test.ts` and `cli/test/session_fork_start.test.ts`, including the REPL `/fork` worktree-refusal test. | Carrying a worktree binding would require creating a second checkout; this remains an owner design decision and is not required for the safe refusal behavior. |\n+| RD-6 | **Done for the safe single-checkout fork behavior**: forks preserve the source and compaction integrity; worktree-bound forks are refused before creating or saving a fork; `forkedFrom` persists. The owner selected the refusal behavior as safer than implicitly creating a second checkout that could omit uncommitted state. Evidence: `cli/test/session_fork.test.ts` and `cli/test/session_fork_start.test.ts`. | No remaining decision for v1.2.x. |\n | CS-6 | Test `cli/test/lsp_real_servers.test.ts`, weekly non-required workflow `lsp-real-servers.yml`, `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`; typescript-language-server and pyright validated locally (pass; mutation-checked); fixed the missing `--stdio` for TS/JS/Python | The workflow has not yet run on a hosted runner; rust-analyzer and gopls cases are written but have run nowhere (not installed on the dev host), so treat them as unvalidated; hover, definition, the trust and sandbox gates and multi-file projects are not exercised against real servers; versions of rust-analyzer and gopls are not pinned by the test |\n | MT-3 | One `redactPlain` and one `safeTerminal` use in place of 7 and 3 inline copies (part 1); part 2: a characterization test pins 23 variants over a shared corpus, `formatSafeSidecarText` now calls `safeTerminal`, and every remaining variant has a row with the reason it differs in `docs/v1.2.1/REDACTION_VARIANTS.md` | Nothing planned. The audit found only one more behaviour-preserving collapse; the rest differ in bytes (escape stripping, classifier or no classifier, control handling order, credential-key replacement). Module-private variants (`ceri` `redactedJson`, `safeTerminalText`, `flat`, `redactDeep`) are not in the characterization test |\n | MQ-7 | **Done**: slices B–G are implemented and merged in PR #627. Slices D (installed-evaluator binding), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery-reference integrity), and G (bounded campaigns) have focused coverage in `cli/test/ceri_mq7dg.test.ts`; B/C coverage is in `cli/test/ceri_mq7bc.test.ts`. | No remaining v1.2.2 implementation item identified in this unit. |\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781","create_time":1795156835.6282158}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":14497,"sampled_through_sequence":14494,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
