{"timestamp":"2026-11-20T07:31:20.348Z","ordinal":33506,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f090-96c7-7f02-ae7d-1a090d633b76","turn_id":"01a1f730-5dda-7050-8748-e755b63ef781","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-4a4e49ed-c0d2-4dca-97d5-68ca4e6f6b07","process_id":"40268","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n -C 4 'MQ-1|MQ-2|PC-1|VC-9|CX-4|WM-3' docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n -C 4 'MQ-1|MQ-2|PC-1|VC-9|CX-4|WM-3' docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md","query":"MQ-1|MQ-2|PC-1|VC-9|CX-4|WM-3","path":"PLAN.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"docs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-5-## External-capacity limits; no paid infrastructure is planned\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-6-\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-7-| Unit | Not done | Constraint | If donated or otherwise available at no cost |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-8-|---|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:9:| MQ-1, MQ-2 live benchmark | Empirical false-completion report (pass rate, claimed-done-but-wrong, verified-but-wrong, Wilson intervals, peer comparison) | No paid inference or benchmark service. D3/D4 already decide the publication rule (publish nothing before a result) and measurement class (small self-run E1); the pinned subset remains an owner choice. | Run a live subset only on donated capacity or an available no-cost local endpoint; otherwise keep live results unpublished and out of scope. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-10-| RD-1, RD-2 | macOS and arm64/musl artifacts | No paid runners. D6 currently accepts Linux x86_64 only; expanding release targets needs suitable donated or otherwise no-cost hardware and an owner go/no-go. | If suitable hardware is donated or already available at no cost, choose the target and reuse `release.yml`; otherwise keep Linux x86_64 as the supported target. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-11-| CX-6 | Live Anthropic/Bedrock cache validation and provider-matrix updates | No paid API usage. The existing cache option remains opt-in; no claim of live provider validation is made. | Validate only with donated credits or an already available no-cost account; otherwise leave live validation out of scope. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-12-\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-13-## No-cost work that remains actionable\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-14-\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-15-| Unit | Not done | First step |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-16-|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:17:| MQ-1, MQ-2 adapter | Benchmark adapter and false-completion report; no live benchmark result exists. | Build and test the adapter against a mock gateway with synthetic results. This does not require paid inference; publish no empirical claims until a live no-cost or donated run exists. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-18-| MQ-4 | Scheduled gateway conformance: the job script, claim protocol and alert path are built and tested (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`); the local scheduler and first live run are not installed or evidenced. | Check whether a user scheduler and gateway configuration are already available. If so, add the timer and record one run; do not make paid infrastructure a prerequisite. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:19:| PC-1 | Median PR CI under 40 minutes (measured 37 to 45 minutes) | Measure the current required checks, then shard the CLI suite and cache toolchains if it reduces elapsed time; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is already in place. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-20-\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-21-## Partly done: what is left\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-22-\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-23-| Unit | Done in v1.2.1 / since | Left |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-24-|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-25-| VC-2 | Operator-authorised regression obligations, replay recording, completion gated on unmet obligations, weakening-edit recording and exact-test result binding. PR #642 adds fail-closed handling for shell edits to oversized verification files and incomplete bounded scans. | In `verificationPaths.mode=block`, the relevant uncertain shell edit is refused before side effects. Remaining limitation: workspace-controlled test commands can fabricate their own output, so a reported passing line is not proof the test body executed. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-26-| VC-6 | **Done for configured project coverage**: every discovered TypeScript, Rust, Go, and configured Python typecheck project is checked; missing configured tools report `UNAVAILABLE`; baseline deltas distinguish introduced from pre-existing diagnostics. Evidence: PR #631 and `cli/test/vc6_typecheck_gaps.test.ts` (discovery, multiple configs, bounded discovery, nested Rust, unavailable tools, shell changes). | Still open: resumed tasks do not retain a trustworthy pre-change typecheck baseline; Cargo is excluded from the baseline run because it is not cheap. These limits must remain visible in the verifier result and completion policy. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:27:| VC-9 | Audit with file:line for 14 paths; BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT with a machine-readable question and `--answer` resume for tool approvals, verification-file edits and the verifier or type-check at completion; a delegated subagent's question is returned to the parent and ends the parent's headless run BLOCKED/NEEDS_INPUT (`spawn_subagent` only); the requirement-verifier approval has its own test | A requirement marked blocked (changes the `REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE` contract and needs a free-text answer); an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar protocol decision) |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:28:| CX-4 | Opt-in masking (`NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS`) with ids resolved through `read_archive` | A default threshold (not shown safe); a dedicated id-read tool; measurement of masking on real tasks |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-29-| CX-6 | Opt-in Anthropic cache breakpoints (`NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`, provider declares `promptCache: \"anthropic\"`) and a cached-token share in the usage summary | Verification against the live Anthropic API; default-on; Bedrock `cachePoint`; declaring the capability from an operator `providers.json`; a share column in the provider matrix doc |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-30-| CX-7 | **Done**: trusted workspaces load all non-empty instruction files in precedence order; imports are constrained by real-path containment; `~/` and absolute paths are supported when they resolve inside the trusted workspace. Evidence: `cli/test/project_rules_imports.test.ts` (including untrusted-workspace, cycle, depth-bound, and symlink escape cases). |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-31-| RD-6 | **Done for the safe single-checkout fork behavior**: forks preserve the source and compaction integrity; worktree-bound forks are refused before creating or saving a fork; `forkedFrom` persists. The owner selected the refusal behavior as safer than implicitly creating a second checkout that could omit uncommitted state. Evidence: `cli/test/session_fork.test.ts` and `cli/test/session_fork_start.test.ts`. | No remaining decision for v1.2.x. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-32-| CS-6 | Real-server diagnostics, hover and definition for TypeScript, Python, Go and Rust; three-source-file navigation; pinned hosted toolchains. Evidence: hosted run [44670790609](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/actions/runs/44670790609) passed on `f87ff07f`; see `docs/LSP_REAL_SERVERS.md`. | Still untested against real servers: trust/sandbox refusal paths, large workspace diagnostics, incremental edits, and server crash/restart. The current hosted result covers small three-file projects only. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-33-| MT-3 | One `redactPlain` and one `safeTerminal` use in place of 7 and 3 inline copies (part 1); part 2: a characterization test pins 23 variants over a shared corpus, `formatSafeSidecarText` now calls `safeTerminal`, and every remaining variant has a row with the reason it differs in `docs/v1.2.1/REDACTION_VARIANTS.md` | Nothing planned. The audit found only one more behaviour-preserving collapse; the rest differ in bytes (escape stripping, classifier or no classifier, control handling order, credential-key replacement). Module-private variants (`ceri` `redactedJson`, `safeTerminalText`, `flat`, `redactDeep`) are not in the characterization test |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-34-| MQ-7 | **Done**: slices B–G are implemented and merged in PR #627. Slices D (installed-evaluator binding), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery-reference integrity), and G (bounded campaigns) have focused coverage in `cli/test/ceri_mq7dg.test.ts`; B/C coverage is in `cli/test/ceri_mq7bc.test.ts`. | No remaining v1.2.2 implementation item identified in this unit. |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-35-| CS-11 | Deterministic loop-pattern transcripts against the real agent turn and the measured table in `docs/LOOP_PATTERNS.md`; the pattern 2b gap (a command failing with only volatile text changing) closed by context-anchored volatile-text patterns, measured on an 86-pair labelled corpus (same-failure merged 6 of 38 to 34 of 38, different-failure merged 0 of 48 before and after) | Four same-failure pairs with no context word to anchor on (digit-less random suffix, unanchored counter, queue depth, short sha), left unmerged on purpose |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:36:| WM-3 | All five gateway aliases measured (single runs) | A reasoning-aware pass rule for aliases with reasoning off (the runner marks them `passed: false` for the hybrid-reasoning check); repeats, to see run-to-run variation |\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-37-\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-38-## Landed after this list was first written (2026-11-18)\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-39-\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-40-- **VC-7** (done): the verifier convergence stop is also judged on the `run_tests` / `run_command` verifier path (`cli/test/vc7_run_tests_convergence.test.ts`).\n--\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-57-\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-58-## Process lessons from v1.2.1\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-59-\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-60-- A release workflow runs tests that pull-request CI skips (host-conditional ones); run those under a flooded environment before tagging.\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:61:- A background agent's reported test subset is not the suite: two failures in the VC-9 PR were found only by CI.\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-62-- An agent's own mutation report is not evidence until re-run; one PR had a surviving mutation, another reported a mutation as caught when it was not.\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md:63:- CI colours its output and runs on Node 22 and 24 with different timing: agent PRs need local runs with `FORCE_COLOR=1 CI=true`, and tables kept in sync by a test (such as the global option table in `eval_cli.ts`) need the new flag added in the same change. Two real failures in the `--fork-session` PR and one in the VC-9 PR were found only by CI.\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-64-- A timing-sensitive PTY test (`the whole live TUI through a pseudo-terminal`) failed once on Node 22 in the VC-7 PR and passed on rerun; it is a known-flaky class (see `flake-report`), recorded here rather than hidden.\ndocs/v1.2.2/BACKLOG.md-65-- A background agent can stall behind its own watcher loops for hours; give agents a foreground-only rule and check their worktree for changes when a status request goes unanswered.\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-30-|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-31-| G-1 | `make test` green at the exact head; required CI green on the release commit; main CI green on the merge commit |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-32-| G-2 | Every P0 unit merged; every P1 unit merged or carries a dated owner deferral |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-33-| G-3 | Live-gateway tool-call matrix (G1.1-4) repeated on the release-candidate archive and recorded as evidence (also closes GA-03 for v1.2.0 if still open) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:34:| G-4 | A first external-style measurement exists and is published in the notes with its limits (MQ-2): at minimum a pinned Terminal-Bench subset run through the Harbor adapter, with the false-completion rate reported beside the pass rate. If no run is possible in the window, the notes say \"no external measurement\" and the release is still allowed; the framing never changes |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-35-| G-5 | No test in the suite can leak a bridge, agent, gateway, child process or timer past its file (PC-2 guard green) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-36-| G-6 | Docs: README, CHANGELOG, manual and examples current (DH-1…DH-3) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-37-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-38-\"Externally qualified\" is defined in section 7 so the word has one meaning.\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-45-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-46-| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-47-|---|---|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-48-| MQ-0 | Run the G1.1-4 matrix on the v1.2.0 release-candidate archive under the GATEWAY claim; record `docs/evidence/v1.2.0/…` and add the figure to the rc notes (closes GA-03) | evidence file with archive sha256, per-alias counts, repaired / invalid counts; notes updated by a docs PR | S | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:49:| MQ-1 | Benchmark adapter: a Harbor / Terminal-Bench runner for Nexus (template: Goose `evals/harbor/`), plus a SWE-bench-style adapter if the task format allows a hermetic run | `nexus` runs a pinned subset in task containers with no network leaks; results JSON with task ids, model, gateway alias, Nexus version, seed or sampling, wall time, and the raw verdicts (VERIFIED / REGRESSION_FREE / UNVERIFIED / BLOCKED) | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:50:| MQ-2 | False-completion metric: for every run record Nexus's own verdict and the benchmark's ground truth; report pass rate, **claimed-done-but-wrong rate**, and **verified-but-wrong rate** with Wilson 95% intervals (the repo already has the interval code in `eval/score.mjs`); compare against the same model through at least one peer harness already adapted in `eval/configs/pilot-*.json` | a report where a false VERIFIED is named and explained; the notes state the sample size and that it is self-run | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-51-| MQ-3 | Real-project smoke: a nightly job that runs a scripted task on pinned copies of representative repositories (a JS project with `node_modules`, a Makefile project, a Python project, a Rust project, and the owner's own repositories where licensing allows) and fails when `task_completed` errors, hangs or ends UNVERIFIED for an avoidable reason. This is the test that would have caught the v1.1.4 every-completion error before release | job red on a seeded regression (a repo whose verifier cannot be bound must complete as REGRESSION_FREE). **Done 2026-11-17** (branch `v1/mq3`): `scripts/real_project_smoke.mjs`, `cli/test/real_project_smoke.test.ts`, nightly `.github/workflows/real-project-smoke.yml` (not required), `docs/REAL_PROJECT_SMOKE.md`. Covers a JS project with `node_modules`, a Makefile project with 140 referenced files, a 24-deep tree and a pytest project; red when a completion errors, hangs or is not REGRESSION_FREE/VERIFIED; mutation-checked against a reintroduced unbindable-verifier block. **Does not cover:** a live model (the gateway is a scripted mock), real third-party or owner repositories (all synthetic), a Rust project, sandbox, worktree or resume modes | M | P0 **First hosted run 2026-11-18 (schedule, main 3c54e601): 3 of 4 repositories passed, the pytest one failed on a setup defect (pytest installed with `pip install --user` was invisible to the isolated HOME); fixed, rerun to be recorded.** **Validated on a hosted runner 2026-11-18 (manual run 44314195365 on a7c07e74: all four repositories REGRESSION_FREE) after the pytest setup fix.** |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-52-| MQ-4 | Scheduled model conformance: a nightly or weekly job that runs the tool-call matrix against the configured gateway aliases and alerts on a drop; claim protocol so it never collides with a human run (template: Goose `model-toolcall-conformance.yml`) | one alert path tested with a forced failure | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-53-| MQ-5 | CERI baseline identity bound to evidence (audit F02): baseline manifest, model digest, sampling and configuration recorded and verified when a comparison is made; a comparison without bound baselines is labelled non-certifying (decision D7, included) | a comparison against an edited baseline fails; test with a swapped manifest | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-54-| MQ-6 | CERI evidence loading verifies the integrity record (audit F06): `passed:\"false\"` no longer loads as true, run-id collisions are refused, a tampered record is rejected (D7) | tests for each of the three defects | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:55:| MQ-7 | Audit Slices B–G, decide each as a unit or a dated deferral, then build in this order: B evidence lifecycle, C verified baselines and paired comparisons (needed for MQ-2 to be called certifying), E request-context manifests, D installed-evaluator adapter, F recovery reference checker, G bounded campaigns (D7). B and C are P1; D, E, F, G are P2 and may roll | each slice either merged with tests or carries a dated deferral in this file. **2026-11-17:** slices B and C built (branch `v1/mq7bc`; builds on MQ-5/MQ-6): B adds resolved (realpath) containment of evidence paths, validated run ids (reserved names, length, case-only collisions) and the written same-user limitation in `docs/CERI_EVIDENCE.md`; C adds a fixture identity, `mismatchedFields` naming every differing identity field, `loadVerifiedBaseline`/`compareToVerifiedBaseline`, and removes the caller flag as a route to CONFIRMED_REGRESSION (a verified earlier run is required). **Deferred 2026-11-17 (D7):** slices D (installed-evaluator adapter), E (request-context manifests), F (recovery reference checker) and G (bounded campaigns) roll to the next release (v1.2.2) per D7; not started | L | P1/P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:56:| MQ-8 | Long-horizon and A/B measurement (the Anthropic trends report, trend 3; `agent-rules-books` and the context-management post both report gains without a methodology): one multi-hour scripted task on a pinned repository with a resume part-way, and paired runs of the same scenarios with an instruction file, or tool-output clearing, on and off; report completion, claimed-done-but-wrong rate, tokens and wall time with Wilson 95% intervals | results JSON with the paired runs, model, gateway alias, Nexus version and seeds; no effect claimed unless the interval excludes zero (needs MQ-1 and MQ-2) | M | P2 (v1.2.2) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-57-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:58:Dependencies: MQ-1 → MQ-2 → MQ-8. MQ-3 and MQ-4 are independent. Decision D3 (what is published) and D4 (scope and cost) gate MQ-1 and MQ-2.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-59-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-60-### VC: verification completeness (the v1.2.0 deferrals)\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-61-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-62-| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-63-|---|---|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-64-| VC-1 | **Done 2026-11-17** (`cli/src/core/boundary_rules.ts`, operation spec `boundaries`, enforced in the review gate; limits in docs/USER_MANUAL.md). SI-5 operator-declared boundary rules (import allow/deny, protected public files) checked on the candidate diff with deterministic analysis; interface/schema/format changes classified compatible / potentially breaking / unknown; coverage limits always reported | boundary violation = REJECT; undeclared dependency = advisory; unknown coverage disclosed; rules absent = no behaviour change; CERI `cumulative-change-03` becomes active | L | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-65-| VC-2 | **Done 2026-11-18** (`cli/src/core/regression_obligations.ts`; operator verbs on `/requirements`; `task_completed` is refused while `unmetObligations` is non-empty, naming each obligation and its replay command, and a recorded pass of that command lets it proceed; weakening is recorded for file-tool edits, including headless runs with no UI observer, and for `run_command` edits via the CS-2 shell snapshot. Limits: a shell edit is seen only for files of at most 64 KiB inside the scan bounds; replay is recorded when the verifier runs, and a verifier that never runs the test passes it vacuously). DV-3 operator-authorised regression obligations: a confirmed failing test becomes a protected obligation only on operator authorisation; the original failure record is kept when later minimised | authorised obligation replayed on the next candidate and a weakening edit recorded; unauthorised promotion cannot happen | M | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:66:| VC-3 | **Done 2026-11-18.** Lineage (parent attempt, transition hash of the two candidate identities: not a content diff), the authorisation gate for a named reference (`/requirements reference`) and the behavioural differential (`/requirements differential <R> --inputs <file>`) are built. The reference is an operator-declared command; the candidate is the objective's latest verifier command; each declared input (JSON array of strings, at most 50, each at most 64 KiB) goes to both on stdin through the sandbox with no network, 30 s per run, output capped at 1 MiB; outputs are normalised with `normalizeOutcomeText` and compared with the exit code. A timeout, error or over-cap run is inconclusive for that input, never agreement. The result is finite agreement on the tested inputs (`agreementStatement`), never equivalence; there is no model-facing path. DV-4 attempt lineage (parent attempt, diff hash) and behavioural differential against a declared reference only when an authorised requirement names one (the Anthropic 2026 trends report's flagship long run, Rakuten's seven-hour single run at 99.9% numerical accuracy against a reference method, succeeded because a reference existed: the reference is what lets a long run stay autonomous. Raising this to P1 if MQ-1 shows reference-checkable tasks are common is an owner decision, not made here) | baseline-defect correction not blocked when an authoritative requirement contradicts the baseline; finite-case agreement never reported as equivalence | M | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-67-| VC-4 | **Done 2026-11-17:** the only pending scenario, `cumulative-change-03`, is active (pending count 0); `compaction-03` became active with CX-5. Close the two pending CERI scenarios once VC-1 lands; assign an owner to every scenario in `semantic_integrity` | pending count 0 or each pending carries a unit id | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-68-| VC-5 | Re-check `verified` on resume (invalidate when the candidate identity differs) and persist the unbound-pass marker so a resumed task does not hit the \"no matching baseline\" refusal | resume test: edited workspace demotes `verified`; resume after an unbound pass completes as REGRESSION_FREE | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-69-| VC-6 | **Done 2026-11-18** (`cli/src/tester.ts`, `cli/src/core/completion_oracle.ts`, `cli/src/git.ts`, `cli/test/vc6_typecheck_gaps.test.ts`; manual section \"Type-check evidence\"). Baseline run (tsc, go vet, pyright, mypy; cargo excluded as not cheap) so an untouched caller broken by the change blocks VERIFIED; shell-changed files counted via the workspace diff; Go and Python (when configured); missing tool is UNAVAILABLE, never a pass; NEXUS_TYPECHECK=off unchanged. Limits: one check per project (first found: tsc, cargo, go, then python), no baseline after a resume, a baseline needs the verifier baseline to have run. Type-check gaps: baseline run to catch callers broken in untouched files, count shell-changed files as modified (workspace diff, not edit tools), Go (`go vet`) and Python (pyright/mypy when configured) | a change that breaks an untouched caller blocks VERIFIED; opt-out stays | M | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-70-| VC-7 | Convergence on the `run_tests` path and for operator-configured verifiers | a `run_tests`-only loop stops with the history attached | S | P2 **Done 2026-11-18** (`dispatch_tool_call.ts` judges `convergenceSignal` after a recorded verifier attempt; `cli/test/vc7_run_tests_convergence.test.ts`, the main case was red before the fix). An operator-configured verifier is covered because it is recorded through the same path. |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-71-| VC-8 | Per-role tool allowlists beyond reviewer and researcher (old backlog REQ:259, accepted 2026-11-15): allowlists for implementer and tester, consistent with SI-2's tester scoping (`roleToolAllowList`, `cli/src/agent.ts:5490`) | each role's disallowed tool is refused with a reason; existing flows unchanged | S | P2 (v1.2.2) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:72:| VC-9 | **Partly done 2026-11-18** (audit: `docs/v1.2.1/VC-9-needs-input-audit.md`; glue: `cli/src/core/needs_input.ts`, `--answer`). Done: tool approval (write, exec, egress), verification-file edit, verifier and type-check approval end BLOCKED / NEEDS_INPUT with a question and resume with `--answer`. **Not done** (design decisions, see the audit): a requirement marked blocked (changes the REQUIREMENTS_INCOMPLETE contract; free-text answer), an unresolved steering conflict (sidecar-only; resolve-after-the-fact), a subagent's question. Headless needs-input audit (Anthropic 2026 trends report, trend 4: agents that learn when to ask for help): list every path where a headless run needs an operator decision (steering that negates a requirement, a requirement marked blocked, a denied approval, a verification-file edit) and give each the same resumable outcome, a stable `needs_input` reason carrying the question and resumable with the answer; build only the missing glue | each path ends BLOCKED with a machine-readable question and resumes with an answer; no path guesses | S | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-73-| VC-10 | Steering semantic judge (optional; evidence in `docs/research/steering-judgment/`): a judge provider interface (none by default, the configured gateway, or an explicit opt-in third party) asked, for each live requirement, whether the steering message withdraws, defers or changes it; conflict (the existing operator decision) if the regex hits or `withdraws` >= 0.7 whatever `defers` says, advisory note between 0.3 and 0.7, `defers` and `changes` informational only; an unavailable judge records `semanticJudge: unavailable` and keeps the regex decision; the steer stays held from the model until the operator decides and no text goes to a third party unless opted in | **gated**: build only after a larger evaluation (at least 100 judgments from real steering messages, with the owner's consent, labelled blind, on a fresh held-out split) shows union recall of at least 0.8 with at most 5% false positives on the non-withdrawals, and after the synchronous path can hold a steer while a judge answers (the pilot gateway judge took a median of about 17 s per message) | M | P2 (v1.2.2, gated) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-74-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-75-### CS: containment and security\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-76-\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-93-| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-94-|---|---|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-95-| WM-1 | Schema-driven argument repair (stringified JSON, \"20\" to 20, nulls, arrays) only where the schema makes the fix unambiguous (reference: opencode `plugin/tool-input-repair.ts`); every repair recorded | repair counted in the matrix as repaired, never as native valid | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-96-| WM-2 | Edit-failure feedback: when an edit does not apply, return the closest actual lines (reference: Aider `editblock_coder.py:100-312`) | test with whitespace and elision mismatches | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:97:| WM-3 | Extend the G1.1-4 matrix to every alias the gateway serves, with pacing and cache-bypass checks unchanged | per-alias table in evidence | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-98-| WM-4 | Function-call-style text recovery (found 2026-11-15 on the published v1.2.0 matrix: `engineering/lead-reasoning` answered the plain `read_file` prompt with the text `read_file(path=\"README.md\")` and no native call, 10 of 10, so 45 of 50 attempts were invalid and the client's safe retry never engaged): recognise a whole-content call to an offered tool written as `name(arg=\"value\")`, or inside a `[...]` list (the pythonic template format), as a call written as text, so the existing feedback and forced-`tool_choice` retry applies. Recognition only: nothing is executed from text (REQ-AGT-112); the G1.1-4 matrix keeps counting native calls only | table of accepted and rejected forms (prose that merely mentions a call, unknown tool, unbalanced, a variable argument); a mock-gateway test where the first reply is function-call text and the retry returns a native call; a matrix taken on the v1.2.1 release candidate before any claim | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-99-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-100-### CX: context\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-101-\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-103-|---|---|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-104-| CX-1 | Label every injected summary block (including `compact --llm`) as untrusted data with its source (G-SI-6) | request-shape snapshot (TI-1) updated; test with an injected instruction in a summary | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-105-| CX-2 | Compaction state stored separately from the canonical transcript and validated by a hash of the covered prefix on resume (reference: Cline `sdk/ARCHITECTURE.md:643-665`); the record names the covered event ids, the summary and where it is inserted, so a compaction can be audited and undone (reference: OpenHands `Condensation` event, docs 2026-11-15) | resume with a corrupted compaction file falls back to the full transcript; the record lists the covered ids | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-106-| CX-3 | Compaction probe: after summarising, a second bounded call checks that the summary keeps every gating requirement (reference: Gemini CLI `chatCompressionService.ts:770`); reject and fall back otherwise. Scope: the `compact --llm` path and any future summarising path only; the default ladder (`admitContext`) is deterministic and writes no summary, so CX-5 alone covers it | closes the check `compaction-03` expects on the summarising path, together with CX-5 | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:107:| CX-4 | Tool-output masking above a token threshold, with the full output retrievable by id (references: Gemini CLI `toolOutputMaskingService.ts:25`, Cline cached tool results). A masked output stays in the evidence store, and the independent reviewer's package (requirements, scope, diff, verifier receipts) does not depend on conversation context, so masking cannot remove evidence (read in `independent_review.ts`, 2026-11-15) | request-shape snapshot shows the mask marker; the id resolves; the reviewer package is identical before and after masking | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL, opt-in. Done: `NEXUS_TOOL_OUTPUT_MASK_TOKENS=<n>` masks, in the request only, a tool result above n estimated tokens once the model has acted on it (a later assistant message exists); the marker names a content id and the full output resolves with `read_archive` query `tool-output:<id>` (live conversation, then archive). Never masked: results not yet acted on, `read_archive`/`read_findings`/`read_objective` results, the pinned ledger, findings and durable records (system messages). Stored conversation and sessions are unchanged. `cli/src/core/tool_output_mask.ts`, `cli/test/tool_output_mask.test.ts` (request-shape marker, id resolves, ledger intact, off by default). The reviewer package is built from the diff and verifier receipts (`operation_runner_execution.ts` `reviewEvidence`), not the conversation, and the review module does not import masking; the test pins that, but it is a structural guard, not an end-to-end review run. Decision: default off, because safe-by-default would need a measured threshold on real tasks (masking can cost the model a retrieval step); a retrieved output enters the conversation whole and is not masked again. Not done: a dedicated id-read tool (the existing archive tool is reused), a default threshold, masking measured on the eval set.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-108-| CX-5 | Post-compaction invariant: after every compaction, every gating requirement of the pinned ledger (`seedRequirements`) is present in the request, or the turn blocks with a named reason (owns CERI `compaction-03`). Covers each rung of the `admitContext` ladder (recent turns 2, 1, 0 against detail budgets 6000 down to 0) and `compact --llm`; the requirement rows are never omitted or truncated, so a ledger too big for the window blocks the turn (`CONTEXT_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED` naming the requirements), which the code already did; only the verbatim objective text has the whole-or-named deferral (`compaction-02`) | a test walks every rung and asserts the ledger survives; a forced zero-budget rung with an over-limit ledger blocks; scenario `compaction-03` active | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-109-| CX-6 | Prompt caching: request cache hints for providers that support them; measure cached-token share | share reported in the matrix | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: PARTIAL. Done: Anthropic `cache_control` breakpoints (system prompt, pinned ledger) behind the provider's declared `promptCache` capability and the operator's `NEXUS_PROMPT_CACHE=1`; request-shape test shows the marker; the usage summary reports the cached share (`cached N of M input tokens (P%)`). Not done: default-on (needs live Anthropic verification, none made), Bedrock `cachePoint`, operator `providers.json` declaration of the capability, a cached share in the provider matrix doc. OpenAI and Gemini cache implicitly and need no hint.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-110-| CX-7 | Project instruction files: expand `@path` imports in `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CODEX.md` and `.nexusrules` (today `getProjectRules` in `cli/src/git.ts` loads the first file raw, so a `CLAUDE.md` holding only `@AGENTS.md` puts that literal text in the prompt); bounded depth, cycle-safe, confined to the workspace, expanded only in a trusted workspace, and report which files were loaded (references: the Claude Code `@AGENTS.md` import convention, the usage guide of `agent-rules-books`) | `CLAUDE.md` containing `@AGENTS.md` loads the AGENTS content once; an import outside the workspace, or in an untrusted workspace, is not read; a cycle stops at the depth bound; the loaded-file list is shown | S | P2 | Note 2026-11-18: DONE as scoped. `getProjectRules` expands `@path` imports (new `cli/src/project_rules.ts`) only for a trusted workspace; real-path confinement, regular files only, 64 KiB per file and 256 KiB total, depth 5, each file read once (cycle-safe); the heading lists loaded files. Tests: `cli/test/project_rules_imports.test.ts`. Not covered: it still loads only the first non-empty file (AGENTS.md wins over CLAUDE.md); `@~/` and absolute paths are not imports; no UI beyond the prompt heading shows the list.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-111-\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-119-|---|---|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-120-| PR-1 | **Done 2026-11-17** (merged earlier as #521: `cli/src/protocols/openai_chat.ts`, request-shape fixtures unchanged; `client.ts` keeps only the transport, retry and error handling and calls the extracted builder, parser and stream reader). Extract the inline `openai-chat` path from `client.ts` into `cli/src/protocols/openai_chat.ts` with the same `NativeResult` contract; no behaviour change | all provider tests unchanged and green; request-shape snapshots (TI-1) unchanged | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-121-| PR-2 | Data-driven provider registry: a `ProviderDef` schema (id, label, protocol, base URL template with validated variables such as `{resource}`, query, headers, auth kind `bearer`/`header`/`none`, env names, model source, capability defaults, `omitParams`, `streamOnly`); the six existing ids become table entries; the Copilot gpt-5 protocol rule and ChatGPT parameter omission become data; a user file `~/.config/nexus/providers.json` (operator-only; a project-level file is refused, like `NEXUS_PROVIDER`); several named compatible providers instead of one slot | a user-defined Azure OpenAI provider works against a mock server (URL with deployment, `api-version` query, `api-key` header); existing providers behave identically; schema validation errors name the field | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-122-| PR-3 | models.dev snapshot: an offline generator script turns the models.dev data into `cli/src/providers.generated.ts` (provider ids, base URLs and env names for the OpenAI-compatible set; per-model context, output, tool-choice modes, reasoning effort, cache and price), dated and schema-validated, with no runtime network fetch; an explicit `nexus models refresh` writes a local cache that overrides the snapshot | table validates in CI; the snapshot date is shown by `nexus providers`; the cache is used only when the operator ran refresh | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:123:| PR-4 | Capability-aware behaviour from that data: use `capabilities.tools.choice` before forcing `tool_choice: required` in the content-form re-ask (a model that lists only `auto` and `none` gets the corrective note instead of a request error), use per-model context and output limits instead of the 65,536 default, offer the model's reasoning effort levels | a mock model that rejects forced tool use does not receive `required`; context limit resolves from the snapshot; G1.1-4 matrix (WM-3) unchanged on the gateway aliases | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-124-| PR-5 | The OpenAI-compatible long tail as data: Azure OpenAI, Gemini's OpenAI-compatible endpoint, Mistral, Groq, Together, DeepSeek, xAI, Fireworks, Cerebras, Perplexity, Cloudflare Workers AI and Bedrock's OpenAI-compatible endpoint with a bearer API key | one recorded-fixture conversation per provider definition (text, tool call, reasoning, error, truncation); no live call | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-125-| PR-6 | **Done 2026-11-17** (`cli/src/protocols/gemini.ts`, protocol id `gemini-generate-content`, auth `header` + `x-goog-api-key`; fixture test `cli/test/gemini_protocol.test.ts`; no built-in provider and no live call, so the real API shape is verified against documentation only). Gemini native protocol (`generateContent` / `streamGenerateContent`; `functionCall` and thought parts map to `NativeResult`; `x-goog-api-key` header kind) | fixture conversation including parallel tool calls | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-126-| PR-7 | **Done 2026-11-17** (`cli/src/protocols/bedrock_converse.ts`, `aws_sigv4.ts`, `aws_eventstream.ts`, `aws_credentials.ts`; docs/CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md; fixtures only, no live call, no built-in provider entry: declared in `providers.json`). Amazon Bedrock Converse protocol with SigV4 signing written on `node:crypto` (no dependency), the AWS event-stream binary framing parser, and the AWS credential chain from environment and shared config files | fixture conversation plus signature test vectors from AWS documentation | L | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-127-| PR-8 | **Done 2026-11-17** (`cli/src/protocols/gcp_adc.ts`, auth `gcp-adc` on `gemini-generate-content`; test `cli/test/gcp_adc_vertex.test.ts`; docs/CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md; fixtures and a local mock only, no live call, no built-in provider entry: declared in `providers.json`). Google Vertex: Gemini protocol with `gcp-adc` auth (service-account JWT signed with `node:crypto`, or a token from `gcloud`), regional URL template with project and location, token refresh | fixture plus a token-refresh test with an injected clock | M | P2 |\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-147-### PC: process and CI\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-148-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-149-| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-150-|---|---|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:151:| PC-1 | CI wall time: run `linux-x86_64` in parallel with `test-matrix` where its work does not need it, split the CLI suite into shards, cache toolchains; update the stale \"12–20 minutes\" comment (decision D10: required check names and runner minutes) | median PR CI under 40 minutes with the same required-check semantics; measured over 20 runs | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-152-| PC-2 | Leak and hang guard: a shared test helper that registers bridges, agents, gateways and child processes and fails the file at teardown if any survive; a per-file timeout in `run_tests.mjs`; a rule that lists test files constructing a bridge or agent without it (2 found, names to be captured first); also re-verify the stale/corrupt lock race and EXECUTION_CONVERGENCE I3/I4 from the old backlog and fix any defect found (accepted 2026-11-15) | the SI-2-style leak fails in 1 minute with the file name, not in 45 minutes; the re-verification result is recorded and any defect it shows is fixed with a regression test | M | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-153-| PC-3 | Flake budget: record rerun-on-failure events, report weekly, fail when a test needs reruns twice in 20 runs | report committed as an artifact | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-154-| PC-4 | Cross-runner reproducibility (22.04 vs 24.04) as a scheduled job, not a manual dispatch **Done 2026-11-18:** `repro.yml` gains a weekly cron (Monday 05:17 UTC) and keeps `workflow_dispatch`. A scheduled run has no tag input, so `resolve-tag` selects the newest published, non-draft RC pre-release (and fails if none exists); later jobs use that resolved tag. Actions stay SHA-pinned; `release.yml` untouched; it is not a required check. The drift alert is a red scheduled run (GitHub failure notification); no issue-opening step was added. **Not done:** the job has not run on GitHub yet, so \"job green\" is unproven until the first scheduled or dispatched run (confirm after merge). | job green; alert on drift | S | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-155-| PC-5 | Opt-in crash and error reporting: confirm what OTLP does today (opt-in status unverified), document it, add a local crash report file under `~/.nexus/` | doc plus a test that nothing leaves the host without opt-in | S | P2 (settled by v1.3 GV-4; see docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md) |\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-170-|---|---|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-171-| DH-1 | README status line, install example and platform line; `CHANGELOG.md` headings for v1.1.3, v1.1.4 and v1.2.0 | no reference to a stale \"stable\" version; a check script fails on drift | S | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-172-| DH-2 | `USER_MANUAL.md`: 54 hits of v1.1.0–v1.1.3 reviewed and corrected; a section for each v1.2.0 behaviour change | review table in the PR | S | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-173-| DH-3 | `examples/`: a minimal verified-completion example, a worktree example, an operation file with `git.isolate`, and the CI example from RD-4 | each example runs in CI | M | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:174:| DH-4 | Issues reviewed 2026-11-15 (D8): #2, #5, #7 and #49 closed as superseded or completed with comments naming where each requirement went (MQ-1/MQ-2, PC-4, DH-6, merged PR #33); #1 (held-out corpus) and #6 (independent continuity acceptance) kept open and labelled v1.2.1. **Done 2026-11-17:** `docs/v1.1.0/STATUS.md` marked historical, the `FINDINGS.md` header corrected, the stale `v1.0.0` label removed from #1; the deferral review is below | tracker has no stale v1.0.0 issue; the review table is in this file | S | P1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-175-| DH-5 | Finish the ledger: read `docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/` and `docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md` and add anything not yet in `GAP_ANALYSIS.md`. **Done 2026-11-16:** both read in full; GA-44 to GA-56 are open or unverified rows, GA-57 to GA-59 record what current `main` already fixes. Unit candidates for the owner, not applied: a small S unit for GA-44 (`patch_file` hunk placement, stale-content guard) and GA-45 (search truncation disclosure), GA-46 and GA-47 (milestone and in-flight-operation state), GA-49 (verify MCP tool execution), and GA-55 (tag signature verification); GA-54 needs an owner decision | gap register updated with sources | S | P0 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-176-| DH-6 | Decide whether to switch `scripts/check_requirements.mjs` to enforce mode (the one open fragment of closed issue #7) and, if yes, bring the ledger rows to their accepted statuses **Decided and done 2026-11-18: enforce.** `--enforce` passes on a clean `origin/main` (123 rows, 12 deferred to exact-RC qualification by `ACCEPTANCE.md`, which is the designed outcome, so no ledger row needed changing). The `test-matrix` job in `ci.yml` now runs it in enforce mode right after checkout (enforce requires a clean worktree). The `linux-x86_64` call runs after a build and stays in report mode so build output cannot dirty the tree. Exact-RC enforcement with a qualification record stays a release step. | decision recorded; gate behaviour matches it | S | P2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-177-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-178-## 5. Sequencing and release split\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-179-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-180-Capacity warning. After the 2026-11-15 decisions the unit list is about three times what v1.2.0 carried, and v1.2.0 took a week of agent work with a 70-minute CI cycle. All of it cannot be one release without cutting corners that this project does not cut. Decision D12 (accepted 2026-11-15): **v1.2.1 = Phase 0 + Phase 1 + Phase 2; v1.2.2 = Phase 3 and the native provider protocols.** Anything not merged with green gates rolls with a dated note in this file; a unit is never half-merged to hit a date.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-181-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:182:Phase 0 (immediately after v1.2.0 ships): MQ-0, DH-1, DH-2, DH-5, PC-2, PC-1. These cut the cycle time, remove the hang class and finish the evidence.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-183-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:184:Phase 1 (independent, parallelisable): MQ-3, MQ-5, MQ-6, MQ-1, then MQ-2 (needs MQ-5/MQ-6 to be called certifying); PR-1, PR-2, PR-3, PR-9, PR-11; CS-3, CS-4, WM-1, WM-2, WM-4, CX-1, CX-5, VC-5.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-185-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:186:Phase 2: PR-4, PR-5, PR-12; CS-7, CS-8 (D5); MQ-7 slices B and C; CS-1 and CS-2 (defaults per D1 and D2: CS-1 stays opt-in unless the default is changed by a later decision); CX-2, CX-3, VC-1, RD-3, RD-4, MT-3, WM-3, MQ-4, PC-3, DH-3, DH-4, DH-6, CX-7, CS-11, VC-9, PC-7.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-187-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:188:Phase 3 (v1.2.2): native protocols PR-6 (Gemini), PR-7 (Bedrock), PR-8 (Vertex), PR-10; CS-9, CS-10, VC-8, RD-7 (added 2026-11-15 from the old-backlog review); MQ-7 slices D, E, F, G; RD-1 (macOS arm64; D6 default is Linux x86_64 only, so this waits for a decision), MT-1, MT-2, VC-2, VC-3, VC-6, VC-7, CX-4, CX-6, RD-2, RD-5, RD-6, CS-5, CS-6, PC-4, PC-5, PC-6, MQ-8 (needs MQ-1 and MQ-2), VC-10 (gated on a larger evaluation).\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-189-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-190-D9 note: \"every cloud provider configurable\" is delivered in v1.2.1 for every OpenAI-compatible provider and for Azure OpenAI, Gemini's compatible endpoint and Bedrock's OpenAI-compatible endpoint (PR-2, PR-3, PR-5), which is most of the 334 providers in models.dev. The three native protocols (Gemini, Bedrock Converse with SigV4, Vertex with ADC) are the largest pieces and ship in v1.2.2 unless you move them up.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-191-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-192-## 6. Owner decisions\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-196-| Id | Decision | Answer |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-197-|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-198-| D1 | Containment default-on when bubblewrap is available (CS-1) | default accepted: stays opt-in |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-199-| D2 | Shell writes to verification files (CS-2) | default accepted: record-only (CS-2 is built as an opt-in hard stop) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:200:| D3 | What may be published | default accepted: publish nothing until a result exists; wording reviewed before MQ-2 reports |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-201-| D4 | Benchmark scope and cost; third-party reproduction | default accepted: small pinned subset, self-run, labelled E1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-202-| D5 | Prompt-injection scanner and egress inspector | **include** in v1.2.1 (CS-7, CS-8) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-203-| D6 | Platform priority and CI cost | default accepted: Linux x86_64 only for v1.2.1; RD-1 waits for a later decision |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-204-| D7 | CERI F02/F06 and audit Slices B–G | **include** (MQ-5, MQ-6, MQ-7); B and C in v1.2.1, D–G in v1.2.2 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-205-| D8 | Relevance of old deferrals and issues | **reviewed and applied** (2026-11-15): issues #2, #5, #7, #49 closed with comments, #1 and #6 kept open and labelled v1.2.1; the five recommended drops approved (DISC-005, cgroup containment, split-record schema, hosted/multi-tenant, autonomous tool generation); deferral table below |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-206-| D9 | Providers | **every cloud provider configurable**, using models.dev data (PR workstream) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:207:| D10 | CI restructure | default accepted: keep required check names, no extra minutes (PC-1 gain is limited accordingly) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-208-| D11 | Integration priority | default accepted: `stream-json` output and the GitHub Action (RD-3, RD-4); ACP waits |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-209-| D12 | Release split | **accepted as recommended** (2026-11-15): v1.2.1 = Phases 0–2; v1.2.2 = Phase 3 and the native provider protocols |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-210-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-211-### Deferral relevance review (D8)\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-218-|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-219-| REQ-PER-107 directory fsync | DONE | `core/src/file_ops.rs:189-194` syncs the parent directory after rename (it ignores the error: a nit) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-220-| ACC-V11-MAO-07 real kill; GIT-02 crash before merge; GIT-04 cancel; CTX-04 checkpoint restore | DONE | `cli/test/operation_release_acceptance.test.ts:264, 343, 424, 462` (not run in this review) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-221-| `repro.yml` pinned to `^v1\\.0\\.0-rc` | DONE | `.github/workflows/repro.yml:43` now accepts any `vX.Y.Z-rc.N` (scheduling it is PC-4) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:222:| `nexus eval` scoring (VER-07b-e) | SUPERSEDED | scoring integrity lives in `eval/score.mjs` (Wilson intervals); `nexus eval` is labelled experimental (DEC-07); MQ-2 reuses `eval/score.mjs` |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-223-| Network-isolated sandbox for generated code | SUPERSEDED (partly) | `eval/isolate.mjs:555,587,723` unshare the network; whether the default client sandbox path does is to be checked under CS-1 |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-224-| Harness² review (#69) | DONE | issue closed; recommendation \"borrow, offline only\" |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-225-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-226-**Dropped (owner approved 2026-11-15; each reopens only on the stated condition):**\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-250-| PTY determinism (REQ:267) | MT-2 | **decided 2026-11-15: folded into MT-2 (v1.2.2)** |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-251-| Per-command approval allowlists (REQ:268) | CS-9 | **decided 2026-11-15: own unit, v1.2.2** |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-252-| `git.publish` PR publication (DEC-V11-12; explicitly refused at `operation_cli.ts:74`) | none | **decided 2026-11-15: keep the explicit refusal;** revisit after CS-8 and CS-1, as a candidate for the next release train |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-253-| Interactive TUI multi-agent pane (`ROADMAP.md:188`) | MT-2 | **decided 2026-11-15: candidate for the next release train** (needs the PTY redesign first) |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:254:| CI `git diff cli/dist` step (`ci.yml:72,176`) | PC-1 | proves nothing; fix or remove in the CI restructure |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-255-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-256-Every row above now carries a decision (2026-11-15). The two sources left open on 2026-11-15, `docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/` and `docs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md`, were read in full on 2026-11-16 (DH-5): the qualification files describe about 60 findings against an October build, most of them already fixed on current `main` (grouped in `GAP_ANALYSIS.md` GA-57 to GA-59, with grep evidence); 13 remain open or unverified (GA-44 to GA-56), mostly P2 and P3 with no unit; `READINESS.md` is a v1.0 status log whose gaps are covered by GA-01, GA-29, DH-6 and the new GA-53 to GA-56. No unit was added or re-prioritised by this review; the candidates are listed in the DH-5 row.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-257-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-258-## 7. What \"externally qualified\" means here\n--\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-291-Status of every unit that is not marked done above, decided under the owner's standing delegation (D1 to D12) when the release candidate was cut. A unit that is not here is either done (see its row) or a P2 unit that rolls to the next release without ceremony (section 5). Each deferral names its reason so it can be reopened.\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-292-\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-293-| Unit | Disposition | Reason |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-294-|---|---|---|\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:295:| MQ-1, MQ-2 | **Deferred to v1.2.2.** No external benchmark result is published for v1.2.1 (G-4 allows saying so in the notes, and the notes do). | Needs a Harbor/Terminal-Bench runner, containers and a model-cost budget (D3, D4) that this release did not have. Nothing was run, so nothing is claimed. |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-296-| MQ-4 | **Built 2026-11-18, not yet scheduled or run live** (`scripts/gateway_conformance.mjs`, `docs/GATEWAY_CONFORMANCE.md`, `cli/test/gateway_conformance.test.ts`): claim protocol, judgement, and an alert path tested with a forced failure through a fake runner. | The gateway is not reachable from GitHub-hosted runners, so scheduling is the operator's (a timer unit example is in the doc); the first live run is still to be done. |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-297-| MQ-7 D to G | **Deferred to v1.2.2** (D7). B and C are done. | |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-298-| RD-1, RD-2 | **Deferred.** The release ships Linux x86_64 only. | No macOS or arm64/musl runner (D6, runner cost). |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md:299:| PC-1 | **Not met; deferred to v1.2.2.** Measured: the required checks of this release's PRs took about 37 to 45 minutes. | Test sharding and toolchain caching were not done; the parallel `linux-x86_64` job is in place. |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md-300-| MT-3 | **Part done** (one pattern table with a Rust parity test); the remaining collapse was done on 2026-11-18 (see the unit row). | The layering works and is tested; collapsing it is a no-behaviour-change refactor that adds release risk. |\ndocs/v1.2.1/PLAN.